      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE cream of a joke should never be sour. A BurroN on the shirt is worth two on the back of the coat. "Reverence your superiors," but don't let them use you for a cushion. No man can successfully fight his way through this world with soft gloves. "QciT not certainty for hope," for a certainty in hand is worth two hopes in a bosh. "SwE's not of my set," said the old hen, as she chased a strange chicken out of the yard. SEEMS as i± the most likely place for a fisher- man to get a bite would be at the mouth of the river. .</p>
        <p>"Is your wile musical?" "Well. I should smile. Why, she can sit or hours and hours and listeu to symphonies." PROBABLY the most forbearing man extant was the one who, when he had his nose pulled, calmly replied that he could not get angry until next tweak.</p>
        <p>His lordship "How old are you, madam 7" Witness—" I have no personal knowledge of my age, and hearsay testibiony, I understand, is not accepted in ttis court." A FASHION journal says :—" Bonnets come high this seasoa." We do not remember when they did not, as any man who has been compelled to pa.y for them can testify. A COINCIDENCE-" All alone, my dear child. I'm afraid that husband of yours neglects you ter- ribly. He's always at his club when I call." "les, mamma; but he's at home at all other times:"</p>
        <p>LITTLE innocent (from town)—And who is that distinguished-looking person in the queer hat? Yachting cousin—On, he's an admiral. Little innocent 1 11y4 How interesting Army or Navy ? A vorNr: lady fainted when told that over 500,000 men died last year, but was, revived by the informa- tion that there were 19,000,000 left.</p>
        <p>"My dear," said a young wife to her husband, "you have never taken inc to the cemetery yet." "No, my love," he replied: "that is a pleasure I have yet in anticipation.'/ A Ii'ENNsyLvANIA man puts a bumble bee and a grasshopper into a bottle together, and they sass each other and call names and tight, and the 'hop- per kicks old bumble to death nine times out of ten ' m about ten minutes.</p>
        <p>OLD gentleman, to boy on twelfth birthday, "I hope you will improve in wisdom, knowledge, and virtue." Boy, politely returning compliment, totally une,onseious of sarcasm, "Lie same to you, sir."</p>
        <p>"Yes," remarked Fenderson, "I was pretty hard pressed for money for a while, but I am now on my feet again." " Are you?" replied Fogg, glancing at Fenderson's No. 11's "you are lucky. Nothing, I should say, could overthrow you."</p>
        <p>"Au, Fogg," began Fenderson, "'What did they say of my address last evening ?" Fogg—" It was the general remark that you were a very happy speedier." Fenderson (delighted)—" Did they ? " " Yes ; but if I am any judge, you didn't have happy listeners." "I've the hardest name in the town," said a working-man to a citizen the other day. "I'll bet a dollar you haven't," replied the other. "Done," said the working-man. "My name is Stone." " Pass over the coin," said the citizen, "My name is Harder."</p>
        <p>" I don't take any stock in this mind cure craze," said Featherly. "I do," said Mrs. Featherly. " You must simply have faith. I could be cured by it." "I am afraid not. There's something else necessary besides faith." "And what is that?" "A mind."</p>
        <p>" PA," asked the small boy, "why do they call the lirst piece the organ plays in church the " voluntary ?'" " Because, my son," replied the old gentleman, " the organist runs his hands over the keys, and the organ goes* wherever it pleases." QUEEN ELIZABETH entering Bristol, a speech was delivered to her. The honest man began— " May it please you, Sacred Majesty, I am the Mouth of the Town," anti then, all amazed, forgot the rest. She sporting said, once or twice, "Speak, good mouth." IN company the ot'Acr evening Henrietta heard some cue across :the room quote the familiar phrase, " Man proposes," and turning to a sister wall- flower she remarked that she was tired of hearing those old sayings that didn't have a single word of truth in them.</p>
        <p>"You must be having a hard time. of it now-a- days," remarked a traveller at a railroad station lunch-counter to the proprietor of the establish- ment. "Why do you think so?" was the query. "Well. I noticed when I bit into this sandwich that you do not make both ends meat." SAID Loi-d John Russell to Hume, at a social dinner, "What do you consider the object of legislation ?" "The greatest good to the greatest number." "What do you consider the greatest number ? " continued his Lordship. "Number one, my Lord," was the commoner's prompt reply. " O'RAFFERTY," said Judge Duffy, of the New York Police-court, "your wife swears you struck her with great violence." " Wid great violins, whin there is not a fiddle, big or little, • on the prameses ! She exaggerates too much entoircly, Yer Honour. It was wid me boot that I rebuked her."</p>
        <p>Douroß (who has been sent for at 2 a.m.)— "Madam, pray send at once for the clergyman, and, if you want to make your will, for the lawyer." Madam (hurriedly)—" Good gracious! Is it so dangerous, doctor ?" Doctor—" Not a bit of it, but I don't want to be the only fool who has been disturbed in his sleep for nothing." CHOLLY—" I see that you have given up weahing that hat-band, chappy." Wally—" Yes, deah boy, had to; doctor forbade it, doncher know." Cholly —" Why, where's the hahm in weahing a hat- band?" Wally—" The doctah said it was such a deusid heavy pwesshah on me bwain that I had to stop weahing it. I say, too bad; isn't it ?" YouNG housekeeper: "My husband says you charge too much for your ice. It ought to be cheaper, we had such an uncommonly cold winter." Old iceman: " Yes'm ; but you see you gets more fur your money." Young housekeeper: "How so ? " Old iceman: "It friz so tight, mum, that the ice is uncommonly solider." Young housekeeper: "Oh, I see l'll tell my husband.</p>
        <p>A FAMILY Aiving at Canonbury found a good thickness of cream on some milk that had been standing over-night, and when the milkman made his usual call, the pleased servant exhibited the cream, and said, "I never expected to see this." The man looked at the cream, winked wisely and solemnly, z,cratched his head, and replied, " Well, I don't know what's the matter with it, but you can throw it out, and I'll.give you a fresh pint in its place." AT an evening party Dumley was introduced to a young lady, and after a remark about the weather, he said gallantly—" And have I really the pleasure of meeting the beautiful Miss Smith, whose praises are being sounded by everybody " "Oh, no, Mf. Darnley." the lady replied, "the beautiful Miss Smith to whom you refer is a cousin of mine." " Oh, that's 'it ? Well, I thought there must be a mistake somewhere," said the gallant Dumley. "I SHALL teach you to speak properly, and then to write as you speak," sa'Al a teacher in the 'public schools. "Poor Billy Willcox " said a little voice involuntarily. " What about Billy ? " asked the schoolmistress. " Please,,ma'am, bespeaks through his nose; so he will have to write through his nose."</p>
        <p>A SCOTCH nobleman of no bright parts, chatting with the late Duchess of Devon:Aiire, she asked how it happened that the-Scots in general made a much better figure from home than in Scotland. "Oh," says he, "nothing is so easily accounted for. For the honour of the nation, persons are stationed at every egress to see that none leave the country but linen of "Then," returned she, "I sus- pect your lordship was smuggled." SAMUEL SMITH was a servant on a Long Island milk farm. One day Mrs. Jones, tht farmer's wife, went into the milk-house and found Sam down on his knees before a milk vat, skimming the cream off with his finger and putting it in his mouth. "Oh, Sam'!, Sam'! !" she exclaimed, "I don't like !that." " Ah, missus," quoth Sam, nothing clis • concerted, "you don't know what's good for your AN, American tourist was visiting Naples, and saw Vesuvius during an eruption. "Have you any tiling like that in the New World?" was the question of an Italian spectator. "No," replied the other; "but we have a Niagara that would t it out in five minutes." A NATURAL, in music, is quite different from A , flat; but a natural, among mankind, is a decided eat. TRuE COURAGE.—" Sambo, you nigger, are you afraid of work?" " Lor' bress you, massa, I'se no 'fraid of work. I'd lie down and go asleep close ky him aide," • 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>" CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>One of Mr. Gladstone's last official acts was to recommend Lady Alice Portal for a pension upon Her Majesty's Civil List, in recognition of the emin- ent public services rendered by her husband,the late Sir Gerald Portal.</p>
        <p>There is a well-known English Bishop in the Southern Province who exels in he art of replying to requests in Scriptural language and on postcards. Prior to an absence from his diocese through illness, he had promised to preach as well as confirm in a country church, and on his convalescence when requested to fix a date hisreply was "Have patience with me and I will pay thee all."</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Cowen's house at Blaydon, Newcastle- upon-Tyne, has probably sheltered more cprispira- tors under its roof thin any other similar building in Europe. The death of Kossuth recalls the fact that the Hungarian patriot was a constant visitor there in former years, and Garibaldi, Mazzini, Bakunin, Karl Blind. Orsini, Louis Blanc, and a host of others famous in the epoch of revolution, were often to be met there in those stirring times.</p>
        <p>Dr. James Martineau, the English Unitarian divine, is perhaps the most wonderful of the eminent octogenarians living, He is four years the senior of Gladstone and ten years of Bismarck. Yet, although he has now withdrawn from ministerial and professional life, not long ago he delivered a long, closely-reasoned speech without the aid of a note, and he is still writing in the reviews on such abstruse questions as the origin of the newly-dis- covered gospel of St. Peter.</p>
        <p>Major Sir George Clarke, in the current number of the "North American Review," calls attention to the fact that a naval union between England and America is much more within the bounds of proba- bility than is generally believed. On both sides of the Atlantic, the democracy are beginning to grasp the truth that "the welfare of the masses is inti- mately bound up with the security of sea-borne trade." Major Clarke is right in saying that the 'British people, who would not tolerate a European alliance, would welcome a naval league with the United States with enthusiasm. One of the great guarantees of the future of such a league lies in the fact that it must be essentially a league of demo- cracies.</p>
        <p>7 The Prince of Wales once had a new title given him. The Prince is a very hearty. laugher. If anything tickles his fancy he roars and roars again. On one occasion His Royal Hi,liness laughed very heartily at a Hindoo schoolboy in Madras. The youngsters had been drilled into the propriety of saying, "Your Royal liigness," should the Prince speak to any of them ; and when the Heir-Appar- ent accosted a bright-eyed lad, and pointing to a prismatic compass, asked, "V hat is this ?" The youngster, all in a flutter, replied., 4‘ Please, its a a Royal compass, your prismatic Highness,' on which came peal after peal of Royal " laughter without any control."</p>
        <p>Professor Henry Drummond,who has been inter- viewed by Mr. Arthur Warren, tells a new story of Mr. Gladstone. Just before the Gladstone Adminis- tration of 1885 were defeaed on the Home Rule Bill, Mr. Gladstone drove home alone. to Dallis Hill, and next da3 he said to Mr. Drummond, whom he met at dinner, "In driving out here last nigkt I counted twenty-eight omnibuses going in one dire•:tion. I assumed that to-night, it being Satur- day, would show a larger number. I estimated the nLmber at thirty-five. There were thirty-six." This reminiscence casts a strong light upon the ex-Premier's well-known faculty of completely dis- associating himself from the events of the bour,and living, as Mr. Drummond puts it," in an atmosphere of thought far removed from the problem of poli- tical life."</p>
        <p>Referring to the vast number of letters of admira- tion and affection which have been addressed to Mr. Gladstone on his retirement from office, I am told that the mails from the colonies in the last few days have brought letters from almost every one of our possessions expressive of the deep sympathy which Mr. Gladstone's life and work have in- spired beyond the seas. In many cases these letters have been written by IriA men and women, And .their fervour and gratitude have made a great im- pression on the ex-Premier. In addition, there iA not a country in Europe from which expressions of reverence have not come, and the messages from the East—from Bulgaria, Roumania, Servia, and Montenegro—are even more fervid than those to hand from the colonial Irish.</p>
        <p>There is something pathetic about the final re- moval of Mr. Gladstone's goods from Dowuing-st. on Tues;dsy. Every bit of his furniture was carted away en route to Hawarden. and all his books went too Previously, during the last twenty years, when he left the Premiership by free will or pres- sure of circumstances, he invariably stored a great quantity of his belongings in a vault under No. 10, Downing-st., giving the sole charge of these to the office keeper, Mr. Foot. The latter kept a clock of Mr. Gladstone going without intermission during the whole time Mr. Gladstone was " out" from 1886 until he came in again in 1892. To-day not a Particle is left, which is strong confirmation of the fact that Mr. Gladstone does not expect ever to be "sent for'' again. He is now getting stronger in health, but still has a troublesome cough. As at present, arranged he comes to town on Saturday— not Thursday, as originally proposed.</p>
        <p>I learn that the London Tories, who are so eager to formulate a London programme, are no nearer the realisation of their purpose than they were two months ago, when they first suggested the iden.For two or three weeks an effort has been made to arrive at a common understanding, but it has failed because of the irreconcilability of the two sections into which London Toryism is divided. One section convinced that it would be well to make some con- cession to the progressive spirit of the day sug- gests some little concession to the proposal for uni- fication and some slight acknowledgment that Toryism is not altogether opposed to what is des- cribed as a moderate taxation of ground values. -However, the other section oppos:es this as relent- lessly as it does the London Liberal programme. The members of this section declare that it is their duty to oppose that programme. The members of this section declare that it is their duty to oppose that programme with a determined negative. The Duke of Norfolk, it, is worth noticinfr,sides with the progressive section,but the other section is so strong numerically that there will be no London Tory pro- gramme just yet.</p>
        <p>• *nt,s, --- • I have before noticed in these columns the num- ber of engagements in the "upper suckles" that have been broken off of late. Week after week the society papers have chronicled the fact that the " marriage arranged between So-and-so, the son of So-and-so, and Miss' So-and-so, the daughter of So- and-so, will not take place." How bald. how tan- talising ! Why will it not take place ? Why are these two (presumably) fond hearts to be severed ? lies a stern father or an unbending mother come between them and ruffled the smooth love waters into a maelstrom of celibacy? Or has a richer party appeared? Or has young Lord This been found cheating at cards or owing "debts of hen- our"? or young the Hon. Miss That in an intrigue or an affaire du cceur in which her fianc6 has no part?</p>
        <p>It really is very aggravating to be left thus in the dark. And, seriously, what does it all mean? These matches in high life aro nearly always the work of papa and mamma—with the consent of the young people, of course—and they are not made public until everything is satisfactorily settled. What can it be, then, of such serious import, that causes the snapping of the chain?</p>
        <p>The widowed Archduchess Stephanie of Austria has joined the already full ranks of lady novelists. She has recentiv finished an exciting romance, which is to be illustrated by the hest Austrian artists. The Arch. duchess has previously written several descriptions of travel, but this is her first attempt at fiction.</p>
      </div>
