      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 31, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 31, 1894.</p>
        <p>Coffee Starch.</p>
        <p>Mix two tablespoonfuls of the best starch with sold water to a paste ; when free from lumps stir into this paste a pint of boiling hot coffee, perfectly clear ; stir it with a wax candle, and it is fit for use.</p>
        <p>How to Cleanse Gold and Silver Lace.</p>
        <p>Mix half a loaf of stale bread with a quarter of a pound of powder blue, rubbing the bread fine and mix- ing the blue with it. Sprinkle thickly over the lace, and in a short time brush off the crumbs with a piece of flannel, and rub softly with a piece of red velvet.</p>
        <p>How to Renovate a Crape Veil. The following instructions are taken from the "Ladies' Home Journal ":—Cover a board with a piece of coloured flannel ; pin the crape smoothly on this. Use a large flat- iron, very hot. Take a piece of cloth large enough to cover the veil and wet it in cold water ; then wring it nearly dry and lay it over the crape. Pass the iron lightly over the wet cloth, keeping the weight of the iron in the hand. Continue this until the whole piece of crape is pressed.</p>
        <p>Everybody's Medicine Chest. Many will be astonished when they learn that they have had a medicine chest in the house for years with- out knowing it—in the shape of a set of well-filled cruets, The salt, for example, is a decided cathartic in the dose of half an ounce or an ounce ; it is also a vermifug,e in large closes and sometimes acts as a tonic. The vinegar, also, is refrigerant, and moderately stimulant and as- tringent when applied externally. It is also useful when soda, potash, or ammonia are taken in over-doses, as the acetic acid which it contains combines with and chemi- cally neutralizes them. Mustard comes next, to which at one time it was the fashion to attribute every virtue under heaven. A mustard emetic was extolled as in- fallible in cholera. A mustard poultice is no mean rival of a blister. Olive oil has great merits. The best dis- pensatory that we have tells us that it is " demulcent, relaxant, and laxative." It is a good antidote against acid poisons. Nor is our chest deficient in stimulants. First comes the common pepper—the black is the stronger. Among its most special virtues let as mention its power, when infused in water, of curing a relaxed throat ; and piperin, the alkaloid extracted from it, has cured ague, An ointment of black pepper has been re- commended against ringworm. The cayenne possesses similar virtues, but in a very exalted degree. It is the king of peppers, and shows itself worthy of its high re- putation. The spice-box and the herbarium of the pan- try would afford materials for a lecture on the " materia medica." Two articles have good testimony in their favour—that is, sage and cinnamon, often eulogized in verse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, So far the weather has been undecided, and weather, you know, has a great deal to do with the fashions. If, however, the promise of a fine warm spring is realised, the ordinary jacket will, I fancy, be given the cold shoulder, as the prevailing idea with every tailor and dressmaker of note appears to be to fashion their dress bodices as much as possible on the lines of an outdoor coat, at the same time preserving as well as maybe the more " fussy " appearance of the house bodice. The new toques are made on an oval shape, like an elongated crown without the brim, and there is a wide spreading bow in front, and a smaller one at the back, while the inevitable violets, generally of velvet, nestle amongst the bows. A brown toque had yellow mimosa scplrating the front loops of velvet, and another cluster peeping below the bow at the back, and almost resting on the hair.</p>
        <p>A quite new and remarkably stylish hat Is of fawn felt, the brim curved in a most becoming manner, with a small bow of geranium•pink velvet resting on the hair in front. The trimming con- sists of large loops of black satin ribbon, the centre one covered with deep cream guipure. These are secured with long pins, finished with large balls of cut jet. A very stylish bodice is of fancy whipcord, trimmed Russian galon, and the back of the bodice is quite tight, but trimmed across to simulate a loose zouave. The front of the lining is arranged to represent a pouffed blouse, with stripe of galon, and the loose zouave over parts are turned back in large revers, and edged galon. This is a particu- larly smart fashion for one material, and the zouave fronts can be fixed down closely if the figure is full and round.</p>
        <p>Blouses and bodices will be very popular during the coming season. Half-worn skirts can be renovated and worn with a silk or muslin bodice eithet at home or abroad. The pretty Eton jacket so popular during last Beason will be worn again. Navy blue is destined to be the favourite colour, so that anyone can clean and retrim 'last season's dress and feel sure that she is "in the fashion." The indications are that skirts will be plain, bell-shaped, and of moderate width. Ladies in moderate circumstances act wisely when they rein- eto follow the very latest fashion. It is almost impossible to remodel a dress skirt that is wide around the bottom and scant about the waist. It is a good plan to allow at least one full width of the material to be gathered or plaited at the back. If the hips are prominent the added fulness will prevent the flat appearance that is so often a distinctive feature of home dress- making. If the figure is slight, the gathers should be drawn towards the hips. If this plan is adopted a dress can be made over without the addition of new material.</p>
        <p>In walking and visiting gowns of the smarter type some of the French models show the effect of violent contrast, but for English taste would be preferable in contrasting shades of the same colour —for instance, the following gown could be carried out in reseda and olive with admirable effect. The model has a bell skirt of bright solferino (ruby) velvet with a pointed over-skirt of relied" habit, oloth, edged with shaded green passamenterie. The waistband is of the folded cloth, and the back of the drapery hangs somewhat like the back of the Greek "peplum."</p>
