        <p>• a .s .-czot„) ; ~ ,e, ..,„ burns, and bruises it is far ahead of any ointmcnt 1 that has ever been before the public. LORD CAR,RICK says "HOMOCEA" cured him of bleeding piles, when all else failed: that he gave , some to a labourer who was lamed by a stone failing upon him, whom it cured. A woman had a pain in the elbow and could not bend it af onrdaanyneathre, rad nsnedititefunrred her, scury y on her leg, and it was doing- her good. One letter closes from him with the words, "It is the most won- derful stuff that I ever came across." LORD COMBERMERE says " Ammer-4." did him more good than any embrocation he had ever I used for rheumatism. 1 Remember thipk,, "HomocEA" i subduek , pou nflammation and -; :.,,, allays irritation al- most as soon as applied. ' lAll ;wholesale -houses stock" Homocne.." It can be obtained from PlaA mists and others at is. lid or 2s. 9d; per box, or ii.ll be sent by post for Is. 3d. and 35., from the r Wholesale Agency, 21, Hamilton Square, Birkenherd. .) , , . , • ~ .; f - , 'Sold in the neigheurb9ol 337 J. R. YOUNG, SANKEY- STREET, NV,, RRI NGT ON ; by the executors of the late ISAAC SPEAKMAN, RRIDGP,-STREET PHARMACY, RUNCORN ; and by by E. BOOTH, 5, CHESTER BRIDGE, CREWE.</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE;</p>
        <p>On, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>By the Author of " DARKWOOD."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI. HIDDEN LIVES AND LOVES.</p>
        <p>This is the life which those who fret in guilt And guilty cities never know; the life Led by primeval afr,z4.*.lcorrupt, When angels dwe'.4 and od himself, with man. TIIOMSON.</p>
        <p>Leaning upon the -arm 0 the Spanish count, the Lady Beatrice traseiO4 lighted hall, descended a broad staircase, crostied the lower hall, and paused before the door of the drawing-room. Sounds of music and merriment came from within. Enjoining silence by uplifted finger, she listened to the sounds as if they were inexpressibly dear to her.</p>
        <p>Then she softly opened the door, and the two stood looking in upon a scene brighter than any fabled to belong to fairy-land. The room was a long, wide, low drawing-room, with bay windows recessed with lace curtains. It was lighted by pendant chandeliers, and fur- nished in a style at once gay, light and cheerful. There were rare pictures on the walls, exquisite statuettes on carved brackets, and a profusion of elegantly bound books on the centre table. The silken couches exhibited pillows executed in the most beautiful embroidery. All that could charm the eye, educate the tastes, or give comfort to the esthetic senses in such an apartment, found an appropriate place here. The room had three occupants. Before an exquisitely carved piano, the conn- terpart of one at Hampton House, sat a fair-haired boy, witk long curls floating about his shoulders. His delicate features, his wide violet eyes, his faintly flushed cheeks, all expressive of enthusiasm, as he evoked sweet harmonies from the pearl keys, were indicative of a poetic soul, and a nature not meant to cope with the hard realities of life. Although scarcely eleven years of age, he was already regarded as possessed of genius, and there hung about him, like the fragrance about a rose, a faint tinge of that melancholy that usually accom- panies genius. _</p>
        <p>- He I,N7as Fay, the youngest child of the count, the darling of his mother. _ln the centre of the room, keeping time with graceful motion to the witching dance music, were two others, the remaining children of the count and the Lady Beatrice. The younger .of the two was a boy of fifteen, with the dark locks and flashing black eyes of his mother. All fire and spirit, all life and energy, as handsome as a boyish Apollo, gay, light-hearted and joyous, he was amply gifted with talent, and with all practical qualities as well. Noble, pure. minded and affectionate, he was a son of whom to be proud. He was Rupert, the eldest son and heir of the count.</p>
        <p>The third member of the group was a girl of six- teen, as beautiful as a poet's ideal. She was slen- der in figure and as graceful as a young willow swaying with every breeze. Her complexion was dark, yet clear, and was coloured in the cheeks with the soft tinting that lines the sea-shell. Her hair was black and hung about her classic little head in tiny curls. Her eyes were blue, the deep- est, most intense blue, .that melt with feeling into the most liquid black. They were large and lus- trous and Ni ere shaded by long, heavy lashes that curled slightly at their edges. Her forehead was wide, and seemed the throne of a noble intellect. Her face mirrored a sunny disposition, a loving heart, and a resolute soul. She was noble, gener- ous, spirituelle. She was Giralda, the earliest offspring of the mysterious marriage between the Spanish count and the Lady Beatrice Hampton. She was moving with the slow, soft grace of a native Spaniard, her brother's arm around her, the German waltz flooding the room with melody. Her dress of scarlet merino swayed with her movements and the tendrils of her hair seemed endowed with separate vitality, dancing over her brow upon their own account.</p>
        <p>The three young people were so absorbed in their pleasant occupation that they did not notice the opening of the door, or the two admiring spectators who stood framed in the doorway. The face of the Lady Beatrice glowed with joy and pride. There was no vestige in face or manure now of the coldness and haughtiness which even her own father deemed a part of her nature. Her dark eyes beamed lovingly upon each member of the little group, resting longest perhaps upon the boy at the piano. "Are they' not beautiful, my husband ? " she asked, her eyes filling with happy tears. "They are different from each other, but all are good, all are noble—"</p>
        <p>She paused, as the music and the dance stopped suddenly, and a joyful shout announced that her presence had been discovered. In a moment she was the centre of a busy, happy group. Soft, clinging arms were around ber ; soft lips sought her own, and glad voices welcomed her.</p>
        <p>Giralda, allvays affectionate and thoughtful, drew her to a great armed chair, and placed a footstool for her feet. Rupert, trembling with delight, hung about her, his arm around her shoulders. Fay, slight and small, sought his usual resting-place on such occasions—his mother's lap. "I don't see that there's any place for me," said the count, standing near, with an expression of preteimed despair, that looked strangely on his dark Spanish face, that seemed intended only to express the gloomier and sterner emotions. " Othello's occupation's gone, I am one too many. I believe I shall have to go up to my study and spend my evening in solitude." • • He made a feint of carrying his threat inte exe- cution.</p>
        <p>"Oh, no, papa," said the gentle Fay, Sliding from his mother's lap and rushing to his father. " There'a room for you. Come back ! " Amid the laughter of the others he drew the coulit to a chair close 'beside that of the Lady Bea- trice, then resumed his own resting-place. "We did not dream of your coming home to- night, mamma,' said Giralda, standing behind her mother's chair and caressing her tenderly. "Hear how the wind howls among the trees. It rained so drearily that I told papa I knew you would not cornet"</p>
        <p>• " I knew you would, mamma," murmured Fay, nestling contentedly on his mother's breast. "Little trusting heart ! " murmured the Lady Beatrice, kissing her girl-like darling, the child whom, unconsciously, she loved the best. "But how did you come from the station, ma- ma ? " asked Rupert, in his bright, impetuous way. "The carriage was not sent ! " "I came in a cab, dear. You all seem to think I have done something wonderful in coming home on a wet night," said the Lady Beatrice, smiling. "That odious opera !" sighed Rupert, his bright dark face clouding. "Oh, mamma, I cannot bear to have you working so bard for us, and away from home, too! If we could only have you with us all the time 1"</p>
        <p>The count echoed the sigh, and his face clouded heavily. Giralda, from her position behind her mother, gave Rupert a warning look, which he compre- hended and acted upon by immediately becoming cheerful. The young girl then approached her father, bent over him, caressing him, and whisper- ing: "Dear.papa, some time mamma will be with us all the time. For her sake let us be cheerful."</p>
        <p>She kissed him and flitted away, leaving the room. " Giralda is our home fairy," said Fay, softly having been observant of his sister's ministratior s. "She makes sunshine for us all, mamma. She teaches us games and plays, and reads us stories, and comiorts us all when you are gone. She is helping me write a piece of poetry to you." " What a good sister!" said the Lady Beatrice, lightly. " Yes, she is !" assented Rupert, his face light- ing up with brotherly tenderness and affection. "But, mamma, she has taken a new tarn lately that I don't like. She's learning to keep house, she says. Meggy don't want her t; learn, but Gir- alda says we can't always expect to have Meggy Fleck, if she is your foster-sister, mamma,. Gir- alda. made the pudding yesterday and to-day, and now she has gone to see about tea." , The Lady Beatrice glanced towards her hus- band.</p>
        <p>" Rupert is right," he said. " Giralda is acquir- ing a knowledge of housekeeping. She is already a skillful little housekeeper, for Aleggy, it seems, has been giving her lessons during the past year. I think Giralda sensible, my darling ; do not you Formerly English ladies, of however high birth, thought their education unfinished if it did not in- clude a practical knowledge of housekeeping." " Yet I do not quite approve this new pursuit of Giralda," said the Lady Beatrice, thoughtfully. "She has such lovely little wliite hands, and her</p>
        <p>complexion is so pure and el-ear. 'iliore is no knowing what may happen, my husband. Giralcla may soon be called to a higher station, and I desire to keep her as delicate and dainty as she is now." The discussion was terminated by the entrance of its subject. "The supper is ready," she announced, with a pretty assumption of matronly dignity, at which all laughed. "Rupert, dear, your arm, if you please." Rupert offered the desired member. Giralda took it gravely, and the two followed in the steps of the count and countess. Fay kept close beside his mother. Behind the chair intended for the mistress of the house stood a woman, the exact counterpart of Mrs. Ma:ry Fleck, the tiring-woman of the Lady Beatrice at Hampton House. This woman, Mrs. Meggy Fleck, was the twin sister of Mary. Both were called mistress out of courtesy. Neither had ever married, and both esteemed themselves hon- ored and blessed in sharing with Count Arevola the secret of the Lady Beatrice—a secret from the knowledge of which even the children of the mar- riage were ignorant. The two women, Meggy and Mary, were foster-sisters of their beautiful mis- tress, and either would have died for her, could .such a sicrifice have benefited her ladyship. The Lady Beatrice entered the dining-room greeting her faithful servitress with affection shaking her hand warmly. " Mary sent her love Meggy," she said, "and will be down on Sunday to see you. So you are surprised to see me? Your table does not show it."</p>
        <p>The housekeeper's grim face lighted up with pleasure. "The arrangements were all made for the sup- per, my lady, before the storm came on," she said, "To think of your being out on such a night as this. Dear, dear I shouldn't a been easy if I'd a known it."</p>
        <p>The Lady Beatrice took her scat at the table, her excursion having given her an appetite. The count sat opposite to her, Giralda at his right, Rupert at his left. Fay sat beside his mother, as usual.</p>
        <p>But one person waited at table, that one being the housekeeper herself. But three servants were kept at the Evergreens, Mrs. Fleck included, the second one being a German woman, who rarely saw her mistress, and who comprehended but a few words of English, and the third being the coachman and gardener, a faithful and devoted fellow. Mrs. Fleck was the . humble devotee of the family, the ardent lover of the children, the trusted, trust- worthy friend. The count did the honours of the occasion, broaching a bottle of rare wine in celebration of the joyful event of the evening. The Lady Beatrice looked on the fearless joyous faces around the board, and at the glowing, happy one opposite her, and she fairly outshone herself in brilliancy of speech and loveliness. Never, at her father's table had her laugh .been heard, but here it bubbled forth like a mountain spring. Never had she been wittier or more delightful, and never had she had more appreciating audience than in the count and her three children.</p>
        <p>The inmates of the Evergreens lived a very se- cluded life; seeing no visitors, rarely quitting their own domain except for daily drives in a pony car- riage. The neighbours knew only that a Spanish count and his family resided there, but they made no effort to become acquainted with the countess. who was reported to be an actress plying her pro- fession under an assumed name in London. Coun- try society was not anxious to make the acquaint;- ance of an actress, or opera-singer, and no visitor ever darkened the doors of the Evergreens. The garden door was always kept locked, and no curious eye ever obtained sight of the inmates of the dwell- ing. The sunny gardens in the rear of the cottage afforded play, ground and walks for the young people, it not being overlooked at any point by neighbouring dwellings. The little fiction that the countess was an [tams was taught also to the children by good Meggy Fleck. No one had a doubt of the truth of the story, and not one wondered that their mother visited them but once a week, generally coming at night. They knew that she came ill-clad, but they also knew that the Lady Beatrice was very beautiful, and they had a vague idea that it would not be quite safe for her to go about unprotected and un- disguised. The Lady Beatrice, as already indicated, had been married many years. Giralda had been born at the Evergreens, as had the others, and they had never spent a night from their home. The three had all been born during the absence of Lord Ham- ton, who had been urged by his daughter to visit the German baths for his health, and who had effected those visits at the very time desired. On each occasion the Lady Beatrice, under pretence of visiting a distant friend, had come to the Ever- " greens to give birth to ct child. This, in brief, is the outline of her ladyship's double existence. - -</p>
        <p>At her father's house the haughty, cold, mysteri ous belle; seemingly heartless and opposed without reason to matrimony. At The Evergreens, the gentle, loving wife; the proud, devoted mother; tho idol of loving hearts; the sunny angel of home! And as' regally beautiful as she was in the for- mer character, it was in the latter that she became actually radiant, her loveliness assuming a higher character, and her soul shining from every feature. " Ah, this is a rare holiday ! " cried the count, enjoying it as if he had not had a similar one once a week for years, his countenance all aglow with joy. His children looked at him in laughing sympathy. The Lady Beatrice looked at each member of the little group, from golden-haired Fay to the count, her countenance reflecting their pleasure. Sud- denly the remembrance of Lord Adlowe, obtruded itself upon her soul, and a pang shot through her heart and a shudder convulsed her frame.</p>
        <p>" Ah," she thought, a strange fear creeping into her soul, as Lord Adlowe's looks and woras repro- duced themselves with sudden force, "what if that wolf should break into this happy, hidden fold 1"</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
