      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Five O'clock Tea Fortune.</head>
        <p>A Five O'clock Tea Fortune.</p>
        <p>At an afternoon tealrecently, a bright young girl begged leave to inspect the dregs of her hostess teacup and straightway evolved from their peculiar shape, size, and situation such an amusing horoscope of the owner's tuture career as called forth the laughing plaudits of her 11( arers. So writes Alice P. Baker in the February "Ladies' Home Journal." A quaint superstition is that of believing that destiny lies hidden in the innocent look- ing dregs of a teacup, and an idea suggested itself which was speedily materialized for a tea-party which we were about to give. On heavy white water-colour paper I first drew a prettily and oddly shaped teacup,aiad on the reverse side a second one. On heavy linen note paper I drew a goodly number of leaves of the same shape. The two heavy covers I painted blue and white, adding little touches of gold here and there, where most effective. An irregularly-shaped white space was left in the centre of the top cover, in which was printed with pen and gold ink, "Five o'clock tea." The inner side of the cup was simulated at the top, the edges being outlined with gold. Within this rim was printed "Cup of Fortune." On the first of the note paper leaves was painted a design of a hand holding a tiny inverted teacup with the lines :</p>
        <p>Leaves of tea Show for me My destiny ! extending carelessly down one side in gold letters. Holes were then punched in both covers and leaves at the top of the handle, and finP gold cord run through and fas- tened securely. A length of the cord, to which a small, white programme pencil was attached, depended from this. The fair young possessor was to ask the friends gathered about her tea-table to read her fortune im the depths of her own cup and then transfer it, with date and signature, to the leaves of the little volume.</p>
        <p>Development of Children.</p>
        <p>In height a boy should measure at birth on an average 18 to 19 'inches ; a girl some half an inch less—the range of health lying between 16 and 22 inches. The child grows with rapidity dorinv the first year—faster than during any other period of the same length ; so that it gains about 8 inches, measuring, when twelve months old, about 27 inches, its weight being about 191bs. Dur- ing the second year it gains only 4 inches on an average, and Sibs. in weight, reaching a statue of 31 inches, and a weight of 241bs. But these fivures represent only the average, the extremes Yanging between wide limits. With regard to weight and plumpness, it should he re- membered that during the second year, when children learn to walk and run about, they naturally become thinner : their incessant restless activity requires for the production of force exerted,a large amount of fuel-food, and it is used to a greater extent in this way, instead of being stored up as fat, as it is during the inactive period of earlier infancy. Children, again. may he extremely fat, and yet deficient in solid bone and muscle. One of the prize children of the first baby show was brought to me, says a writer in " Cassell's Book of Health " suffer- ing from rickets, fat, flabby, and cartilaginous. Yet there is no harm in the fat in itself ; many healthy chil- dren are strikingly so before they begin to run about, especially if fed on food which contains a large propor- tion of sugar, as Swiss milk, for example. It is the want of colour, and hardness and firmness of flesh in addition, which is unsatisfactory. as seen in soft white children fed on artificial farinaceous food. As a rule, children of average development begin to walk from the twelfth to the eighteenth month. Those who learn to crawl often find this mode of locomotion sufficient, and will not trouble to learn to walk until two years old or more. Large heavy children, especially if at all rickety, should not be encouraged to walk before eighteen or twenty months old. With regard to talking, also,there is great variation. 'llle baby usually begins to lisp a Word or two soon after the end of the first year ; but if a child has learnt the art of pantomimic gesture to ex- press its wants, it may be two years old or more before it will take the trouble to use articulate speech. One of the standard signs of the progress of bone development is the condition of the anterior fontanelle--s-the so-called " soft place " in the head, well known to mothers and nurses. It is situated on the top of the skull, about one- third of the way between the forehead and the crown. The fontanelle represents a space between the bones at the point where they approach together, which is bridged over by membrane at birth, and is gradually obliterated by extension of bony formation as growth proceeds. At first it is of irregular quadrangular form, in area per- haps equal to a florin. At six months this should be re- duced to the size of a shilling or thereabouts. At the end of the first year the membraneous portion should not be larger than sixpence, and it thenceforth rapidly decreases ; so that from the twentieth to the twenty- fourth month. if bone formation proceeds naturally, the opening f-hould be no longer appreciable to the touch, although the actual joining of the bones is not finally completed until much later, viz., about the fifth year. This clocure of the fontanelle at the end of the second year affords a fair test by which to judge whether the development of the hone is proceeding regularly accord- ing to the standard rule.—From " Cassell's Book of Health "for March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RESTORING PARADISE.</head>
        <p>RESTORING PARADISE.</p>
        <p>• Many now-a-days,labour under mistaken notions con- cerning the teachings of the Bible, and many there are who intrepret the teachings of the book in a wrong sense, while others do not scruple to make fun of certain scrip- tural personages. However, fun is all well enough if it is innocent. The following is slightly trite, but, never- theless, it crops up new and again. It is said to have originated on the Continent, but any well-informed Per- son will reject that theory. and lay the blame on our. American cousins. Some call it a travesty on "Paradise Regained." ' An artist,' we are told, was employed. in a Cathedral to repair the interier, Ste., and was asked; when finished with his work, to submit. a" detailed statement of his account." :Here it ii •</p>
        <p>(1) Corrected the Ten Commandments £1 10 0 (2) Embellished Pontius 'Pilate and put a ribbon in his bonnet 0 8 1 (3) Put a New,Tail on the Rooster of St. Peter . _ _</p>
        <p>and mended his 'Comb 0 12 0 (4) Re-plumed and gilded the Left Wing of the • Guardian Angel 0 15 6 (5) Washed Yhe Servant of the High Priest and put carmine on his Cheek 0 1 0. (6) Renewed Heaven, adjusted two Stars, and CleanelLthe Moon 1 16 0 (7) Re-animated the flames of Purgatory and Restored the Souls (8) Revived the.lilameisof Hell, put a blew Tail on theiDevilmeaded his left hoof, and didseveraljoba for the.damned ...... 1 16 6 (9) Re-bordering the-Robes of 'Herod and re- - adjusting•hiaWig 0 17 3 (10) PO new: spottardashes on, the Son of Tobias, and dressing.on his sack - 0 7 6 (11) Cleaned the Ears of Balaam's Ass and shod , him (12) Put Earrings in the Ears of Sarah ..... • 0 9 2 (131 Put a new Stonein, Davidl Sling,enlarged . the *to of Goliath, and extended his • legs ' 0 8 8 (14) Decorated Noah % Ark. , •0 17 6 Mended the shirt of the Prodigal Son, and Cleaned his Ears 0 15 3'</p>
        <p>• £l7lO 5</p>
        <p>' VADBURYI3 uocos has, in a remarkable degree, those oatural elements of sustenance which give the system endur- ance,and hardihood, with a steady action . that renders it a rnst acceptable and reliable beverage."-- fiealth.</p>
        <p>A society scribe at Monte Carlo gossips about the people there. The Duchess of Devonshire (she says) always dresses well. She is indefatigable in her expedi- tions, and is often at the tables with her son enjoying a gamble. Baron Rothschild plays now and then, but never seems to know if he is winning or losing. Colonel North is heart and soul in the game.</p>
        <p>Thr, Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STthicu GLos.: is unequalled for imparting a beautifnl finish to starched linen, &amp;c., either cold or boiliug water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs. Bradford : "Dar sit,— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at id., 3d., and 6d.„by most starch Ballets. If not in stock will procure it. T. CRITCELE7, Blultburn, P:oprietor.—lldot.</p>
        <p>OPENING OF NEW BOARD SCHOOLS AT MOULTON.</p>
        <p>MR. BRUNNER, M.P., ON THE EDUCATIONAL MOVEMENT, Mr. J.T. Bruuner, M.P., on Monday, formally opened new school at Moulton, near Northwichs, which have been erected by a school board at cost of £2,600. Twelve mo.iths ago an agitation. was set on foot which resultea in a bitter contro- versy between the Church and unsectarian parties. The • 214 village children had to walk two or three miles to attend school. To remedy this the Bishop of Chester guaranteed the erection of a national school, but at a town's meet- ing,the people of Moulton ciccided that unless they could secure proper control they would establish a school Al board. Mr. Brunner, M.P., was appealed to, and be •srs offered £5OO towards the building of a school to be con- .4 trolled by a school board. The Offer was accepted, and it, the work executed. Mr. Brunner, who drove into Moul- ton,. was met by a procession of members of the School Board and children, headed by a brass band. Arches , of evergreen and bunting had been erected in the A, principal streets, and the village was en fete. On reach- • -1' ing the schools, Mr. Brunner was presented with a sil- ver gilt key, which bore his coat of arms and the in- scription "Presented to J. T. Brunner, Esq., M.P., March 26, 1894, at the opening of the Moulton Board Schools." Having returned thanks, the member for the Northwich division inspected the institution and then delivered an address. He asked them to always remember that that was the first building which had ever been reared belonging to the people of Moulton. The move- ment in which they were taking part was connected , with a great national movement which might be des- cribed as having begun with the Free Libraries Act and , to have been continued by the Education Act of 1870, and nobly extended by the Local Government Act of 1894. (Applause.) In their time, there haa been legis- ' lation—commencing with the abolition of the Corn . Laws, the adoption of Free Trade, and the establish-- ment of savings banks—which had for its object the - raising of the material well-being of the pesple, and 411 which, coupled with the efforts of the working classes to benefit themselves by their own exeetions and through . isTL the operations of trade unions, had accomplished a vast •1• amount of good. But the educational movement had for its object the raising of the whole population in cul- ture, self-respect, and self-reliance. Since the year be- I fore the adoption of the Education Act of 1870, they had reduced the unhappy population of our prisons by one-third, and there had been a great advance in the in- telligence and manners of the masses. Some day they • might be satisfied with the amount of comfort to be had by every family in the land ; but they would never com- plete the raising of the people in culture and self-respect. They would never all be Newtons, Shakesfa-ares, or Mil- , tons, but it was a glorious goal at which to aim, and it, was because they had entered definitely and formally upon the work that be was with them heart and soul.— (Aiiplause.), The erection of those schools was the alop- „, tion of a very powerful means in the prosecution of the work, and the building itself was an actual and veritable embodiment of this desire to help i 4 the work and to • "-- benefit it for generations to come. They would always t!'e able to come there with the proud consciousness that the building belonged to them, subject to no control whatever, and there, their children would meet with in- • i 11'; ilisences which would be of the greatest importance in .• securing that the future geneeation should be honourable, courteous, strong and capable,and religious. (Applause.). There had never been a well-conducted school which did not improve the physical health of the children, and if at first some came there bearing the marks of home neg- lect the number of such would steadily diminish. (Ap- plause.) He trusted that there would never be a word of peevish complaint about having-to pay ss. or 63. a year for all these advantages. The rate would, he believed, amount to ss, or 6,-3. for the first . year, and afterwards would be 2s. 63. or 3s. per house S; but this sum was nathing when compared with the benefits which would be derived by their little ones. (Cheers.). A cordial vote of thanks was tendered to Mr. Brunner- for his gesierous gift to the schools of £5OO. At the- close of the meeting all the school children and a large- Number of Moulton people were entertained to tea. AD. entertainment was held in the evening.</p>
