      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>GLAD TIDINGS.</head>
        <p>GLAD TIDINGS.</p>
        <p>-Yes. Each day is a parable of life. Morning repre- • Bents youth ; noon is middle life ; evening is ripe and and sober age. 'Tis rounded at either end by sleep ; unconscionsness at the outset, and oblivion at the end. And the lesson is this : Occupy the time. No matter about yesterday ; that is gone. No matter about to-. 31"rrow ; it may never come to us. To-day, only is eur. This we can make what we will. Let others' ex- rrieace judiciously guide our present actions. Major Alctaurrav, of Springfield House, Killuney, Armagh, in hue, 1890, writes : " I was a martyr to rheurnatic gout from 1855 until April 18E3 ; being laid up with it regularly every six months ; soineVrnes for weeks and sornetimes for months. In April, 1888, I commenced to ,take Warner's Safe Cure. I found relief after the fourth !bottle. I took twenty-one bottle. Since I stopped tak- l_tig the medicine' in Julv, 1888, I have not had the atlghtest indication of rheumatic gout." In August, he again writes : " I have pleasure in confirming above statement, made three years ago, and adding : 1117 Old enemy has not visited me since."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LOCAL VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL. h The vestry meeting in connection with the Grappen- iti Parish Church was held on Tuesday morning. The tair Was occupied by the Rev. R. Greenall, rector, and ere were also present—Messrs. A.Lioter Bave,Leyland, Jones, Mainwaring, and Burrows. Messrs. Ley- Lister Kaye were appointed churchwardens, and Messrs. Daltin and Jones sidesmen. Messrs. Dakin rid Leyland were elected to attend the ruri-deacanal con- Thegr accounts were pasied, and thc,. meeting sed with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
