        <head>STRETTON CHURCEL</head>
        <p>STRETTON CHURCEL</p>
        <p>The - annual vestry meeting of the parish was held in schcnlroom at noon on Monday. The vicar (the -",y• Canon Dodd) presided. The following were present 411"—Yr. Moberly, and. Messrs. C. T. Whitley. H. Derby- ire, J. Whitlow,J. Ellison, Thos. Pixcival, E. Brockle- blipt, and J. Rutter. The accounts, which showed a aaixe in hand, were read and pissed. The following, Zeers were appointed :—Parish warden, Mr. J. Ron- 'w • vicar's warden, Mr. J. Rutter. Two sidesmen )4ere appointed for the first time, instead of one, viz., thesBrs. E. Brocklehurst, and H. Derbyshire. A vote of lenks was unanimously pissed to the retiring wardens A eB6, ra• El. Derbyshire,and J. Whitlow for their services. am:War compliment having been accorded to the chair- for presiding, the meeting terminated. The ringers 154 usual, were entertained to dinner at the Cat and Lion 4,4+ and several peals were rung on the bells during the</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH. ,„111e.annual vestry mecting in connection with this urch was held on Monday morning. The Vicar (the 1,711. V. Pigot) presided. The accounti showed a bal- c,..c'e in hand of £2O 14s. lEd. The Vicar nominated IT: Smethurst as his warden, and the vestry elected Mr. Chantry as the parishioner's warden. Mr. SmethurA el7llinated Mr. T. Morris as his sidesman, and Mr. Law- 'vas nominated as Mr. Chantry's sidesman. Six ,a4ditional sidesmen were elected by the vestry as follows: chbr. Adams and Messrs. J. Haves, J. Lawless, T. W. so:4ltry., J. Robertson, and J. Craddock. Mr. W. Robert. and Mr.D. W. Finney were elected lay represtatives: bit Was resolved that the pew rents for the next 12 viellths be paid to the Vicar, inf,tead of five sixths to the Car—ene sixth to the vestry—as before.—There was 81.23 e conversation about enlarging the vestry and the irevial of the tower clock. The usual votes of thanks ere Pused. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH. 1.,..The annual vestry meeting in connection with Holy ity Church, Warrington, was held on Tuesday even- the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, vicar, presided, and iee:gst those present were the Rev. H. R. Wild, and rs• W. J. Forshaw, E. Rose, James Harding, E. J. M. Beacon, T. Welsby, F. J. Dell, J. Dunbabin, Howson, John Hatton, W. H. Harrison, H. oCelit, R. Dobson, and George F. F. White (vestry 111:'0• Mr. Welsby read the accounts for the past year. git' receipts amounted to £264 2. 44d., and the expen- ititire. including a debt balance from last year of £7 7cl•.*Was -0265 13s. 10,12 d., leaving a balance due to 1,2 Wardens of £1 us. 6d. The accounts, which had audited by Mr. Harrison, were approved on the I°n of Mr. Hatton, seconded by Mr. Howson. The 4-4 nominated Mr. Welsby as his warden, and Mr. _attott Was elected people's warden. Mr. Welsby 4,,s'llinated Mr. Rowson as his sidesman, and Mr. Hatton jecinlinated Mr. Harding as his. The vestry elected as :—Messrs. E.Gaskell, 11. Routh, E.Booth, John bittopae2 E. Rose, John Hesketh, J. M. Beacon, W. J. Ite:;ww. H. J. Westbrook, R. Brierley, and Barnes. 81.8. R. W. Howson and R. Dobson, jun., were re- lay representatives, and Mr. White was re-ap- *et d vestry clerk. Messrs. Harrison and E. A. White e re-elected auditors. The meeting terminated with usual votes of thanks. ST. JAMES' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The arri ual -tr;"M- cr eetin .at St. James' Church ote-hford, was vheeld "on Monda-y, and was presided over the Vicar (the Rev. i . n presenting F. Sh,ter). Mr. C. S. Burgess warden) VriCar's - , the a lin •Tots said that the t, receipts for the rear amounted to £2Ol Os. 7d., and c.7expenditureo t £216 15. 1 Rd. It was a noteworthy t7set that Easter Day and tile Feast of Our Lady oecurred ul'agaetther. This had only happ-. ned once since 1744,and Was in the year 1883. They would probably rernern- gr the wmplet 'When Easter falls on Our Lady's ,lap lie w Let England then beware of a rap.' bee aa glad to say, however, that so far the year had %4,11 Unattended by any untoward event in national ft:fa. In alluding to the deficit in the accounts,Mr.Bur- PiC said that lie had gone to the trouble of ascertaining aadat, the cffertories amounted to in the previous years, `utind that they were much greater than at present. goliNretted this, especially as they were compelled to outside aid.—The Rev. F. Slater said he was die- ete take a more cheerful view of the aspect. They GreA hp doubt aware of the fact, that Sir Gilbert ' a/id-tlLall had become the patron of St. James' Church, had tried to make the living of the had'" Worth -2300, but he thmught lie had livi a difficult task, as the endowment on the hadrig was only worth £l9O per year. Sir Gilbert 1 ato4l3oght for £5OO a piece of land upon which now Of the new vicarae'h and they had had a further grant' turth„ made them by te Commissioners.—After a little tturc,,V :r uiscussion.the accounts were adopted.—Mr.C.S. Was was appointed vicar's warden, and Mr. John Bh Ltirt°o People's warden, and Messrs. Venn, Dagnall, Thaew' Aecto,h, and J. Milward were appointed sidesmen. to tut:;°; 011 Owing gentlemen were appointed as delegates a-,;'-`-leaconal and diocesan conferences :-.-Messrs. C. -rge-ss and B. N. Holiqiiton. Err ANN'S CHURCH. vestry tocciaog ()I b-. Ann's The annual * A Church was held 0 j'anlefintjusday morning, the curate•in-charge,the Rev. othert:lnes presiding. There' were present among Clica."„'r• E. W. Woods (clerk), Mr. W. H. Rigbey kr. It,vj- Warden), and Mr.,). Baird. The vicar appointed Dear a ja• Rigbey as his warden for the forthcoming bah e 4. Glover was appointed people's warden. 13retb--"wlug sidesmen were appointed : Messrs. H. H. ,raerton J. Baird, 'T. Houghton, Jo:•epli Bunch, and to tbll°r• There were also appointed as lay delegates Ranr,,uri-deat.onal conferences Messrs. John Baird and _ttlat tii`..,; The vicar remarked that he was anxious wbt t-`4ltY should take their proper share in the con- ter do L'ae church, and this he believed they would bet- at,tendina and taking part in these conferences. tiloer • Woods said that there generally was a aia to every dark cloud and lie was happy te, paaa_iat after the trouble through which they had 4,4te'ur during the previous year there had come pleas- tht?oble it.°BPects• They would be aware that sonic ),s'oosa s -as experienced by the fact that the vicar had kiTietlrhaattrltedbeelneave pilon taehd nce and that the Rev. J. BCcurate-in-charge, whilst to I,_tithiattaiu both Clergymen they had at first hardly suffi- tri,,,evealict°,9Lnleht to maintain one. He was glad to say Mrs, Beamont had relieved them in this I,_ generously increasing the endowment by Nartx, had been invested in Mersey Dock and re:ur , ru bonds. The trustees to this endowment ies 'l`. Reamont and Messrs. R. Davies, Vere ;toe "• Woods, and John Burgess. He thought tberia-Ww:,°gbition should be made of Mrs. Beamont's L'4lllla "t-3;,,'—Nir• W. H Itigbev proposed a vote of lleamont, and Mr. Baird seconded the ukvies %hleh was carried unanimously.—The Rev. Gitbe.r 'Posed a vote of thanks to Lord Lilford and ;',+ey h,ArL Greenall for generous contributions which gFanted during the year. Mr. Rigbey seconded 4046:1Z_tutp:10 which was carried. This concluded the ' s'o No accounts were submitted. 1311,!, v. WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. "1/4td at"nual vestry meeting of the above church was 11. Np tho National Scheols Church-st. on Tuesday &amp;,`Qkir. e Rector (Rev. F. W. Willis) occupied the tas 41;.ttbere were also present: Rev. F. W. Tim- A• A. Markham; Messrs. R. T. Fairclough, L'',utge• and H. B. White.—Mr. A. Woods, tht Nta.rden, submitted the annual accounts. Ile i'coatit4t Ile had greater pleasure in presenting the ;4.1 than he had twelve months ago. Considering jlt_aq:_t!ade of the smallpox in the sear as the result of the coal strike, and the V`a, t.litlbf_ the visitatiota jtopy haZirjal.ances were in a very prosperous condition. o,;,t'ota o.,s'ecadod to alter their financial year and reckon V;_9 .71ottnas to Christmas, and therefore he could howe:: e finances for nine months this year. He 41,54 thbritvheer' glad to tell them that the receipts for the ksiate,;;_..`ivere more than the receipts of the whole of (Applause.) Last year they had ttritted but during the past nine months they had (Applause.) Mr. Woods went on to various improvements made during the past th also to the improvements they had in view cmning year. He appealed to the people to 144,3411.. Si their-gifts to the church as it would be decorate the interior of the church during Loatiirav4:_t•welve months.—The accounts were parsed tiTloadee,7.ls-:-_- ,On the motion of Mr. S. Davies, ktiztlo2-27 xi% . Price, a vote of thanks was passed utsly to the churchwardens for their services dnr..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>\ AAI</head>
        <p>\ AAI</p>
        <p>ing the past year.—The Rector announced that he would nominate Mr. R. T. Fairclough again as his warden for the ensuing year.—Mr. Alfred Wo•cls was again unani- mously elected peeple's warden on the proposition of Mr. Peacock, seconded by Mr. W. Price.—Mr. Faircleugh appointed NIr.J. Walton as his sidesman for the year,and Mr. A. Woods appointed Mr. William Gill—The Rector said that perhaps the principal business of the meetin:2 had now to be done, viz., the election of eight other sidestnen. The church had suffered the loss of Mr. Nathan Percival who had died during the year. He (the Rector) could bear his testimony to the worth of the deceased gentleman. Another sidesman Mr. Walton had been chosen for another position so that these vacan- cies would have to be filled. The other retiring sides- men were Messrs. J. Craik, S. Davies, J. Dewhurst, T. Mason, T. Moores, W. Price and Scotson ; who were eligible for re-election.—The following were elected : Messrs. W. Price, J. Craik, J. E. Wright, T. Mason, J. Dewhurst, S. Davies W. Coates, and T. Moores. Mr. Banner was appointed sidesman for St. George's Mission, and Mr. Arthur Browne for Bank-st. Mission.—Messrs. J. D. Buckton and Wm. Coates were appointed lay representatives to the diocesan and ruriedeacenal conferences.—Mr. H. B. White was unanimously re- elected vestry clerk, and in replying, he said that he censidered it a high honour to be vestry clerk to the parish of Warrington. He had had the effice for thirty years, and should not like to lose it. There was no other office he valued so much.—The proceedings were brought to a close by the usual votes of thanks.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S, WALTON. The annual vestry meeting at St. John's Church, Wal- ton, was held on Monday morning, at eleven o'clock, when there were present :—The Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, 8.D., vicar ; Messrs. Burgess, Cooper, Muskett, J. Weir, W. Weir, and T. Lane, organist. The Vicar appointed Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., as vicar's warden (this for the fifth time), and in doing so expressed the hope that he (Sir Gilbert) might be spared to them for some time to come. Mr, John Weir was appointed the people's warden.</p>
        <p>ST. MICHAEL'S, BURTONWOOD. The annual Easter vestry meeting was held in the vestry of the church, on Thursday evening. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell presided, and there was a very small at- tendance. Mr. H. Gawthorpe presented the statement of accounts, and showed the income for the year to be £108,£70 of which had been derived from the offertories. The expenditure was £124 10s. ,showing a balance due to the wardens of £l6 10s. The Vicar's warden (Mr.Borron) did not seek re-appointment, and the Vicar withheld his nomination. Mr. Gordon was unanimously elected people's warden, and the sidesmen were appointed as follows—Messri.Perrins, Sanderson, Barrow,Gawthorp'e, and Rawlinson. The lay representatives appointed to the ruri-deaconal conference were—Messrs .VV.Fai rclough jun., and A. Jackson. Votes of thanks to the Chairman terminated the meeting, which was of a purely formal character.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL REFORMED CHURCH OF ENGLAND,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. The annual Easter vestry meeting was held in the Church Parlour over Arpley Station on Thursday even- ing, the vicar (Rev. Edward T. Reed) presiding. The meeting was opened with prayer, and the accounts for the past year submitted. The receipts had been £265 164., and the expenditure £2BO ss. 4d., leaving a balance of £l4 9s. 4d. A vote of thanks to the retiring church wardens, Messrs. S. Page and S. Lees was moved by the chairman, seconded by Mr. Norman, and was unanimously passed. Mr. S. Page was re-appointed vicar's warden; and Mr. John Allen, people's warden, the retiring warden,Mr.Lees,not seeking re-election. Mr.Pa,7e appointed Mr. G. Norman as his sidesman and Mr. Henry Gandy was appointed by Mr. Allen in a similar capacity The following were elected membixi of the vestry for the ensuing year. Messrs. Thomas Gandy,Samuel Lees George Norman, H. Gandy, H. Landon, W. Parsons, Thomas Allen, and Wm. Atherton. Messrs. S. Page, and H. Gandy, were elected representatives on the Nor- thern Council of the Reformed Church of England, The Vicar nominated the following as a Parish Council for the ensuing year : Mr. and Mrs. .14tIn Allen, Mr. Joseph Callan, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Gandy, Mr. Joseph Heron, junr., Miss Lees, Mrs. Norman, Mr. W. Parsons, and Mrs. Reed. Mr. Arthur Landon was appointed auditor. A cordial vote of thanks to the organist, choir master,and choir for their services during the year was unanimously passed. A vote of thanks to the chair man was passed unanimously. The meeting closed with prayer and the benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The Testy meeting Of Christ Church, Padgate, was held in the vestry, on Monday morning. In the absence of the Vicar (Rev. J. J. Langharn), the chair was occu- pied by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright, L.Th. (curate), and amongst those present were—Messrs. John Houghton, William Houghton, F. Blackwell, James Houghton, Thomas Houghton, J. W. Houghton. J. W. Fowles, W. Royston, W. Hodgkinson, T. H. Granger, and T. S. Steel. —The accounts were presented by Mr. W. Royston I(vicar's warden). and passed. The total receipts rneunted to £53 75., the warden's collections being £4B 3s. The balance in hand was £7 17s. 3d.—Mr. William Houghton moved the adoption of the accounts, I Mr. John Houghton seconded, and they were passed. —The Chairman read a letter from the Vicar appointing Mr. William Flodgkinson as his churchwarden. lie nominated the following gentlemen as sidesmen : Messrs. Wm. Royston, Wm. Houghton, Joseph W. Beevers, and George Flatten. Mr. Longhorn added : " Under the altered circumstances of the church at Or- ford, it appears desirable to appoint additional sides- men to serve at Orford. I,therefore, venture to suggest that the vestry should proceed to elect four such addi- tional sidesmen. In the event of this suggestion being accepted by the vestry, I beg to nominate Mr.J. Corbett and Mr. Charles Grounds as the two Vicar's sidesmen for Orford, leaving the vestry to appoint the other two. Mr. J. W. Fowles was appointed parishioners' warden, and Messrs. J. W. Houghton, F. M. Appleton, G. Mather, and J. Gibson people's sidesmen for Padgate. Messrs. W. Pollitt and John Grounds were appointed people's sidestnac for Orford. Mr. James Houghton, as allotment warden for the township of Woolstoti, pre- sented the accounts, which showed a balance of 12s. 9d. in hand. Mr. Royston proposed that Mr. J. W . Fowles be elected senior warden: Mr. T. Houghton seconded, and the proposition was carried. Messrs. W. Hodgkin- son and W. Houghton were appointed to 'represent Pad- gate at the Diocesan Conference, and Messrs. W. Corbett and W. Pollitt, Orford. A long discussion took place as to the accounts of the Orford Church not being presented at chat meeting, in the course of which the Chairman said the accounts were made up at the end of June, but it was intended to makethem up on Easter Sunday,and he hoped they would be presented at the next vestry meeting The Chairman moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Royson for the able and courteous way he had discharged the duty first of all of people's warden for many years, and then as vicar's warden, in all for about 25 years.—Mr. W. Floughton seconded.—Mr. Hodginson said Mr. Roy- ston's resignation came as a surprise to him, and he was very sorry to hear of it. He was very reluctant to undertake a duty previously performed by one who had held such office for 25 years.—Mr. James Houghton moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Royston for his services as allotment warden.—Mr. W. Houghton seconded.—On the votes of thanks being put to the meeting, they were carried with applause.—The meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the Chairman. ST. THOMAS', STOCKTON HEATH. The annual vestry meeting in connection with St. Thomas', Stockton Heath, was held on Tuesday at 12 o'clock. The Rev. T. S. Tofts (vicar) presided, and there were also present—The Rev. R. C. Vernon (curate), Messrs, Robert Davies and W. S. Woods, churchwardens, John Crosfield, Seward, Welsby, Hall, Phillipson, Hough- ton, Spencer, Timperley, Hallows, and Ashton Hayes. Mr. Woods presented the accounts for the past yeer, which showed the receipts to be, general offertory, £lB2 10s. id; special offertories, £3l 10s. 4d.; balance on churchyard fund £1 us. 6d.; °alone° on bazaar fund, £1 Os. 4d. The expenditure amounted to £220, and there was a balance due to the churchwardens of £3 7s. 9d. The Vicar nominated Mr. W. S. Woods as his warden, and Mr. Logan Wright was appointed people's warden. The sidesmen appointed were : Messrs. R. Davies, Tinsley, Francomb, Vere Davies, and A. Down. Messrs. Tinsley and Woods were appointed lay repre- sentatives to the ruri-dea canal and diocesan oonferences.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOLMASTER AS AN UNPAID ORGANIST. Mr. Welsby said he did not see any reference to the salary of the organist in the accounts. Was he not en- titled to some remuneration ?—The Vicar said he paid nothing to the organist. He occupied the position under an arrangement made at the time he was appointed schoolmaster and during the time he (the Vicar) had been in the parish he had never known there to be a sepa- rate organist. The organistand schoolmaster had always been the same gentleman, and he held one or two offices besides, being also choirmaster. The arrangement pro- bably came about through it being considered advisable that the schoolmaster should be the organist, because it would enable them to have a better man for both posts. If he rave up'the post of organist he would have to give up that of schoolmaster also.—Mr. Welsby.—Are they both connected ?—The Vicar :—When a schoolmaster mates application for the Post he knows that he will be required to be the orgsnist as well.—Mr. Welsby said the former schoolmaster received something additional for the work.—The Vicar :—lf the parish like to vote him something it is all well and good.—Mr. Robert Davies remarked that a house had been bought.and given to Mr. James, and he agreed to play the organ,but nothing bad been said about payment.—The Rev. R. C. Vernon asked Mr. Welsby if Mr. James had instructed him to say what he had ?—Mr. Welsby :—I am not here to be cross-questioned, but to make a statement.—Mr.'Fim- perley said he thought Mr. James was perfectly satisfied with his position, and had not heard him make any complaint.—After some more discussion the subject dropped, and the accounts were passed, on the motion of Mr. Houghton, seconded by Mr. Phillipson. THE QUESTION OF PEW RENTS. Mr. Crosfield said he had a favour to ask. He found it very inconvenient when coming into church to have to walk about looking for a sear. Mr. Vernon was at- tracting such large congregations that presently they would have to stand up in the aisle. He should like to pay rent for a pew of his own, if the churchwardens would sanction it.—Mr. Davies said he never was in favour of the church being made free-seated. According ,to the trust deed, rents should be paid for certain pews,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>11-1 E EXAMINER, S A TURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</head>
        <p>11-1 E EXAMINER, S A TURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</p>
        <p>and these rents should form part of the income of the clergyman. He spoke to the Bishop of Chester about it las year, and he (the BiAop) suggested that some arrangement should be come to whereby the eeats should not exactly he reserved, but bespoken. He (Mr. Davies) rr.plied that in a little parish like that such an ‘ rrange- ment was not feasible.—The Vicar suggested that Mr. Crosfield might solve the difficulty by a leing to the church.—Mr. Robert Davies said lie had always wished to se an addition made to the building. At a small expense an aisle could be made at the north side.—The Rev. R. C. Vernon said all in the sight of God were equal and attending church was not like going, to a concert. They came to worship and every man had a right in the Parish Church, while those who came first got the best seats if they liked, and those who came late got the worst. He did not think they ought to buy and sell in God's house, for that was what it would amount to. He had had to preach in a church when all the seats in the front were empty, while those at the back were full. It was not pleasant to think that the people at the back could come so far and no further, whilst others could come to the front. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Crosfield observed that at St. Margaret's Church, Westminster, no one was allowed to enter until the organ began to play, and he t houf7ht some arrangement of that sort would do very well at Stockton Heath. As regards buying and selling they might use the same objection in regard to the offer. tory.—The Rev. R. C. Vernon : That is a gift. Contin- uing,he said that if they rescinded the resolution making the church "free and open" they would be going back to years long ago, and to a state of things which were really unnecessary, when sittings were sold and those who had the most money .got the best seats, just as was the case with the Jews in the Synagogue in the days of our Lord. They wanted every man to feel that he had a right to go into his parish church and sit where he liked, provided he came in tiwie—those who came earli- est got the best seats.—Mr. Henghton : I quite agree with you.—The Vicar pr.inted out that the matter under discussion was a very difficult one to decide, but if some one came forward with a proposal that would give satis- faction to all parties no doubt it would be adopted.—The discussion then dropped, and the meeting concluded with votes of thanks being passed to the officers for their services during the past year. ST. BARNABAS CHURCH. . .</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting of the above church was held on Tuesday morning in the church vestry. The Vicar (Rev. G. Allton) presided, and there was a good number present,though the attendance were not so large as at some previous meetings. Among those present were M9ssrs. W. H. J. Jenkins (vestry clerk), R. T. Smith, R. Y. Woollacott, W. Throssell, R. Proud,.W. Proud, Benjamin, and H. Jackson. • THE POSTING OF THE NOTICE. Before the business of the !meeting commenced, Mr. Jenkins said that he had been asked outside whether it was legal, not to put up the notice of convening meet- ing upon the church door,and perhaps that question had better lee dealt with at once, and it would be as well for the question to be put to the Vicar. Mr. Benjamin hereupon put the question.—The Vicar : The notice has been put out.—Mr. Benjamin : I did not know that, I have been looking for it, but could not see it.—The Vicar : It was placed opposite the west door. Whon it was placed on the front door, it was torn off.—The Clerk said that he was afraid that Mr. Ben- jamin did not look sufficiently for it. The Vicar haftl done his duty, and he was sure that the question was merely a device to create mischief, and it was a petti- fogging, question.—Mr. Benjamin objected to such lan- guage going unchallenged. He did not wish to create mischief. He had come to exercise his right as a pari- shioner—Mr. Jenkins said that he would advise the Vicar not to take the slightest notice of such a capric- ious objection, as this way of creating mischief was con- temptible.—Mr. Benjamin : It is remarkable that the notices are placed outside the door everywhere except at St. Barnabas—Mr. W. Proud: And the notice never was torn off the door, except when it was taken down to read at the vestry meeting.—The Vicar was about to replr to the various criticisms but was disua.ded by Mr. Jenk,ns who told him that he would act more wisely by not saying anything at all. ELECTION OF OFFICERS. . . .</p>
        <p>After the minutes of the previous meeting had been read, the Vicar said that they had spent a very happy year, and he thought a prosperous one. The church- wardens and himself had worked together well, and the workers generally had done their duty well. In fact they could not have desired a better year than the past one. This year, he had to change his warden, and he begfred to nominate Mr. John Looker as his church war- den for the ensuing year. That gentleman had also desired him to nominate Mr. Trevet as his sidesman.— Mr. Griffiths proposed Mr. R. T. Smith as people's war- den. He said that as he had worked with Mr. Smith during the past year, he could say that he had acted very honourably and straightforwardly. He (Mr. Griffiths) could safely say that he could never wish for a better ca-warden. He thought at the commencement of the year, that Mr. Smith was scarcely everything that he could desire, but he was happy to say that whatever Mr. Smith might have been in previous years, his con- nection with him had been a very pleasant one. (Hear. hear.) In view of this, and seeing that Mr. Smith had sufficient moral courage to stand up with the people of the parish, and seeing that the vicar of the parish stood in so independent a position in the existing state of the la Tv, it was desirable to have a man who would, when necessary, oppose any unreasonable demands of the vicar.—Mr. Benjamin in seconding the motion said that in face of the encomiums past upon Mr. Smith by the Vicar's warden of last year, it was plain that he must be the right man in the right place.—Mr. Smith was elected unanimously, and the following sidesmen were also chosen :—Messrs. William Fairclough, Princep, and J. Edwardson.</p>
        <p>LAY REPRESENTATIVES. The Vicar said that the next question was the election of lay representatives to the diocesan and ruri-deaconal conferences. Mr. Smith informed him that the ss. which had to be paid every par had ben called fnr,and vet'they had no lay representative.—Mr. Griffiths : " I ' beg your pardon -I was at he conference last vear."— The Vicar said that he was glad to 1 ear that, but he would like some one to a with him to the conference. Mr. Griffiths name was suggested, and that gentleman Promised to go provided his expenses were paid. The Vicar promised to pay them, and Mr. Griffiths was then elected on the propesit,on ot the Vicar, seconded by Mr. Smith.</p>
        <p>was decided that the Wardens should be the lay repre- fientatives to the ruri-deaconal and diocesan conferences. The accounts of the D.-an Trust Charity Fund were laid on the tab'e for Pxaminati,m. A vote of thanks to the Vicar for presidia brrm:zlit thp meting to a dose.</p>
        <p>enlargement of the church. To meet this expenditure, we have not received the general support of the con- gregation, notwithstanding our appeal of last year, and we have had to fall back on a few supporters of the ehurch, who, as usual, have responded to the appeal. For this, we as church officers, beg to tender those friends our thanks. We certainly think unles the general congregation support the wardens more heartily, the time is coming, when there may be a deficiency. Possibly the mode of collection may have something to do with this, but this is a matter for your consideration. We beg to cominend to your thought, and that of our successors the question of the seating of the congrega- tion. It has long been a source of complaint to us that Ninnies regularly attending service have been unable without great trouble to get suitable accommodation, and while not suggesting that the principle of free and open seats should be interfered with, we think that such families might be better accommodated. We have tried to do it, but were met with an initial difficulty. Perhaps our successors will have more success. We are pleased that on the termination of our office, the church is in such good condition, and that our fund is free from debt."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TUE ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>TUE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said that he would like to submit the accounts but would like two auditors elected to ,go through them previous to their being printed.—Messrs. Throssell and Benjamin were elected as auditors unani- mously, and Mr. Smith then handed the accounts to the Vicar who said that it would not be necessary to audit them there as they were to be printed, but he might say that the income of the parish for the past year had been i.:57 103. 11d. and expenditure £57 03. Bd. leaving a balance in hand of 93. 5.11 The Vicar said that he was glad to see how the congregation had rallied round the church during the past year, although it had been such a had year in consequence of the coal strike. He thought they ought all to be thankful. In conclusion he would propose the re-election of Mr. Jenkins as vestry clerk.— The motion was unanimously carried. ST. BARNABAs A PATTERN. TO OTHER PARISHES! Mr. H. Isherwood proposed a vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding that morning. He said it was gra- tifying to have had such a pleasant meetihg. St. Barnabas Church had been notorious for some years past,but they had promised to become a pattern to other parishes. The Vicar had been amenable to reason, and Ile believed he would do all he could, consistent o the dignity of his position. Perhaps next year, he ivould even put up the notice at the outside of the church door. (The Vicar: "Outside or inside whichever you wish.") The majority thought it ought to be outside the door. He had great pleasure in proposing the motion.—Mr. Throssell seconded the motion which was carried unani- mously.—The Vicar, in reply, said that he was very thankful for the friendly feeling which had prevailed at the meeting. He was always willing to do anything to meet the wishes of the parishioners, and was always willing to meet anybody. With regard to the terrible notice which Mr. Benjamin mentioned, he would see that it was put on the church door nest year and would send one specially to Mr. Benjamin.—The meeting then ter- minated.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTCWN. There was a large muster of parishioners at the vestry meeting in connection with th's church, and the vestry being overcro‘ ded, an adjournment was made to the church, on the suggestion of the vicar (the Rev. T. R. Matthews) who presided. After the formal opening of the minutes of the last vestry were read and con- firmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FINANCES.</head>
        <p>FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. A. Pinsblett, vicar's warden. presented the ac- counts for the past year, which showed the receipts from all sources to have been £195 2.5. IClqd., and the ex- penditure £243 ss. 6,1-d. The sum of £44 had also to be raised for the heating apparatus, making the total liabilities, £92 4s. 10d. Mr. Pimblett explained that the deficit was in some measure due to a decrease in the offertories, which were less than the preceding year, there was also a falling off in the special collections. The accounts were adopted on the motiqn of Mr. G. Jackson. ELECTION OF WARDEN AND SIDE6MEN. The 'near appointed Mr. T. A. Pimblett, as his war- den for the ensuing year.—Mr. Andrews moved, and Mr. Winstanley seconded the re-appointment of Mr. P. Foden, as parishioners warden.—Mr. Cookson proposed and Mr. Harrison seconded Mr. Lawless as people's war- den.—Mr. G. Ratcliffe said they had no fault to find with Mr. Foden, and he could not see why the existing order of things should be changed.—Mr. J. Jones said they had nothing against Mr. Lawless, but he ought first to have served as a sidesman.—Mr. Winstanley was of opinion that Mr. Foden had been in office too long. He had been People's warden 12 years, and he thought it was not the custom for one to stay more than three years.—On a vote being taken, 36 voted for Mr. Foden and 10 for Mr. Lawless, Mr. Foden was therefore reappointed.—The Vicar appointed Messrs. J. Jones, J. Cuncliffe and T. Latimer as his sidesmen, and Messrs. Andrews, Wigall, and Anderton were appointed peop'e's sidesmen. VOLUNTARY RATE, &amp;C. A voluntary church rate of 2d. in the £ was laid. It</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARGRAVE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry in.&gt;rtin,..l.)f the N:wtAn-in-Maker- field Parish Chur,Th, Wargrave, was held on Tuesday evening.—The Rev. J. Carson, rector, presided and amongst those present were Me!ssrs. C. B. F. Borron, A. Purnell, J. Wright, J. Crampton, H. Sweetlove, J. Moss, Szc. After the minutes of the last vestry meeting had been read and confirmed, Mr. Borron presented the statement of accounts for the past year. which showed !be receipts, including a balance of £lO Ss.. to be £235 9s. 11d., and the expenditure £275 03. 6d., leaving a balance of £lO 94. sd. The debt incurred by the altera- tions to the organ had been cleared off during the year, and £l3 43. 2d. had been realized by the voluntary church rate.—On the motion of Mr. J. Wright, seconded by Mr. J. Moss the accounts were adopted. ELECTION OF WARDENS, &amp;c. . . .</p>
        <p>The Rector said he had pleasure in nominating Mr. Borron as his warden for the ensuing year.—Mr. J. G. C. Starkie was appointed parishioner's warden, on the motion of Mr. H. Sweetlove.—ln the election of sidesmen the Rector observPd that it was unprecedented that the church should lose two sidesmen by death, during the year, for both of whom they all had a high regard. The following were then appointed sidesmen ; Messrs. T. Molyneux, H. Sweetlove, A. Purnell, T. Haselden, W. Thompson, jun., T. Cave, J. Moss, James Wright, W. Norman, J. B. Porter, G. Bailey and J. White. ORGANIST'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Borron moved that the salaried officers of the church be re-appointed for the year, subject to the minute passed in 1884. He thought the organist (Mr. F. Leyland) had great ability, and the attention that he paid to his duties as organist and choirmaster deserved some recompense at their hands by an increase in his salary for the year. He devoted more than the usual time to his duties beyond what was required of him, and they ought to see that he received some recognition. (Hear, hear.)—He proposed that his salary be increased by £s.—The proposition was seconded by Mr. James Wright, and unanimously carried.—lt was decided that the gratuity annually given to the choir should be £l5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DIOCESAN CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>DIOCESAN CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Messrs W.Thompson and T.Molyneux were appointed [ay representatives to the diocesan conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>VOTE OF CONDOLENCE</head>
        <p>VOTE OF CONDOLENCE</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Moss said they should he lacking in their duty as parishioners and churchmen if they did not move a vote of condolence with the widows of Mr. A. Wright and Mr.H.Bradshaw. These gentlemen had during their lifetime rendered good service to the church, and he moved that a vote of condolence be sent to the widows of the deceased gentlemen named.—Mr.Borron seconded, and assured them that he felt very much grieved at the loss sustained. During the time Mr. Wright was with them, he constantly devoted himself to the duties of the church. Mr. Bradshaw had been with them a considerable time, he believed from his boyhood,and his beterest and heart was in Wargrave Church. He was certain they would all feel the loss they had sustained. The motion was heartily acquiesced in.</p>
        <p>GENERAL BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>The statement of the Dean School Charity Trust Fund was presented,the financial statement showing a balance of about £44. The Rector mentioned that a small por- tion of the churchwarden's receipts was derived from certain burial fees, which he was quite content to waive his claim to, as did his venerable predecessor. A short discussion ensued as to the advisability of obtaining a supply of water from the town main, and on the motion of Mr. J. Wright, the rector, wardens and sidesmen were instructed by the vestry to ascertain the cost of laying down pipes for supplying water for the use of the church. The motion was carried. Votes of thanks to the retiring wardens and the Rector for presiding were accorded, and the meeting closed. ST. JAMES', HAYDOCK. The annual vestry meeting in connection with this church was held in the schoolroom, Haydock on Thurs- day evening. Rev. C. E. Bowden, vicar, presided, and amongst those present were Messrs. A. H. L. Evans, E. Jackson,R. Bradshaw, J. Matthews J. Walden J. Tur- ton &amp;c. The vicar proposed a vote of thanks to the wardens and sidesmen for their services during the year which was heartily accmrded.</p>
        <p>A QUESTION OF OFFERTORIES. The Vicar nominated Mr. A. H. L. Evans as his war- den. Mr. Bradshaw proposed the re-election of Mr. E. Jackson as parishioners warden. Mr. Jackson said before accepting the position again, he should like to mention a point concerning the offertories.—Hitherto it had been the duty of the churctwardens to take charge of the whole of the collections. 'He was sorry to say an exception had been made. There was a coqction made at St. Alban's chapel last Sunday which had not beerk handed over to the churchwardens, and for that reason 7 ho had not been able to complete the accounts, as he did not consider they would be correct if that collection was omitted. If they (the wardens) allowed a thing of that sort to go without calling attention to it, and getting the reasons, they would not know what collections would !..e taken at some future time. The Vicar said he could not help thinking there must be some little misunderstanding. On Good Friday he asked, Mr. Andrews (the curate) in the morning whether the congregation at St. Albans would like to give their contributions for the same pur- pose as at the Parish Church, viz, the Jerusalem fund. Mr. Andrews said it had been suggested to the people that during Lent they should put by a little money to be given towards the rebuilding of a church at Shrews- bury which had been destroyed by fire. These little contributions were put into bags, and were placed in the alms dish on Sunday, in order that they might he offered, but they were not apart of the offertory for the day, and he supposed those who put in the bags would give their ordinary contributions also, which would be handed over to Mr. Jackson. Mr. Jackson said not a penny had been handed to him for Easter Sunday. After some further discussion the matter dropped. The Vicar nominated as his sidesmen, Messrs. J. Cawley, P. Cunliffe, E. Leyland, and W. Holland. The follow- ing were appointed parishioners sidesmen, Messrs. Whit- field, Derbyshire, Matthews, and Walden. The two churchwardens, Messrs. A. Evans and E. Jackson were appointed lay representatives to the Diocesan and ruri- Deaconal Conference. Mr. Bradshaw moved and Mr. Jos. Heaton seconded, that each warden and sidesmen be allowed 23.6 d. towards their expenses in attending the visitation change.--Carried. The meeting was then adjourned until 26th April to receive the balance sheet.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S, NEWTON. The annual Easter vestry in connection with the parish of St. Peter's, Newton, was held in the Town Hall Newton, on Tuesday evening: The Rev. H. Monk pre- sided, and there were Present, Rev. F. W. Johnstone,Dr. Watkins. Dr. H. Watkins, Messrs. R. :Stuart. W. H. Flindley, J. Smith, W. Hindlev, Smith, G. Clarke, J. Liptrott, &amp;c. Mr. W. H. Hindlev read the balance sheet which showed thTe receipts to be £133 17s. 2(1., including a balance of £29 3s. 9d., and expenditure £132 16s. Bcl., leaving a balance of £1 Oa. 61.—Mr. Stuart read the report of the year as follows :—To the parishioners of S. Peter's. We beg to present our report and balance sheet for the year ending Easter 1894. From the balance sheet it will be seen that we have a very small sum to hand to our successors, but this arises from our endea- vour to promote the comfort of the congregation, as well as to carry out the necessary repairs, cleaning, &amp;c. The expenditure this year has been very heavy for repairs, as will be seen by reference to the accounts in addition to the resulting increase of expenditure, through the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The Vicar nominated Mr. James Liptrott as his warden for the ensuirig year. Mr. G. Clarke proposed, and Mr. J. Smith seconded the election of Mr. J. Bateman as parishioners' warden. The Vicar nominated the following as his sidesmen : Messrs. Speakman. Matthews, Moscrop, and Dr. H. Watkins. Mr. Stuart proposed and Mr. Brierley seconded the following as parishioners' sidesmen : Messrs. P. Percival, Mayor. Ritchie, and S. Davies. The wardens were appointed lay representatives to the ruri- deaconal conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said as they had spent about £4,000 in en- larging and beautifying the church he should like to ask as to the insurance of the church if a fire took place. Mr. Hindley said it was proposed to insure the church for £3,000, including the old insurance.—Dr. Watkins thought in the opinion of the chancellor it would not be sufficient, and he should propose that it be increased.— Mr.Hindley thought it was quite enough, they could not burn the stones.—Mr. Brierley said it must be under- stood that unless the church was insured for the full amount, they were dividing the risk of the Insurance company.—Mr. Stuart supported Mr. Hindley's views that there was nothing in the chancel itself, practically speaking that could possibly get on fire, because there was no heating apparatus behind the oak panelling. It was not a very large question, perhaps a question that a few shillings a year would settle, but he did not like Mr. Hindley's suggestion that the stones would not burn ; perhaps it would do them good, if they did get burnt a little. (Laughter.)—Mr. Hindley : There is one stone that some folks would. like to see burnt.—Mr. Stuart said they had done what they thought was sufficient, and if their successors thought differently it would be for the vestry to decide.—Mr. Liptrott was quite of Mr. Brierley's opinion, and thought the insurance should be increased.—Dr. H. Watkins, asked what the extra cost would be.—Mr. Stuart said about 2s. 6d. per £loo. Captain Royse thought the insurance should be for the full amount, and for the sake of about 305., he did not think it wag-worth while to run the risk, and he would prop's@ that it be increased to £s,ooo.—Mr. Stuart seconded, and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FREE AND UNAPPROPRIATED SEATS</head>
        <p>FREE AND UNAPPROPRIATED SEATS</p>
        <p>3 he Vicar said whilst waiting for the auditors to verify the accounts, he had a little explanation to make. For 29 years he had been in Newton, and about 23 years of that time he had been its vicar. For some portion of that time rumours had reached him which he could not take any notice of, but he thought he could give some information to the vestry, which might be of interest to them, and if they would allow him he should like to make that statement now. Two years before he was made vicar, the church of St. Peter's Newton was free, and he happened to have a copy of Mr. Whitley's letter to the parishioners dated Christmas 1869 in which the seating of the church was declared free and unappro- priated. He virtually accepted the benefice upon the understanding that this was to continue, and he read an extract from his first sermon as vicar designate,preached in 1871 acquiesing in the arrangement. He did not mean to say that regulations made 25 years ago, should like the laws of the Medes and Persians, never be altered. He thought the time had come when it might very ad- vantageously be reconsidered. It was fairly established that the abolition of pew rents should be substituted by voluntary offerings. The wardens fund was always intended to be supplemented by a voluntary church rate. When he came to that benefice his stipend was about £3OO a year, but it had been gradually getting less until now that it was a little over £2OO, and this for the last two years had been returned to the church.—Mr. Stuart asked if it could be taken that Mr.Whitlev's letter should be read in conjunction with his sermon.—The Vicar: They both bear on the same subject.—Mr.Stuart said he should like to suggest to the vestry that Mr. Monk read out from Mr, Whitley's letter, that the consent of the war- der's had to be received to the disposal of certain funds. If that letter was right, arid Mr. Monk was going to abide by it, he (the Vicar) had not done be the people's warden, as suggested, whatever he may have done by the Vicar's warden, and he was extremely sorry that Mr. Monk had raised the question that night, especially when the letter of Mr. Whitley's should be asked to be taken notice of by the vestry, and when that letter had not been acted upon during his term of office, and the same spirit which had brought that to Mr. Monk's keinwledge had operated all through. The tone of Mr. Whitlev's letter, and Mr. Monks' sermon, he could not possibly object to,but what he did object to, was that it had not been carried out. He had mentioned to that vestry before, it had been mentioned to him as the only official representing the parish ; conversations with the vicar had no effect, conversation with the vestry had no effect, and the complaints had been stronger to day, that they had music in the church whichltno one but the Vicar could join in, and it amounted to this,when they came te the "Te Deum," it came to the Vicar and choir to says' We praise thee, 0 God," and the congrega- tion had to listen while they did it. That was what he objected to, and had objected to all along. Alterations had been made in the form of worship in the church,and no consultation took place between the Vicar and his warden. The Vicar might probably tell them that he thoiveht he had a perfect right to do as he chose, and perhaps under certain considerations, he (Mr. Stuart) should not say he had not, but if the Vicar ef the parish want-d to be in perfect touch with the wardens, and throne!) the wardens, the sidesmen, the Vicar had no right to do anything of the kind without consultation with the wardens. When he took the offertory of the churelt ae their officer, lie was accustomed to deliver it to the Vicar or curate at the altar rails, and when he ascertained, without any suggestion or consultation, he was to be met at the foot of the chancel steps, he con- sidered it a deliberate insult to him as a man, and to them as their representative, and because of that he had taken no further part in the service. There was another point he would like to mention. There were men in the parish, into whose houses the Vicar had never been.— The Vicar: I should like to have the names.—Mr. Stuart: I will give them to you privately.—Dr. Watkins : I must protest against these personalities.— Rev. F. W. Johnstone said the question before the vestry was one of funds.—Mr. Stuart: Mr. Johnstone, I don't want to say too much.—Mr. Johnstone: You can say whatever you like.—Mr. Stuart: I don't want to crush a butterfly. The second question and the most import- ant question, notwithstanding Mr. Johnstone's interrup- tion, was the seating of the conieregation. He main- tained 12 months ago in that room, and he maintained</p>
        <p>SUNLIGHT SOAP COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>Prizes of Bicycles, Watches, 232,000 and Books, value £4 I 9904</p>
        <p>First of these Monthly Competitions will be teld on January 31st, 1894, to be followed by others each month during 1894.</p>
        <p>0. of', For tli4 Cotypetiticm the United litrigdo.n will be divided into Trict. Ie Divtriztx tut under :</p>
        <p>Value of Prizes given each month in each dLstrict.</p>
        <p>The Pricer will be awarded every month during 1894, Tot. In each of the 8 Districts, as under Prizu, districts IRELAND. g L d. 4 s. d. 2 SCOTLAND. 3 MIDDLESEX, KENT, and SURREY. 4 NORTHUMBERLAND, o o ')600 o DURHAM, and YORK- too o SHIRE. s CUMBERLAND, WEST- • MORELAND, LANCA- 84 o o 8064 o o SHIRE, and ISLE OP MAN. so o o 4800 0 0 CHESH 'FORDSH 52 to o 5040 o o ' -tSHIRE. ISHIRE 50 0 0 4800 o o RDSHIR—. 30 0 0 4800 0 0 90 0 0 4800 0 0 ••••••• 41904 0</p>
        <p>6 WALES, CHESHIRE, STAFFORDSHIRE, SHROPSHIRE, WOR- CESTERSHIRE, MON- M 0 UTH SHIR E, and HEREFORDSHIRE. 7 N OTT INGHAMSHIRE. DERBYSHIRE, LI N - COLNSHIRE, LE ICES- T E RSH IR E, WAR• WICKSHIRE, RUT. LAN DSH IRE, NOR- FOLK, SUFFOLK, CAM BRI DG ESHIRE, H UNTINGDONSHIRE, NO RT SHIRE,HAMPTON- BEDFORD- SHIRE, and OXFORD- SHIRE. $ ESSEX. HERTF 0 R D- SHIRE, BUCK IN G- H A MSH IRE, BERK- SHIRE, SUSSE, HAMPSHIRE, WILT- SHIRE, TERSHIRE, SOMER- SETSHIRE, DORSET- SHIRE, DEVONSHIRE, CORNWALL ISLE OF WIGHT, and CHANNEL ISLANDS.</p>
        <p>now, that it was the necessary function of the church- wardens to prop(rly seat such families as came together in the churth. He gathered fr )in what Mr. Monk had said, that lie was prepared to agree to a committee of responsible m.n in the narish joining with him to see what could he done in the matter. He was now seeak- ing in all good faith, with the sincerest desire for peace and friendliness all round. During the four years he had been warden he had come in contact with most of the people in the parish, and lie could assure Mr. Monk that it only needed from him a little consideration for those peoples feeliAgs, to have the greatest harmony, and the happiest parish in Lancashire. (Applause.)—Mr. J. Smith quite agreed with Mr. Stuart in regard to the music, and to the appropriation of the seats.—The Vicar said the music was simpler now than it had been for 12 years past.—Mr. Brierley moved the adoption of the accounts, Mr. Clarke seconded, and it was carried.—Mr. Brierley moved a vote of thanks to the wardens, sides- men and building Committee, which Captain Royse seconded and it was heartily accorded.—Mr. W. H. Hinciley thanked them for the vote, and alluded to the most insulting manner in which one member of the con- gregation had treated him for two months past, but which he put down to his ignorance. He could prove what lie had said.—Mr. Stuart also returned thanks for the vote. He did not know anyone who could say a pleasant thing in a nastier way, or a nasty thing in a pleasanter way than Dr. Watkins. He must remind him that the snows were whitening his head, that times were changed, and that they were not going to be satisfied with what took place during the time he was churchwarden. They were going to have things no to date, and they would do their best to have ' them. He concluded by moving a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which Mr. J. Smith seconded, and the meet- ing terminated.</p>
        <p>~e....,,,,,., au tag Er.. i: 41,(141, i 1,1,-::::1 "..--St tfkrirl,ify .......?.::%::' .z.. ;•::,it.:.:-. ....If-4.; e---;1221i ..el,---,t. 1:-.I.-.::</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RUNIORN PARISH CHURCH. ENCOURAGING STATEMENTS.</head>
        <p>RUNIORN PARISH CHURCH. ENCOURAGING STATEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn Parish Church vestry was held on ruesday evening in the National Schools, Church-street, Runcorn, the Rev. Canon Mait- land Wood (vicar) presiding. There was a large atten- dance of parishioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman intimated that he would not detain them with a long speech before proceeding with the ordinary business, as he might find it necessary to speak on any special point that might arise in the course of the proceedings. It gave him much pleasure to look back upon the work that had been done dminfz the past year. It was due to the officers that he should say this as they had worked together cordially and harmoni- ously. He had found a great many helpers during his experience, and last year was no exception. He had every r-ason to think that as things had been managed so aarnirahly in the past, the present order of things should not he clisturbed,and accordingly had great plea- sure in nominating Mr. J. E. Meredith as his own warden for the ensuing year. (Applause.)—On the pro- position of Mr. Key, seconded by Mr. Bate, Mr. John Littler was unanimously re-appointed parishioners' war- den for the coming year.—(Flear, hear.)—Mr. Meredith appointed as his sidesmen Messrs. Walter Timmins. Edwin Gregory, Richard Abel, and Waiter Jones.—The remaining: sidesmen elected were Messrs. W. Crosby, Rogerson, Digges, and Hewitt.</p>
        <p>ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said they would all regret his inability to again appoint Mr. Speakman as his warden at this church, that estimable gentleman having passed away from them. The loss of Mr. Speakman was very great, because during his term of life, and more especially in the course of his connection with the wardenship of St. Michael's, he had always done his utmost in the inter- ests of tho church. He (the Vicar) had considered it his duty to ask Mr. Weston to take the vacant position for the interval, and as that gentleman had performed the work so sarkfactorily he had pleasure in nominating him as his warden for the year 1891-5. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Mellor was elected as people's warden. and Messrs. She- rlock, Speakman, and E. Harrison were chosen by Mr. Weston as his sidesmen.—The parishioners' sidesmen were appoin,ed as follows :—Messrs. Cropper, Blundell, and Lightburn. ST. JOHN'S CTIURCIT, WESTON.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that during the past year Messrs. T. T. Sill and Aaron had served as wardens of the above church. The first was anxious to be relieved of that office, and therefore could not be nominated. He was indebted to Mr. Aaron for his past services and was pleased to nominate Mr. James Ford, junior, as his warden for next year. (Hear, hear.) On the motion of Mr. Meredith seconded by Mr. Haidock, Mr. Robirmon was elected people's garden at St. John's Church—The sidesmen anointed were Messrs. John Mather, Mitchin- son, T. T. Sill and Farrell.</p>
        <p>LAY REPRESPNTATITE:g.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen were elected lay representa- tives :—Parish Church : ' Messrs. John Lightburn and Timmins ; St. Michael's Church ; Messrs. Mellor and Alfred Lightburn ; St. John's Church, Weston : Messrs. T. T. Sill and Robinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>STATEMENT OF CUARITIES:</head>
        <p>STATEMENT OF CUARITIES:</p>
        <p>The Vicar said that the various charities were almost identical with those of last year. The Itiehv Charity showed the total amount of receipts to be £l3 10s. This amount was expended on underclothing for the poor.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>The statemsnt of the accounts of tha three churche4 was submitted and adopted on the motion of Mr. J. E. Meredith. The Chairman in referring to the statement of accounts said that the figures were an azreeable sur- prise to him. He had not anticipated such a return, but everything had been so well managed that the result was,the Parish Church had done more towards support- ing the staff than ever before. With regard to St. Michael's Church he thought a little more had been col- lected and a little more spent. Next year he hoped to see a better return. The year had been a very short one there only having been fifty-one weeks altogether, so that on the whole they had every reason to congratulate themselves upon the results of their efforts in the past year. It was very encouraging to find that St. John's Church at Weston was doing so well. The parishioners here had shown a hearty spirit in the welfare of their church, and the result was very gratifying indeed. He should like to take this opportunity of asking them to try and help Weston as much as they possibly could.— Mr. King hoped that the congregation would to do their share towards seeing the choristers properly robed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was accorded Messrs. Imison and Lighthurn for auditing the accounts.—Canon Wood in proposing a vote of thanks to choirs and their officers, said he thought it was hardly necessary for him to say more of the work of the Parish choir. They were all very much indebted to them for their services. He generally found that when strangers visited their ser- vices they spoke well of the choir, the organist, and the conductor.—Mr. Diggs seconded :he proposition, and in doing so said there was not the least doubt that the choirs deserved their most sincere thanks. The vote was very warmly aecorded.—A similar compli- ment was paid to the ladies who had decorated the churches with flowers on the various occasion, and also those who had given flowers or helped the workers in anyway .—Mr. James Littler, in returning thanks, said he would always endeavour to keep the choir up to the highest standard of efficiency. He would, however,like to say, that the ladies, who were always willing to help the church in any way might find an nutlet for their surplus energy in the shape of improving the state of the choristers' surplices which were at preseat hardly presentable. Mr. Meredith proposed that the best thanks of the meeting he given to the Vicar for presid- ing.—Mr. Imison seconded, and the Vicar in returning thanks said it was the seventh year in which they had met to transact the business of the vestry under the present arrangement. Sometimes he used to go to the meetings with a very anxious heart, but he always went away very happy, until now he had become to like the meeting. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that they would never have any disagreeable proceedings, hut if any difficulties should arise that they would look them in the face and overcome them as best they could. (Ap- plause.) FRODSHAM VESTRY MEETING. LIVELY PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Parish Church Vestry took place on Easter Monday morning in the chapel. the Rev. H.B. Blogg (vicar) presiding. The remaining gentlemen present were Messrs. C. Reynolds. 1.P.,A. Thomas, J.P., C. Linaker (church warden). T.D. Timmins. T. Riley, R. Bate, J. M. Williams. H. E. Heywood. F. W.Dunsford, J. Ashton, Charles H. Flibbert, John Nicksnn, J. Nicholls, H. Tiley. John Austen, Thomas Davies. W. E. Shore, S. Davies, Milner, Turner, R. Foulkes, G. Aston, H. G. Linaker, J. B. Holland, T. Lewis, F. W. Spencer, H. Hutchinson, J. Gorst, G. Maddock, H. Davies, John Pollard, W. Noden, S. Sharpe, Richardson, W. Gaskell. and Occleston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MR. FOULKES PROTESTS.</head>
        <p>MR. FOULKES PROTESTS.</p>
        <p>At the outset Mr. Foulkes protested against a meeting such as that being held in the church and thought that the school was the proper place for the meeting to be held. THE YEAR'S FINANCF.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Linaker presented the churchwarden's accounts showing that at the commencement of the year there was a b 'lance of £1 18s. 11d. The Parish Church nffertoriesamounted to £2lB 13s. : sundry receipts. £9 25.; Iron Church offertories, £39 7s. 5d.: Harrison's charities, £3 12s. 11d. ; total, £272 14s. 3d. The ex- penditure was : Parish Church salaries, sexton, £4O ; orcran blower, £4 ; alms paid to vicar £2l ; insurance, E 7 03.9 d. ; sundries. £l5 13s. 4d. ; Iron Church ex- penses, £29 2s. ; charities, special church collec- tions, £73 3s. 6d. ; Harrison's charity distributed, £2 18s. ; maintenance of divine service, washing sur- plices. £4 is. 9d. ; ringers and ponsioners, £2B ; heat- ing. £8 14s. sd. lighting, £l7 10.. id. ; choir, £lO 35. 7d.; total expenditure, £261 7s. ; leaving a balance cf £ll 6s. B?,,ti.</p>
        <p>MR. FOULKES BEGINS TO CRITICISE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Foulkes remarked that the balance iheet showed that £l7 10s. id. had been spent in gas. They had ser- vices in the winter months before 8 a.m. and others in the evening. The congregation seldom consisted of one in addition to the officials. The holding of these services only resulted in the parishioners' money being wasted. There was a reduction of -422 on the collections compared to those on previous occasions.—Mr. Linaker explained that the gas account was for five quarters, the March quarter being included for the special benefit of Mr. Foulkes. (Laughter.)—The accounts were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>Arßq. So A. ALLEN''</p>
        <p>WORLD S</p>
        <p>HAIR RESTORER.</p>
        <p>An absolutely perfect Hair Restorer and Dressing, justly celebrated for it; wc-Ind-rful life-giving properties to feded or falling hair,</p>
        <p>Restores the youthful colour.</p>
        <p>CONSUMPTION,</p>
        <p>BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>I will demonstrate to the whole world, and tAo sufferers from these unfortunate maladies, how they can be perman- ently cured, by an entirely New Method of Treatment, with- out the chance of failure. Write with full confidence to Mr. WILLIAMS, 11, Thirwood PlAce, Norfolk Crescent, London. He will send you full particulars free of all charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The Frodsham and Stockham Charity, the Harrison account, and the day school and organ trustees accounts were all submitted and also those of the Grammar bchool and the Upton Charity.—Mr. Fou!kes thought that the Bottom school accounts should be presented in order that the resolution on the vestry's books might be carried out.—Mr. Reynolds did not suppose that there was any objection to their being produced if desired. —The Chairman said there were circumstances why the reports were not produced. Up to last year they had been presented.—Mr. Reynolds said that if the matter was nalv,d the accounts would probably be pre- sented twelve months hence.—Mr. Nickson promised to effect the presenting of the accounts next year if possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF CHURCIIWASDENS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF CHURCIIWASDENS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that he was satisfied to know that he had secured the services of Mr. Linaker for another year as his warden. He was deeply indebted to I him for the many servius of value which he had during , the past year rendered to the parish.—Mr. Ashton pro- posed that the vestry's hearty thanks be accorded to Dr. Robinson for his past services and that they tender him , their deep sympathy in his illness. (Hear, hear.) He wished further to propose Mr. T. D. Timmins as church- warden.—Mr. Thomas seconded.—Mr. Rigby regretted that he had to move an amendment which was to the effect that Mr. Ralph Bate, of Netherton, should occupy the vacant position of churchwarden. He was a native of the district and would prove himself a representative man.—Mr. Lewis seconded and the amendment was carried by 16 votes to 14.</p>
        <p>MR. FOULKES ANXIOUS TO BECOME A MENTOR. At this stage Mr. Foulkes remarked that he possessed a book of instructions which would certainly prove of much advantage to the churchwardens. The Vicar would also be personally bound by the instructions.—Mr. Linaker : Have you a motion to propose ?—Mr.Foulkes : It is for the Chairman to rule this meeting.—The Chair- man intimated to Mr. Foulkes that if he had no motion to put forward he was out of order.—Mr. Foulkes said he would tell them their duties—(cries of " order they were to see that the book of homilies was</p>
        <p>inside the church and that the Command- ments were in the proper place.—llle Chairman I ruled Mr. Foulkes out of order and the latter thereupon remarked that he had a motion to bring forward.—The Chairman's ruling was, therefore, upheld by the meet- ing, and the motion of Mr. Foulkes was consequently not heard.—Mr. Timmins moved and Mr. Reynolds seconded that Mr. Bate be parishioners' warden.—This proposition was unanimously carried.—Mr. Foulkes again rose but was again objected to by Mr. Linaker who was told by Mr. Foulkes that the chairman was in authority there. ge rtain parties came there who did not like to hear the truth. The churchwardens before they accepted office should know what their duties were. —Mr. Ashton : Perhaps Mr. Foulkes bad better take the wardens out and lecture them. (Laug,hter.)—Mr. Foulkes said he simply wished to speak the truth and not to lec- ture to the wardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ELECTION OF SIDEsSIF.N</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF SIDEsSIF.N</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker chrse as his sidesrnen" Mes:rs. Heywood, Gleave and Spencer, and the meeting elected as parishioners' sidesmen Messrs. Joseph Gorst, George Ashton, and T. D. Timmins.</p>
        <p>LAY REPRESENT ITIVES. Messrs. John Nickson and T. D. Timmins were ap- pointed lay representatives for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen wer, re-elected auditors : Messrs. Sant, Daviea, and John Pollard. A vote of thanks was also passed to these gentlemen for their ser- vices during the previous year.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF THE PARISH CHURCH ORGANIST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker produced a letter from Mr. Riley, in which it was announced that he (Mr. Riley) intended to retire from the position of organist on the 29.11 Sept. next. Continuing, Mr. Linaker remarked, that Mr. Riley had been their organist for nearly 33 years, dur- ing which period he had always performed his duties with punctually, and had taken great pains and care with the church music. Mr. Riley informed him that he found it difficult to get to church on winter evenings. He had grealitpleasure in moving that the vestry ex- presses its regret at Mr. Riley's retirement, and that they wish him every happiness and prosperity in his future life.—Mr. C. Reynolds seconded, and the motion was carried, the Chairman likewise speaking in high terms of praise of Mr. Riley's services to the church.— Mr. Riley suitatly acknowledged the remarks made re- garding him and thanked the meeting accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TLIANICS TO THE SINGERS.</head>
        <p>TLIANICS TO THE SINGERS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Reynolds, seconded by Mr. Nickson, a vote of thanks was accorded to the adult singers at the Parish and Iron Churches.</p>
        <p>MIL FOULKES OB.TECTS TO FLOWERS IN THE CHURCH,</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood proposed a vote of thanks to the ladies who had decorated their church. Notwithstanding what Mr. Foulkes inieht have to say about the flowers being brought into the sacred edifice, he was sure that those who had admired the beauties of nature and the beautiful things Ciod had provided for them would not object to them coming into a splendid church.—Mr. Gaskell seconded.—Mr. Foulkes proposed that the ladies in future keep the flowers at home. (Laughter.) People living in the country did not require flowers to be brought into a place of worship. It was not in accord- an e with the teachings of the Bible. It tended to sensuous worship, and it was more or less a desecration against which St. Paul had spoken. When the vicar c0.31d prove out of the Bible that it was right then he would agree with them.—Mr. H. G. Linaker remarked that floral decorations were not so desecrating as tak- ing notes in church. (Laughter.) The amendment found no seconder, and consequently the original motion was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>BURIAL FEES.</head>
        <p>BURIAL FEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fatilkes inquire'] what burial fees a person would have to pay sunposing he or she had purchased a grave and then left the pari;h. Did the same rules apply as in Runcorn cemetery where they were doubled for non-resi- dents in the district.—The chairman replied affirma- tively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LITTLE SCENE.</head>
        <p>A LITTLE SCENE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Foulkes referred to a letter which had been pub- lished in a Chester paper since their last vestry meeting with rkference to a pamphlet circulated in the church and taken away by a lady. Having made inquiries he found that the letter WAR written by some anomymoul scribbler who had not the courage No put his name to the article. Mr. Foulkes having read the matter alluded to,the Chairman asked him if he had a resolution to pr- pose to which he replied that he wished to point out the truth of the subject. He had again began to read when Mr. Reynolds rose to a point of order and said that the subject had nothing to do with the present meet- ing.—Mr. Foulkes objected and for some moments there was a scene of confusion which resulted in Mr. Foulkes being again ruled out of order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PASSING BELL.</head>
        <p>THE PASSING BELL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reynolds desired an expression of opinion from the meeting as to the advisability of reducing the time for ringing the passing bell.—Mr. Linaker intimated that the matter was in the hands of the Vicar who would probably have no objection to guide them in their action. —lt was at length decided that the time should be re- duced to half-an-hour. There was no change made in the sexton's fees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TEA WS.</head>
        <p>TEA WS.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded to the churchwardens and sidesmen for their past services on the motion of Mr. Riley, seconded by Mr. Ashton.</p>
        <p>LOOKING FORWARD. Mr. Reynolds in moving a vote of thanks to the Chair. man was interrupted by Mr. Foulkes who said that he had a proposition to make with regard to the future meetings being held in the schohlroom in the evening of some day other than Easter Monday.—Mr. Linaker seconded the motion.—Mr. Lewis then moved that the meeting beheld as hitherto on Easter Monday morning. —Mr. Reynolds seconded.—The amendment was, after a little discussion carried. "THE DAWN OF DAY !"</p>
        <p>Mr. Foulkes produced a magazine entitled the" Dawn of Day," which he stated was the most Romanish maga- zine he had ever seen.—Mr. Linaker : Are you going to move a resolution ?—Mr. Foulkes: Let the Chairman rule the meeting. I have a resolution.—Mr. Reynolds: I move a vote of thanks to the Chairman.—Mr.Foulkes : My resolution is that the magazine be not circulated in the village further than the parish.—Mr. Reynolds : It has nothing to (la with the business of this meeting.— Mr. Foulkes: It has.—The Chairman said this was a , question within his own province, and he had a right to circulate it in the parish.—A vote of thanks to the Chair- man was carried, and the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
