      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>SATPRDAY, MARCH 31, 1594.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>iL.</p>
        <p>Mx. WILLIAM MATHER, M.P., and 11.41"ahiP Experiment. his partners in the Salford Iron- works,hav e done a good stroke for he eight hours day, or, at all events, the forty- 'eight hours week. In view of the strong demands that are being made 'in various directions and *3Pecially amongst skilled workmen for a reduction of the hours of labour, the -owners of the Salford Ir°uworks at the beginning of last year resolved t° Make a twelve months' experiment of the forty- eight hours week. Considering the business interests involved in an establishment employing I'milethinc, like twelve hundred men this was a hold venture.,and it. was not entered upon without 8C471e anxiety being felt as to the result. The . Beherse Was, however, arranged on the most th°rough and businesslike basis by Mr. MATHER 04 the one hand, and the Executive Council of the 4tualgamated Engineers, representing the wt.*- . 'lien, on the other. As the employers had volun- !arily offered to give the forty-eight hour week a .' :`,..air trial, the local branch of the Amalgamated -4ngineers agreed that,should the result be unsat- isfaetory,no obstacles would be thrown in the way of reverting to the old system. The Amalgamated flgineers went further than that,for they decided that during the twelve months of the experiment, t 11037 would not authorise any movement in the district for the shortening of the hours at other fir?rl4% All the conditions seem to have been ‘aithfully and scrupulously carried out on both elides- The experiment commenced on March -.lit last year and terminated on February 28th this , Jear. Mr. MATHER has lost no time in pre- his report to his workmen and the country trierallY• From the point of view of the advo- a--"es of shorter hours of labour the test has been -the°raplete success, and one of its results will be ~.-"e Permanent establishment of forty-eight hours a week's work at Salford ironworks. Mr. ;:atiler reports that the output during the trial 7t has been actually larger than the average of h-e't. a;Le Preceding six years when a week's work con- flsted of fifty-three or fifty-four hours. When, • °lvevert the turnover of last year is corn- , , 'ared with the turnover of those six preceding 1 Years there is a very slight decrease, but that 13 entirely due to the fall of prices, and had prices 1:111ed the same there would have been an increase 1: turnover corresponding with the increase of but Put• Another result of the experiment has theen to show that there is less " lost, time" under ' short hours system. This argument has often been used by the advocates of eight hours and the W. e'lve months test at SalfOrd supports it. The workers are the only class who have at all i ftered from a monetary point of view by the tehhange in the number of hours worked, and what 1 --eY have lost has been infinitesimal. But there see ins to be reason to conclude that even the piece w•erkere Will be no losers in the end, for as the t • 1.....14-1e went on they apparently adapted themselves i 'eu the , new conditions and their earnings at the ; he' of the year were very nearly the same as they . wandi leen in previous years. In the" conclusions" he appends to his report,Mr. MATHER urges tri his fellow employers to give the results of vi e e'xperlinent the fullest consideration, with the making the forty-eight hour week univer- ;" in the industry, and he points out that the "'teal riain ._ 1 title ..1 e aLed Society of Engineers are now en- -n. to recommend the adoption of the system. 0 maintains that the two hours before breakfast ' :pre.enot worth the trouble they cost, whether to o 11.0_,Peeple or employers. ,‘The effect on the 1 - borilTeople must," he adds, "be most damaging riot° PllYsleally and mentally ; otherwise we can- tl account for the remarkable fact that - when t. foirers..e_ hours were struck off as much work was per- li,e,lr ten all the year round, as when these hours bei e ernPl°Yed. Not only are these two hours their /Ireakfast almost worthless as time, but lr en'ce.o e.. on the physical and. mental condition of an tilei ni°ll is to depreciate the vigour, freshness thrightness which ought to prevail throughout e" o w°rlcing day if the best 'results are to be oh- \ wo'r,e(l." We do not see how the wisdom of these fapu: ;11). be onestionecl. Mr. MATHER can now k_f_lw3 claim. that he speaks only of that which he br, . • Ills conclusions are strengthened not only b"Y.415 own experience of the last twelve months, t also by the similar experiences of other em- Plo Yers in ether parts of the country. The ques-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>tion of shortening the hours of labour is bound to be—is now, in fact—one of increasing impor- tance. The present Government may claim that, if' it has not led the way, it hateat all events promptly joined the company of a few of the most enlightened and progressive employers in the country. Mr. MATHER, speaks strongly in favour of mutual arrangements between employers anti work-people as against legal compulsion, and there can be no doubt that the foimer is the more desirable method if it can be made effectual. But all employers of labour are not of the same type as Mr. MA.THER,and there have been Governments in this country, and may be again, with a labour policy not so enlightened as that of the present Government. It may be, therefore, that the general shortenkng of hours of labour will not be brought about by mutual agreements between employers and workpeople. In some trades—that of mining for example—the bulk of the employers seem determined to set their faces against the demand for shorter hours, and wherever this is the case the workmen have no choice but to seek relief through legislation.</p>
        <p>THERE are two or three matters of Warrington Town Council. interest and importance on the agenda of the meeting of the War- rington Town Council to be held on Tuesday even- ing next. The letter from Mr. John Platt resign- ing his alderrnancy and his position in the Town Council will be received with regret by the Coun- cil and the town generally. Mr. Platt has been a member of the Council for a long time, and .his election,a few years ago,to an aldermanic seat was only a fitting recognition of the 'very active and prominent part he had taken, both in Committee and in the Council, in the municipal business of the borough. Daring the last two or three years owing, unfortunately, toinpaired health and resi- dence away from Warrington, he has not been in such close contact as previously with the affairs of the town. That he has felt it necessary to resign will, as we have already said, be generally regret- ted. This resignation will, of course, involve th 9 election of a successor to Alderman Platt, and in regard to this it may, we expect, be taken for granted that the usual practice will be followed. This practice has been, so far as the aldermanic seats are concerned, to elect a Liberal to succeed a Liberal, and, similarly, a Conservative to fill the place of a Conserva- tive. The relative positions of the two parties in the Town Council are not altered by such a course, while the legitimate claims of the minority to a proportionate number of the aldermanic seats is fairly admitted. As mattcrs now stand there are six Conservative as compared with three Liberal aldermen, and we should think that no one will argue that the Liberals are unduly favoured by such an allocation of the seats. Another part of the business of the Council on Tuesday evening will be the consideration of the estimates of ex- penditure for the next twelve months of the various committees, and the laying of an improve- ment and sewers rates. It is proposed that the improvement rate shall be three shillings and eightpenee in the pound and the sewers rate two- pence in the pound. The proposed improvement rate is the same as last year, but it should be remembered that this rate reached a higher figure last year than it had ever done before—at least for over thirty years. The sewers rate shows a decrease of twopence upon that of last year. It is not necessary to say that these two rates do not by any means cover all the claims that are made against the ratepayers, and it is understood that there will be an increase in the total rates of some- thing like fourpence in the pound ; that is to say, from six and fourpence to six and eightpence. Only on two occasions within the last thirty-sevel4 years have the total rates reached as high as six and eightpence. In 1881 they were at the same level, and as far back as 1862 they suddenly went up as high as six and tenpence.</p>
        <p>THE vestry meetings which have been The Vestries numerous this week have, on the whole, passed over as quietly and decorously as the most peace-loving of parish- ioners could desire. A few years ago Warring- ton and its neighbourhood was rather famous for its lively vestry meetings, but this year no dis- tinction of that kind can be claimed. At Frod- sham that most indefatigable of controversialists, Mr. ROBEILT FOULKES, made another attempt to set things right according to his own views, but his efforts were not successful. Those in author- ity at the Parish Church of Frodsham seem to prefer to be wrong without Mr. Foulkes than to be right with him. At least, that is no doubt the conclusion to which Mr. Foulkes has been forced by this time. At St. Thomas, Stockton Heath, an interesting point was raised. The schoolmaster at the Church schools at Stockton Heath, as at many other Church schools, is organist and choir- master. The three offices are combined, but to one only does there seem to be a salary attached. The schoolmaster is paid but the onerous duties of organist and choirmaster have to be thrown in. There is something rather contemptible in exac- tions or impositions of this kind. The school- master's salary will, probably, be almost if not , entirely. derived from the State grants to the schools, yet it is made to cover services to the church. Arrangements of this kind are not creditable to a wealthy organisation like the Established Church, especially in view of the eager protestations that the Church is in no way subsidised by the State.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND WIGAN POSTAL RE-UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND WIGAN POSTAL RE-UNION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and Wigan postal officials took advantage of Bank Holiday to, d battlo on the football field and afterwards. held a ,very successful re-union at the Patten Arms. About 70 postal officials sat down to a capital dinner, which had been provided by Mr. Kierna n. Dinner over, a smoking concert was held presided over by. Mr. Bennett, superintendent, Warrington, and a very entertaining programme was gone through. Thanks are due to Mr. G. Riley who acted as accompanist, and also to Mr. Kiernan for the excellent dinner which be had provided for the gue,Ets. BEYOND THE JUDGE'S JURISDICTION.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court, on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, the Far Nicer Tea Company, Limited, Lythwie.!4-lane, War- rington, claimed £3 from W. Thornton,lll, Chorley- road, Swinton, grocer, for tea supplied. Mr. Percy Davies appeared for the kiefendant, and at the out- set objected to the case being heard on the ground that the company was in liquidation, but his Honour amended the sufnmons by substituting the name of the liquidator. Mr. William Furness, for that of the company. Mr. Davies then contended that the case did not come within the jurisdiction of his Honour, seeing that the goods were ordered at Swinton, the address on the invoice being 19, Corporation-street, Manchester, and the defendant did net know that the company had a business at Warrington. Mr. Farness stated that the regis- tered office ,was in Manchester, but the business was at Warrington, and a slip of paper with " War- rington "on was usually attached to the bills, but he observed that it had not been done in th pre- sent case.—His Honour held that he had no juris- dictien, end the me fell through.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
