      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>INTIM-WORKS FOOTBALL COMTSTITION AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The following games have been played during the week :—On Tuesday, Mathieson's 2 tries. Wiggs nil ; on Wednesday, Deacon's 2 goals, 6 tries, Atlas iiil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HOLY TRINITY DAY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The remarks of the Diocesan Inspector in his report on the recent examination in Holy scripture at the above schools are very encouraging. The answering was very good, and the examiners expressed themselves as well pleased. BALL AT WIDNUS. _ _</p>
        <p>A very successful hall was, held in the Drill Hall, on Monday ni,At. Tier e was a large company present,and dancing was kept up till an early hour on Tuesday morning to the music of Mr. Powell's band. The pro- ceeds go to the S. Mary's band. 6wiNG BOAT ACCIDF.NT AT WIDNES. A youth named Joseph Rotherham, of GlKdstone-st.., was on Monday thrown out of a swing boat near the promenade. He was taken to the Accident Ho-pitakand attended by Dr. flutchinson, who found him suffering from concussion of the brain. OAKLAND-ST. WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPM, WIDNES. A meeting was held in the schoolroom, on Tuesday evening. There was a large attend nee. An excellent programme of songs, recit ttions, &amp;c., which were illus- trated t,y a magic lantern, was given by Mr. Blinston and friends, and was much appreciated. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It you want your WATCH HIPAIHED by skilled works:tea take it to PRITCITARD'B. Adt.t. SIMM'S CROSS PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHNPEL, WIDNES. A grand concert was given in the anove place, on Wednesday evening, by the membns of the Runcorn Glee Party. Councillor S. Owens presided over a large attendance. An excellent programme, consisting of song,s, duets, qoartetts, recitations, Ste., were gone through in an admirable manner. ST. MARY'S AMATSUR DRAMATIC SOCIETY, WIDNES. This society aopeared for the tint time before an au- dience, at the Alexandra Theatre, on Saturday night. There was a large attendance. The inembers gave an excellent representation of the hist,rical Irish drama " Robert Emmett." The proceeds will he devoted to- wards the St. Mary's band new instrument fund.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCITARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 61, If ig Ituneorn.—ilirt. A VIOLENT WOMAN. - - -</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Ass-izes. Jane Kelly (22) was indicted ter having unlawfully wounded Gordon AP Kay, on the 3rd Mar2h. The evidence showed that during a drunken spree in the prisoner's house at Widnes, the prisoner wounded proiecutor with a mug. The jury found the prisoner,guilty. His lordship, in passing sentence, said the prisoner was a violent woman, and she must go to gaol for 18 months with hard labour. 0. to PRITCHARD's, 51, High-st:, Runcorn, for tbe best FIVE GUINEA WATCH a working man can have.—Advt. BANK HOLIDAY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Monday was'observed in Widnes as a general holiday amon“st tradesmen, but almost all the works were in full swine. 7,0 day opened very promisingly and a great number of people took advantage of the various trips, promoted by the railway corn panics, to spend a day in the country or sea-side: For the "stay-at-homes" hobby horses, swings, Sto„ had been erected near the promenade, and they were well patronised.</p>
        <p>When in want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING Or BIRTHDAY PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-street.— Adt.t.</p>
        <p>ASSISTANCE FROli RUNCORN TO THE NATIONAL UNION</p>
        <p>OF TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Amongst the many purses received on Wednesday by Princess ChriFtian a,t the annwal Easter Confer- ence of the National Union of Teachers at the Sheidonian Theatre, Oxford, and which contained contributions to the Teachers Benevolent and Orphanage Funds were the following from they Runcorn district :—Greenway-road and Mill-brow Board Schools, ; Wesleyan Schools, £2 10s. ; National Schools, £2 ss. ; Newtown Infants' School, 135. ; Weston Wesleyan School, £1 75.; Weston Point Church School, 55.; Halton Grammar School, Cs. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, 51, High-street. Itnneora, keeps SPF.CTaCLES to suit all.sights, from a shilling a pair.—Adat. MISETING OF THE RUNCORN SONS OF TRKPERANCE. Mr. Thomas Clarke presided over the usual fortnightly meeting of this Association on Saturday night last. Mr. George E. Sutton prdfoundly interested the audience by reading an essay entitled " Moses : the Illustrations Prophet and Legislator of the Hebrews '' which had the effect of provoking a discussion on many of the points dealt with by •Nir. Sutton. Finally Mr. Sutton received the thanks of those present for his invaluable contribu- tion to the evening's proceedings. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for fifty years. and quality and first class workmanship are still guaranteed. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Tai LATE MR. EDWARD HIGGINS OF RUNCORN</head>
        <p>Tai LATE MR. EDWARD HIGGINS OF RUNCORN</p>
        <p>The solemn ceremony of interring the remains of the late Mr. Edward Higgins of Regent-street, took place in the Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday, the Rev. W. Albert officiating. The body was borne to the graveside by four members of the local branch of the Sons of Temperance, with which society the deceased had for some time buen an associi:te. Several other members of the same body formed a portion of the funeral procession. Mr. Higgins was only 28 years of age, and his loss is deeply mourned by his young widow and son. Numerous floral tributes of sympathy and respect were laid upon the cuffin by the relatives and friends of the deceased.</p>
        <p>DEMENTED OR NOT DEMENTED ?</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes,Charles Price,aged twenty-seven, labourer, was on trial convicted of a criminal offence against Lillie Lee, a child under four years of age, at Widnes, on the 4th March. During the evidence reports were produced to show that the prisoner had been in the Army, from which he had been discharged on account of dementia. Dr. Bearnish, surgeon of Walten Gaol, how- ever, testified to having had the prisoner under his ob- servation for some time, with the result that he had found him to be weak-minded,but still capable of know- ing what he was doing,. Tne prisoner could also read and write. Dr. Allen, of Widnes, was also of opinion that the prisoner was quite rational. His lordship sen- tenced the prisoner to eighteen years' penal servitude, observing that he was totally unfit to be at large.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>•WIDNIIS 14'REE LIBRARY COMMITTER.</head>
        <p>•WIDNIIS 14'REE LIBRARY COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C., was in the chair. There were also present Aldernaan S. Sadler, Councillor Wright, O'Donnell, Messrs. F. Neil, D. J. 13rookes, J. Morrison, F. H. Pankhurst. T. Craik (deputy town clerk), S. Hoyle (accountant), and Miss Procter (librariao). The librarian reported that during February 13,952 persons visited the reading rooms, which gave an average at- tendance of 589 for 24 days, as compared with a daily average of 539 in the corresponding moth of last year. Total number of books issued 6,1 87, which showed a daily average of 258 ae.ainst 215 last year. The c, so received amounted to £2 193. sd. including £2 is. 6 I. , for fines. The report was adopted as satisfactory. Accounts am.unting to £l6 63. 43. were passed for Imy• ment. A list of new books ordered to be purchased co- tamed about 100 volumes. SATURDAY NIMITS' CONCERTS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The last of the series of concerts, promoted by th,. United Alkali Co., took place in the Baths AsgemNy Rooms on Saturdarevening. There wal a hrge attend- ance. The prizes were distributed to the competitors who were successful in thQ several competitions held during the run of the 04mcerts. Prize for clog dancing, Mr. J. Cass.ons ; Ist prize for sentimental song, Mr. J. Norris ; 2nd prize for ditto, Mr. H. Pilgrim ; Ist prize for comic song, Mr. P. Garrity ; 20d prize for ditto, Mr. C. T. Kenyon. A prize for the best rendering of a quartette was awarded to employs from Messrs. Musprati's Works (Masters R. Warburton and H. Martindale, and Messrs. A. Kenyon and J. Norris). An admirable pro- gramme was then gone through. Mr. E. M. Harrison was presented with a handsome volume of music and a pipe and case for the great service he had rendered through the season as accompanist, Tw,. members of the Committee wore also presented with an elegant cigar cage each.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A MAN AT RUNCORN: THE INQUEST. On Tuesday afternoon at the Runcorn Town Hall, Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, held an inquiry into the cir- cumstances attending the sudden death of John Albert Cook, general labourer, who recently resided at 43 Caw- dor-st., Runcorn.—Mrs. Annie Cook, mother of the deceased, deposed to her son being nut of work for a considerable period. He was 24 years of age and up to Saturday last he had not complained of feeling ill. On the morning of that day he left the house for the purpose of searching for work but returned at 12.30 saying that none was to be found. He then went to the outhouse at the rear of the house and subsequently walked upstairs into the bedroom without saying a word. After a short time wit rtes became somewhat frightened and went up- stairs also. On entering the room she found him lying on a box breathing heavly. Thinking that something had happzned witness sent immediately for Dr. Mangan, but before that gentleman arrived her son had died. .Some weeks ago her son had complained of a pain on his chest bUt irf nothing else.—Dr. Mangan stated that he had attended deceascd about fifteen or sixteen .months ' ago for nervous prostration,he being then unable to follow his employment.-. Since that time he had not seen de- ceased previous te. being called in on Saturday last. When witness reathed the house the man was dead and upon a post mortem examination being made it was found that the cause of 'death was failure of the heart's action, due to valvular disfase. This was a disease which caused death sometimes with very little warning. Witness thought that all the mischief was attributable to nervous exhaustion.--A verdict of " Death from natural causes" was recorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
