      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EX MJ.XE ATURDAY. MAIXEI 31. 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EX MJ.XE ATURDAY. MAIXEI 31. 1894.</p>
        <p>AN OLD OFFENDER IN THIII RUNCORN POLICR COURT.</p>
        <p>Yesterday (Friday) morning before Mr. P. Speakman, at the Runcorn Police Court, Harriet Baxter, a frequent visitor to that court, was fined 103. and costs or 14 days imprisonment, for having been drunk and disorderly at Runcorn on the previous day, Thursday.</p>
        <p>COMING OF AGE CELEBRATION AT BILL CLIFFS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the coming of age of Mr. James Strange, jun., third son of Mr. James Strange, of Summer View, Walton, was celebrated by a party,which took place in Hill Cliffe Baptist School- room, and which had been very kindly lent for the occasion. About seventy friends accepted the in- vitation of the host and hostess to tea. After 'par- taking of a most excellent meal the health of Mr. and Mrs. &amp;naive, and Mr. James Strange, jun., was drunk, and short congratulatory speeches delivered by Mr. William Roberts, Mr. Owen, Mr. Bradford, Mr. Critchley, Mr. Breakall and other friends. I capital musical programme had also been provided. Altogether a most enjoyable evening was spent,and the company separated shortly after midnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ANTI-VACCINATION AT rttINCOR.N.</head>
        <p>ANTI-VACCINATION AT rttINCOR.N.</p>
        <p>The members of the lininswiek Mutual Improvement Class held their weekly meeting aa usual on Wednesday evening, when a powerful denunciation of vaccination was delivered, in the form of a paper, by Mr. Walter Millington. The speaker cited f3DITI, evil eff,cts which are said to follow the operation and cwitended very strongly th it the existing vaccinator' law should ls)e speedily amended or else repealed. TherA was a large attendance and the proceedings proved exceedingly interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>TABLEAUX VIVANTS AT WIDNItg.</head>
        <p>TABLEAUX VIVANTS AT WIDNItg.</p>
        <p>A sere gof tableaux vivarits was given in the Paro- chial Rooms, on Thursday “ lid Friday evenings. There were largo attendanees on both nights. Dr!! parts were taken hy the following :—Mesdarnes Cliam•wrs. Tiw Misses Huhn., Jones, Preston, E. Preston, Deaeon, a, Deacon, ft:yl,l ani White. Messrs. Timmitis., D,-aeom, Lightburn, T. Jones, A. Carey, E. Carer, and A. N. Other. The various tableaux repre- sentations were very effectivcily rendered and elicited loud applause. The proceeds will be devoted ,to the Sc. Paul's Parochial Rserns. THF. _BATHS_ AGITATION IN RUNCORN%</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Collier is still endeavouring to awaken the people of Runcorn to th 4 pressing necessity tor the pro- viskn of public baths ill the town. He has been instru- mental in drawing up a requisition, and the convening of a meeting of ratepayers to consider the question. Signatures to this cbicurnent have been solicited with success, some of the names being those of well-knnwn .ratepayers in the town. Mr. Collier has also iroached the matter to several of our medical gentlemen, many of whom assert that such a pnvision would, from a sani- tary point of view, confer incalculable benefit upon the town. It is understood that Mr. S. Taylor, chairman of the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners, will shu-tly convene a public meeting to discuss the matter. FINANCIAL REPORT OF TRH ORDER OF RECHABITES,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. Tha Runcorn and Frodsha.m district of the Independ- ent Order of Rechabites, have just published their sixth annual report from which we extract the following : The receipts in connection with the adult funeral fund amounts to £B7 7s. 10., and the expenditure £BO. The income of the juvenils funeral fund was £3 5L 3d., And the expenses di!. The management fund receipts amounted to £2l 19s. 5d., a,minst disbursements £l9 19 20. The balances stood—adult funeral fund, £272 us. 9d.; juvenile ditto, £22 16s. ld. ; and man- agement, £6 is. 51. ; total £3Ol 9s. 4d. The officers chosen for the year ensuing are as follows :—District Chief Ruler, Mr. D. Hulse, Frodsham ; District Deputy. Ruler, Mr. W. Cartwright,Runcorn ; Past District Chief Ruler, Mr. Robert Evans, Runcorn ; District Treasurer, Mr. James Lea, Runcorn ; District S-eretarr, Mr. J. E. Warburton, Runcorn ; District Trustees, Mr. Joseph Leadstone, Runcorn ; Mr. Samuel Sharp, Frodsham ; and Mr. Robert Evans, Runcorn. WOUNDING A WIDNES CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Widnes Petty Sessions, Eizabeth Jones, a married -xoman, of 25, Derby-road, Farnworth, was brought up on remand charged with wounding P.C. Heaton on Monday evening. P.C. Heaton ststed that on the night in question he was on duty at Farnworth, and about 150 yards from prisoner's house. He could hear screaming and shouting and found it came from prisoner's house He went into the house, when de- fend int picked up something and threw it at him. It struck him on the upper lip and blood began to flow. He noticed that a glass had gone from the table. He took prisoner into custody and on the way to the police station defendant said it was a glass she had thrown. He had the wound dressed and had since been off duty. Dr. Hutchinson stated that the last witness came to him. He found an irregular wound on the upper lip, nearly three quarters of an inch in l!migth. An artery wound had been severed. Samuel Fenny and George Wright spoke as to seeing the constable go into the house, and on his coming out noticing his lip cut. Prisoner wag committed for trial at the County Quarter Sessions. Bail was allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Two MORE SUDDEN DEATHS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>Two MORE SUDDEN DEATHS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Joseph Hancock, chemical labourer, aged 35, and re- cently residing at 5, Ciranville-street, Runcorn. was on Monday evening ,suddenly taken ill while passing over Runcorn Bridge. lie was assisted home by some friends arid subsequently attended by Dr. Robinson's assitant. Notwithstanding this gentleman's skill and attention, flanc,)ck died on the afternoon of the following day,the opinion of themedical practitioner being that the cause of death was heart disease. The Coroner on being in- formed of these facts decided not to hold an enquiry.— George Thomas Haves, aged two months' son of Samuel Hayes, of Lowe's Court, Cooper-street, died somewhat suddenly on Tuesday morning at five o'clock. The Coroner was communicated with and it was his inten- tion to hold an inquest that same afternoon but the towa nurse informed him that the child had been weakly from death and he afterwards considered that an inquest was unnecessary. Whilst givieg expression to this deci- sion on Tuesday afternoon the Coroner said that in the district in which he lived they were trying to make pro- vision for a nurse. Nurses were a blessing to the com- munity, invaluable assistance being often rendered by them, and he was pleased to see that they had a nurse in Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EIGHTEEN LASHES WITH THE "CAT."</head>
        <p>EIGHTEEN LASHES WITH THE "CAT."</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, on Monday, 64ore Mr. Ju,ltice Day, John Shields (31) labourer living at Warrington, and who is well known to the police, was indicted for robbery with violence at Warring- ton, and was sent to penal servitude for live years, and ordered to be once flogged, receiving eighteen lashes with the cat. His Lordship said the prisoner was a notorious character, and he considered it absolutely necessary to prevent him from doing any further mischief for some time to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FELL IN THE CANAL.</head>
        <p>FELL IN THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>• A boy seven years of age, the son of Mr. Turner, New-road, Latchford, was nearly drowned in the I Latchford and Runcorn Canal at Stockton Heath,on I Sunday afternoon. The boy with a number of other children, was playing on the banks of the canal near the swing bridge when he fell in. The acci- dent was observed by several bystanders who imme- diately ran to the boy's assistance, and he was rescued just as he was sinking for the third time. lie was conveyed to Captain Nlytton's house in a state of unconsciousness, and restoratives being applied, in a short time he recovered sufficiently to enable him to proceed home. FIVE YEARS FOR ASSAULTING A HUSBLND.</p>
        <p>Mary Ihlliweil,4o, was charged at the Liverpool Assize, on Monday, before Mr. Just c Day, with unlawfully wounding her husband at Wrightington. Mr. Justice Day said it was fortunate for the pri- soner that she had not been tried on the capital charge,for the wounds she had inflicted on her hus- band's throat with a razor were almost sufficient to cause his death. It was one of the most: aggravated cases he had ever tried, and he must award the pri- soner the maximum penalty of five years' penal servitude. The prisoner fainted on hearing the sen- ii,nc. Mrs. Hall, well some tima ago lived in War- rington, where. ,Ito is well known. suicmli AT WARRINGTON.----INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. hr house held an inquest at the Three Pigeons lan, riallam-lane, on Tuesday aftemson,on tie, body ef ilenry Aston. a tanner, who committed suicide On Ft lay mcirninz.—Jane Aston said her told her that he had been given notice to leave his work. Hs did not seem low or depressed then. He went to bed after he had had his supper and seemed very restless. Early in the morning he got up and put some clothes on and went down- stairs. On witness going down some time after- wards she did not see him in the kitchen, but on going into the cellar she.found him hanging by a rope.—Charles Geddes said he was attracted to the deceased's house by hearing the noise and confus- ion. He went and saw the deceased hanging from a beam in the cellar with his feet clear of the floor. Witness cut the rope. Deceased was quite dead and • cold.—Police Constable Fish also gave evidence to seeing deceased lying down at the foot of the cellar stairs.—A man named Percival, the foreman at the works where deceased had been employed, said it was through no fault of his own that deceased was given notice to leave. Some machinery had been put down at the works and lessened the demand for manual labour.—The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide whilst temporarily insane." WARRINGTON CRESS CLUB. The season which was brought to a close last evening has been the most successful since the formation of the club. There has been a marked improvement in the standard of the member's play as the record of matches proves. To win four and draw two out of a total six matches is eminently satisfactory. The result of the club competition is that the trophy is awarded to the Rev. A. D. Firth who scored VE wins out of 9. A special meeting will he held at the Y.M.C.A. on Friday evening next to which we understand all outsiders interested in the came are invited, to present the trophy which will for a day or two prior to the meeting be en view in Mr. Garland's window, Bridge-st. In ad- dition to that of Father Firth the trophy bears the names of the previous year's winners, Messrs. Dunce and Chandler. The follewing is n list of the prin- cipal scorers in this year's, competition: Garr es played. Won. L. Chandler ' lO ...</p>
        <p>A. D. Firth F. G. laylor F. Dunne F. L. Fleming 9 ... A. Wood .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EASTERTIDE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>EASTERTIDE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Lt the various places of worship Easter Sunday was appropriately observed and celebrated in Run- corn. Special services were conducted at the Parish, Sr. Michael's, and Holy Trinity Churches, all of which were tastefully adorned with flowers. The choirs at the respective churches rendered special musical selections in a most praiseworthy manner, which was thoroughly enjoyed by the large congregations assemblod. High mass was celebrated in deep solemnity by the Rev. Dr. Ciastaldi at the St. Edward's-Roman Catholic Church the rev, gentleman subsequently delivering an able address. The beauti- fully fine weather continued and on Monday, large num- bers of people left the town, some visiting the neighbour- ing pleasure resorts whilst others launched on trips to Chester., Manchester, North Wales, and other places. Several hundreds of folks were, on the other hand, attracted to the hoire football field where an exciting Match for the championship of the Liverpool Junior League was played, the contestants being Runcorn " A " and St. Anne's, Thrionhead, teams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MESSRS. FULLERTON AND S3f ISSION TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. FULLERTON AND S3f ISSION TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>(Br THE" REV. J. W. VARLEY.)</p>
        <p>It is said that a young clergyman once went to his Bishop, to ask permission to use a service not in the Prayer Book, and was met with the injunction to confine himsolf strictly to the order of morning and evening prayor as set forth for all the clergy. " But, sir," said the stripling, " I can never reach the pevle in that way." " Lien 80 much the worse for the people," said the Bishop, and there is not the slightest doubt that he profoundly believed it. Fortunately for all concerned, there are but few with ideas like this Bishop. Men and women of all shades of opinions are looking to their respective communions not alone for reverent worship, but first for a message of life and grace. Tired men and discouraged men, and quiet men,people, who,weary and heavy-laden now as of old, are waiting till some clear and persuasive " voice" shall bid them "come."</p>
        <p>Commencing to-day, there will be carrinl on in Warrington, a mission by two well-known Evange- lists Messrs. Fallerton and Smith. This mission will be held until April Bth. Evangelistic agencies in the domain of religion are no neVir thing. It would be impossible to writ( intelligently the reli- gious history of Great Britain without taking into account this Lature of it. Not in one sect or com- munion alone, but in almost all, its methods have obtained, and its results have been strenuously sought. Even if we go back through the centuries, we shall find the Evangelist has occupied a distinc- tive position in the religious world. Whether we look at Peter the Hermit, or Francis of AssiA, or Savonarola, or John Huss, or George Wnitfield and John Wesley,the thing is too familiar to be ignored or wholly disesteemed, and no effort to distinguish betweEn great national or ecclesiastical movements occurring at, long intervals, can prevent us from acknowledging that there still remains a place even in the best ordered and best instructed system for a fresh voice ; for that gift which even all great preachers do not possess, of direct and personal ad- dress, and most offiall, for those personal contacts which are, after all, the most'potent force in any ministry, even as they are the rarest and most difficult to achieve.</p>
        <p>Now! one of the most beautiful things that we can see and for which we require to be thankful is the fact that in this work of evangelistic activity in Warrington we have the different denominations joined. In ale harmonious blending of diverse elements we find true unity. We believe in unity. But we can have a unity which may mean death and not life. An elder of a church wa:i asked how his church was getting on—they had had divisions and fights for many years—" Well," said he, " we are united at last." "how is that," said the other elder? "We are frozen, frozen clear through from top to bottom" replied he. But that, is not the unity which the various religious boaies engaged in this mission manifest. We recognise the fact that even the divisions of our denominations are not radical or antagonistic divisions. Each is doing its part in the great work, end all are united in seek- ing by this mission to extend truth ; to uplift righteousness to convince men and women and children that one world is not enough for them. _</p>
        <p>That the Nonconformist ministers have acted wisely in inviting l!kiessrs. Fullerton and Smith to visit Warrington is not open to argument. These two gentlemen have had many many years' experi- ence in such work as itis proposed that they should carry on here. They have visited almost every im- portant centre of population in this country and everywhere they have been honoured with abund- ant success. Two facts are worthy of trwntion. Wherever the Evangelists have been, they receive requests for a second and even a third visit and they are always heartily welcomed. As they, usually labour in connection wi h churche. there is a surer harvesting of results than is of: en realised under other circumstances. Christian workers of all fellow,hips have invariably and heartily joined in their work, and it is hoped that such will be the case in t him town.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ful!erton in connection with his c-)lleague has been devoted for the past 15 year,: to this special work. It is no exaggeration to say that, the con- verts of his ministry may he reckoned by thou • sands and these for the most pin have kiined varieue•rehgions bodie.. His gifts for ,erviee are many and diversified, lie was trained in the col- lege retired by the late Charles Haddon ;:purgeon, whose pulpit he has occupied over 109 time, nn evidence at once of his popularity as a preacher end of Mr: Spurgeon's contilence. He has the power of laying under tribute ,he usual stores of a cul- tured mind. and the careful husbandry of close ob- servation and experience. Those who have studied the man and his resources during a campaign, some- times. extending over several weeks, have been sur- prised at the freshness of his addressei and the force and aptitude of his appeals Mr. Fullettcae's Bible readings reveal a mastery of the subject. he has before him, and are eminently useful and re- frc,ehinz. It may not be known widely that, he has recently accepted the pastorate of Melborne Hall, Leicester, a church which ever since its founda- tion by the Rev. F. 13. Meyer, 8.A., has taken high rank among our provincial churches as one of the most important evangelistic centres. The church has seating accommodation for fully 1,500 people. It has 800 members. There are 2,000 children in the Sunday schools while its agencies are " Legion." It is believed that, Mr. Fullerton will very worthily sustain the honourable record of service cArried on by gifted men for many years. So recently as 1891 Mr. Spurgeon said "Among the great preachers of the present day, Fullerton deserves one of the front seats."</p>
        <p>J. Manton Smith is his very worthy helper. The part he usually takes is the service of praise and prayer, and accompanies the reading of the Scrip- tures with racy exposition and illu,tration,and thus prepares the way for the address of Mr. Fullerton. Mr. Smith is a typical Englishman. He is so good all round—genuine, devoted,earnest, versatile—and so successful, that it, is quite impossib:e to say in what respect you would like him to be different to what he is. lie has a voice of,rare quality. His silver cornet with which he leads the service of praise,is an additional charm to most.. As a speaker to young people he is probably unsurpassed,and his "Children's services" are usually remembered .for many years. When, therefore, one contemplates the object of the mission,or reviews Vic gifts which are to be con- secrated to it, no person need be afraid of its ulti- mate success. The race of Evangelists is not yet extinct. We have John McNeil, the Presbyterian; W. M. Hay Aitken, the Church of England clergyman ; D. L. Moody, who belongs to all denominations ; Thomas Champness, the Methodist ; but we can confidently place W. Y. FuLerton and J. Manton Smith among the bravest and most consecrated of them all. And those who go to-night to liar Mr. Smith, and dur- inr the next eight days to hear them botb,will pro- bably admit then the truth of these wcrds.</p>
      </div>
