      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>WARItINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS OF THE NEXT MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Town Council meeting will be held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening at six o'clock, when the following business will be brought forward for consideration: AN ALDERMANIC VACANCY:</p>
        <p>A letter will be read from Alderman Platt resigning the office of Alderman of the Borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NEW BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement and —Lighting Committee recommend that the pans of the following buildings be accepted :—Seven cottages in Wellington-st., for Mr. Peter Rigby; thirteen houses in New-st.,off Manchester- road, for Mr: John Appleton; bakehouse in Lytligoeg- lane, for Mr. Furness ; alteration to house in Clegge-et.. for Mr. McCue ; two cottages off Liverpool-road, and near Welltield-st., for Messrs. J. aod A. Payne ; altera- tions and additions to Volunteer Inn, Cockhedge-lane. for Messrs. Oreenall, Whitley, and Co., Limited ; c )t- -tage in Orford-lane, for Mr. William Brooks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>QUEEN'S HOTEL STABLES.</head>
        <p>THE QUEEN'S HOTEL STABLES.</p>
        <p>The following recommendation is sti-l;:nittNl by the Street Improvement Committee : "That permission be given to Mr. Lawrence Flood to sub-let the yard tied stables at the Queen's Hotel to Mr. Edward Connor, for the purpose of his business."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DEPUTATIONS.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommended that the Chair- man, Deputy Chairman, and Medical Officer of Health be appointed to attend the congress of the British Insti- tute of Public Health, to be held in London on the 26th to 31st July next, and that the Chairman, Councillor Roberts; Medical Officer of Health, and Town Clerk be appointed to attend a Preliminary Conference at Man- chester on the subject of the appointment of a Joint Board as a Port Sanitary Authority for the Manchester Ship Canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>THE_INFECTIOUS DISEASES IIOOPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE_INFECTIOUS DISEASES IIOOPITAL.</p>
        <p>'The Health Committee recommend that the Borough Surveyor and the Medical Officer be instructed to con- sider and report on the proposed extension of the Hospi- tal for infectious diseases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SANITARY WORKS COMISITTZI?.</head>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COMISITTZI?.</p>
        <p>. This Committee recommend that the Chairman, De- putv-Chairman, and Mr. Deas be appointed to attend the -Congress of the British Instituto ot public health, to be held in London, on the:26th to 31st July next.</p>
        <p>CARTING TO BB DONB BY CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>Ihe Paving and Sewerage Committee has considered the question of stable accommodation, &amp;c., and recom- mends that no additional horses be purchased, and that all carting under this department *be done by contract after the horses now employed I,ave been dispensed with. This Committee further recommend that the main sewer be extended in Marshhouse-lane to the east side of the Barrack.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>It is recommended by tiriTC-o-m;i7Cee. isirai Mr. Coun- cillor Garner be appointed Deputy-Chairman of this C,)mmittee and that Mr. Councillor Baird be appointed a member of this Committee.</p>
        <p>GIFTS TO TME MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>The Museum, Library, Arts, and Technical Instruc- tion Committee report ,the following gifts, for which the thanks of the Commitee were ordered to be sent to the donors :—Stoat and Water rail from Appleton: from Mr. Broady ; Pencil Drawing, by Fuery, of " Warrington from Prospect Hill in 182 " ; Photograph of Church Gatos ; Ivory Tioket of Thos. Robson as a Student at the Royal Academy ; Silver Nledal oLSociety of Arts pinci by Thos. Robson in 1819 ; Eight etters and Parcel of Pamphlets, from Mr. Win. Robson ; Cairo-st., chapel report for 1893 ; GeohgicAl Map of Scarborough Dis- trict, lrom Mr. W. U. Woodc,ck.</p>
        <p>TEES COUNTY COUNCIL, GRANT.</p>
        <p>The Museum, 4Sr., Committee report. a letter from the Technical Instruction Coia.mittee of the County Council' stat inv. that no further giants cnuld he made in response to so application of this Committee.</p>
        <p>THE CIIIIETEIt Y.</p>
        <p>From the report of the Registrar, it appears that the interments for the past four weeks were :—Adults, 38 ; minors, 44 ; stillborn and premature, 11. Correspond- ing period last year :—Adult, 53 : minors, 48 ; stillborn and. premature, 14. The receipts for the past four wee's were £67 is. 10:1., corresponding period last year, £92 13s. 6 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TIIIS BATHS RHCEIPTS.</head>
        <p>TIIIS BATHS RHCEIPTS.</p>
        <p>the report of the bath-keeper states that the income from the Br hs for the pat four weeks was £1.5 1. 6d.</p>
        <p>MA MEET RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The tDtal income from all sources for the past four weeks was £315 is. 23. Corresponding period last. year, £283 185.3 d. Increase, £3l 2s. 11d.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GAS.</p>
        <p>The average illuminating power of the gas for the past four weeks was equal to 18•86 at both works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The Rivers Committee recommend that the horoug h surveyor prepare a map showing position of Ship Canal in relation with the borough with the heights of all weirs between the river Rollin and Bank Quay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PRECAUTIONS AGAINST FIRE AT THE WOIVEROUSE.</head>
        <p>PRECAUTIONS AGAINST FIRE AT THE WOIVEROUSE.</p>
        <p>The Fire Engine Committee propose that the Guar- dians be recommended to lay on an 8-inch water main to the Workhouse, with proper and sufficient hydrants.</p>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommend that an 8-inch main be laid from the Barracks, Marsh-house-lane, to the end of Gorsey-lane, and that upon the application of Mr. A. Lister-Kaye, the main in Manchester-road be extended from Bruche House to Bruche Lodge,at the cost of £125, upon an undertaking being given to pay £l.O icier annum for five years (such sum to include a supply for domestic purposes until the water rate yields that amourst),and to pay the cost of laying the main to Bruche Hall and farm. The charge for watsr to be at the ordinary domestic rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>Tite following estimates of expenditure for the year commencing March 26, 1894, will be submitted for approval. Watch Committee for pay and clothing of police, £4,672 Os. 6d.; and estabiishnient expenses, £528 10s. 04. Street Improvement acd Lighting Com- mittee, £2,930; Health Committee,on improvement rate, £9OO ; hospital department, £1,968 15s. ; borough rate' analyst'a department, £65; contagious diseases (animals) department, £2O; total, £2,953 15s. Sanitary Woiks Committee: On the nightsoil department, £4,080 18s.; Longford denot, £2,709 3s. ; intercepting depots, £823 125., total, £8,413 19:3. Paying and Sewerage Commit- tee :—On the paving department, £5,320; scavenging department, £3,298 193. 6d. ; total, £7,706 19s. 6d. School Atteneance Committee : Of £595 10s. Cemetery Committee : £724 ; Town H ill, etc., Commit- Tow7ci Hail depart ment, £1,155 14s. ; Hank Park department, £1,353 16s. ; is department, £6BO 10s. ; total. £3,193. Markets Committee : Markets depart- ment, £1,362 125.3 i; weights an 4 m,asures department, £163 Bs. ;total, £1.526 0.:. 31. Fire Engine Committee : £482. General Porooses Coinnaitte: : £7BB 75.11 Finance Coinrnit'ee : £6,534 2i.</p>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>The foliewin,r tenders ar, recommended for accrpt- ance.—Watch Committee : Of Mr. Henry lirailt, for the pain inz of the outside of the Bridewell at £l4 10s. tianitary Works Committe of the Pearson and Knowles Cost and Irno Co., Ltd., for the F:ipply of engine and pinvirs at £355, cast iron pipes at 10s. per cwt., steam pirs at lie. per cwt., and two sets of drawings, com- plete, showin, sections of strata, _427 103., and of Mr. Thomas Pilkington to supply horses and men at 7s. per day or night, and to cart ashes and house refuse at is. per ton for six months. Town Bali, Park, and Baths Committee, of Mr. W. Flet!her for the painting of the baths it. £l5 be accepted. Gas Committee ; of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway for the supply of 61,009 bricks at S. 41. per 1,000 :—Water Committee ;of the Stanton Iron Works Co., Limited. for the supply of 670 yards of 8-inch pipes, at £4 7s. 01. per ten.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COIPIITTRII.-TRE NEW RATES.</p>
        <p>• That the res4;nation of Mr. A. W. Il.award as as,is- taut collecter be accepted, to take effect in four weeks frotn this. date ; that calls be made on the overseers of the tcwnships of Warrington and Latchford on borough rate account for the sum of £10,347 4s, 3d., payable by two half-yearly instalments ; that the Finance Committee be authorised to make an im- movement rate for the year coin inencing 26th March, 1894, at 3s. Bd. in the to be collected by two half- Nearly instalments, which is estimated to produce about £24,600; that a general se v era rate he made for the year commencing 26b March, 1894, at 2d. in the pound, to be collected by two half-year!y instalments, which rate is estimated to produce about £1,175.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The prizes earned by the members of this society for regular attendance were distributed on Sunday. The books were placed in the v?.stibule and were given to the members on their entry. A list con- taining about eighty excellent books had been given to the members to select from. The Rev. J. Yonge was the chairman at the meeting and said that day was the anniversary cf the greatest victory the world ever saw accomplished—the victory of Jesus Christ over the grave. In referring to the prize distribution, he said that on the previous occasion 399 books were given and 80 certificates of merit awarded. This half-year they had distributed 630 books and 151 certifimtes, which made a total of 1,029 books distributed in connection with that Wycliffe P.S.A. Mr. Swift, of the British and Foreign Sailors Society, was the speaker and laid before his hearers the claims and the good work done on behalf of sailors by the society he represented. Ile considered that sailors deserved more puhlic sympathy and support than was at present given to them. The addruss was a very interesting and instructive one and was listened to with much attention. Mr. A. Griffiths sang the solo " The Sailor's Grave." Messrs. S. W. Jeffery and B. Farrar also took part in the meeting. There was a very large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BOLD WOU-NDING CASE.</head>
        <p>A BOLD WOU-NDING CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, on Monday, Ellen Ellison (29) was indietey for Lavirg at Bold, on the I.ln Deceml er, wounded Margaret Kinne.tr.-.--The jury f.)und the plis• oner guilty of unlawful wo..uiding.—She was sent to gaol for six months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The paper on the artistic aspects of photography read at the monthly meeting of the Amateur Photographic Society on Tuesday night, by Mr., Aylwaril, the master of the School of Art was an', excellent one, and full of good advice to am.iteur-, photographers. As a teacher of art, • he speak with authority upon the subject, and it is to be regretted that there were so few present to hear him. The Easter holidays as the chairman remarked were no doubt the cause of the meagre attend- ance, and, as Mr. Aylward has promised to again read the paper,he will no doubt have a much larger attendance, when that time comes.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting at the St. Barna.bas church this year proved a very tame affair and must have been terribly disappointing, to those who dearly loves row. Things have gone more smoothly during the past year than for some time past, so that there was not sufficient grievances, real or fancied, to arouse the passions of the parishioners. There was a slight chance of having the Emile], scenes, however,. at the commencement, when Mr. Benjamin found fault with the Vicar for not putting the notice on the church door, An d when Mr. Jenkins described such questions as" pettifogging" sad "contemp- tible.' The Vicar appeared to be rather anxious to join in the fray, but Mr. Jenkins, after giving his opinion of Mr. Benjamin's grievance, became a peacemaker and thus the incidenz: closed. The tusiness of electing a people's warden has prev- iously been the cause ot some of the most lively scenes, and the war of words between the vicar and Mr. Smith has been delightful (?) to listen to. On this occasion, everyone appeared anxious to compli- ment somebody else, and Mr. Smith was over- whelmed with expressions of esteem by the vicar's warden of last year, and was re-elected unani- mously. After this, no doubt St. Barnabas will be a " pattern to other parishes," as one of those prevent put it, for the vicar promises to meet everybody and even put the notice of the vestry meeting inside or outside the church, whichever might be wanted.</p>
        <p>There are a number of mischievous persons at the Warrington Parish Church as at other places of worship, who delight in putting buttons and other articles into the collection boxes, when they are handed round. Mr. Alfred Woods mentioned this fact at the Parish Church vestry meeting on Wed- nesday night. This practice is not a very hrlnour- able one,as it makes the person appear to be gener- ens, although at the same time he is the reverse. Another incident at this vestry meeting shows that political sympathies sometimes enter even into the question of election of sidesmen. During the elec- tion of the eight sidesmen, when the name of Aid. - J. E. Wright, who was one of the candidates, was put to the meeting, a voice was keard plainly all over the building, " We'll vote for him. lie's a Tory." Of course, I do not mean to imply that sidesmen are voted for on political lines, but still some people cannot keep politics out of anything.</p>
        <p>I notice that the Paving and Sewerage Committee are proposing to sell off their horses and to trust to contractors rather than their own workmen for the performance of their carting. Is this not a retro- grade kind of proposal ? I have noticed that all the more progessive Corporations and other public bodies are showing a tendency in exactly the opposite direction. Instead of resorting still further to contractors and middlemen they are more and more inclined to directly employ the labour they require. That is certainly the policy that is fav- oured by trades unions and working class ratepayers generally.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>We are, and have been during the whole of this week, in the midst of business and pleasure. .Eastertide, bring with it vestry and township meetings by the score, and some of these tifford almost as much reason for laughter and amusement as might, perhaps, he gained by visiting a punch and judy exhibition at a seaside resort. Judging from the attitudes which srne of our country wor- thies take at certain of these annual gatherings, it might be thought that they had been undergoing diligent training since the last occasion for the purpose of championing their" fads."</p>
        <p>The Frodsham vestry meeting generally partakes of the "lively" element, and last Monday proved no exception to the rule. There was a succession of "scenes," Mr. R. Foulkes of course being the originator. This worthy gentleman is never tvtppy, apparently, except when he is proposing the aboli- tion of some time-honoured custom. On Monday he asserted that floral decorations in church tPnded to encourage desecration; and accordingly he politely proposed that instead of the ladies being thanked for having decorated the building, they y should be asked to keep their flowers at home. o Needless to say this amendment was only favour ii - able to Mr. Foulkes himself and was consequently !'t negatived and the original motion of thanks car- • ried. He was also of the opinion that the wardens of the church did not understand their duties and began to haul out of one of his pockets a book which would teach them what duties they should perform. Some laughter was caused by one of the vestrymen proposing that Mr. Foulkes should take the wardens out and "lecture them."</p>
        <p>It is certainly a pleasant contrast to compare the orderly manner in which the business of the Run- corn Parish Church vestry is transacted to that of the Frodsham vestry. From the remarks passed by several of the speakers at the former meeting on Tuesday evening it is evident that the manner of conducting the business under the chairmanship of the Vicar was exceedingly agreeable to all, so much so that it was quite a pleasure to attenii. Such a state of things it is to be hoped will long continue and, as the Vicar remarked, if difficulties did arise, that they will be met in a manly spirit ant: dis- posed of in a proper manner.</p>
        <p>The idea prevails amongst local cyclkts that a cycling club should .be established in Runcorn as soon as possible. Such an idea has not been formed - a moment too soon as such beautiful weather as • that which we are experiencing at present might be taken advanmze of with benefit. The favour- able situation of Runcorn will, in a large measure, guarantee success to the movement, inasmuch as no difficulty will, I think, be experienced in draw- ing up a numerous list of pleasant runs.</p>
        <p>One of the most enjoyable soe;al,r!athnrinze which bare taken place lately in Ituneorn,v:as that</p>
        <p>of the Runcorn Constables on Wednesday evening in the Bethesda Lecture Hall. Surely, to no other set of public servants is such an event more wel- come than to the local police force. In order that we may rest throughout the night in a senst of perfect security, the police are obliged to weather the roughest storms of rain, and to face the fiercest</p>
        <p>gales. Mr. Taylor spoke very truly on Wednesday evening, when he said their lives were very monotonous, and they enjoyed to the fullest ex- tent any little break or relaxation of duty such as 44 the ene over which he was presiding. Every •vi pra6e is due to the committee. Messrs. T. Cooke, t:lt S. Ci,ventry, T. Wood, and J. A. Smith, for their un- bib remit! ing attention t') the work. end it is extremely 741 gratifying to know that their efforts have been. so triumphantly crowned with success. ,;t,•_„;"ii (.;;;;1</p>
        <p>Our worthy chairman of Commissioners, Mr. Samuel Taylor, must be a very happy man. He cannot have any public reason to be otherwise, en- joying as he undoubtedly does, the good opinion of such a large number of people. The eulogium which was poured upon him by every speaker at. the gathering was plainly an evidence of this, though perhaps on a somewhat small scale. We all know this that he is ever willing to lend a crin helping hand to any object sufficiently deserving his patronage and support, more especially if it is likely to result in the improvement of the social ,#O4 condition of the inhabitants of Runcorn. e</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL SUNDAY COLLECTIONS.</p>
        <p>itk3</p>
        <p>Since the publication on March 17th of the bpi amounts of the Hospital Sunday collections the fol- f‘lfS lowing have been received either by the Treasurer * or myself. A. J. JOLLEY, hon. sec: • 'T s. d. Wycliffe Church lO 14 2 '1 Be-road Wesleyan Church St. Ann's Church Orford Church Leh-st. Baptist Chapel</p>
        <p>nrcifinfare &amp;much</p>
        <p>Wel:Fh Church 0 0 0</p>
      </div>
