      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THE COOK'S YOUNG MAN. John Jones who said he was a painter from Warring- ton, was charged at DarFsbury Petty Sessions on Tues- day, with having on the 26th inst. at Moore' been found on a certain enclosed place for an unlawful purpose. The constable stationed at Moore said at nine o'clock the previous night be saw the prisoner coming from Miss Bessie Dutton's house. Ho (witness) stopped him and he said he had been to see one of the servants at the house. He searched the man and found a parcel of food on him. The man was in the habit of going to the house night after night and sometimes slept there. The prisoner stated that he had been courting the cook for eleven years and she gave him the food as he was cut of work. Superintendent Atherton said Miss Dutton did not wish to prosecute but she did not want the man to visit the premises again. The magistrates discharged the prisoner on his promising not t) go on the premises again.</p>
        <p>SOLD FOR A " At Paresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Isaic Kirk- ham, no fixed abode and employed on a threshing machine, was charged with having on th.: 23rd February at Antrobus stolen a sheep dog value £3, the Aroperty of Henry Norsott, farmer. Antrobus. The complainant stated that on the 23rd ult he loosed his dog on the farm at nine o'clock p.m., and at 5.30, the next morning the dog was missing. 'The police were informed, but nothing was heard of it until the 27th inst when he (witne) found the dog at a farm at Budworth. Frederick Wiltshire said he was emplo,ed on a threshing machine with the defendant. On the 23rd February the defendant hrotwht the dog to Mr. Icaa:.•.'s farm at Tabley where they were working. The defen- dant said the dog was his and asked witness if he would take the dog home at night. He did so and brought it back again the next morr ing. The defendant asked hini what he would give for the dog and witness told him that he had no money. The defendant then said that "he would take a wet for the dog." He (witness) paid for some beer for the defendant, hut the do, was after- wards claimed by Mr. Noreott —The defendant said he had no intentions of stealing the dog and denied having said the dog was • his. The magistrates fined him ss. and costs or in default fourteen days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MINOI: CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOI: CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATIIRDA.Y.—Before Mr. John Burgess (chairman), Messrs. T. Birehall and John GoAdes.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTS .—Joseph Bibby. jun , was charged with having on the 12th August. 1893. unlawfully assaulted and beaten Thomas PartridgP.—Prisoner pleaded guilty. —Mr. A. Browne prosecuted. He stated that on the 12th August, the defendant accompanied by anther man went into the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-road. 'I hey bad some beer, and soon afterwards had a Tow, and thet7</p>
        <p>had to be ejected. The manager, Mr. Partridge ejected the defendant and in so doing the defendant butted him in the chest, struck him some severe blows on the head with his fist, and when outside the hotel the companion of the defendant threw a shoe at him.—P.C. Corbett was informed but he refused to lock the men LID as they were not drunk and advised Mr. Partridge to summons them. This was done but the defendant did not appear. He surrendered himself to the rolice that morning.—Mr. Partridge corroborated Mr. Arowne's statement.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 403. and costs or one month's imprisonment.—Wm. Bayley, fruiterer, was charged with assault'ng lii3 wife on the 21st inst.—MN. A. Bayley deposed that on Worines,clay night the de- fendant thrashed her. He ~truck her on the i(le, and</p>
        <p>on the face, and also turned her out She hld nrt spun him since until that morning. Tie took £4 from her. He was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days' hard labour. THEFT OF A PAIR OF TROTTFERS. —Anna Scott. a aranger in the town, was charged with Ftealinz from the back yard of Thomas Kilduff, Buttermarket-st., man's pair of trousers. yalur 2s. between Tuesday and Wednesday.—The Chief Constable stated that the com- plainant lupt a shop in Buttermarket-st. She washed the pair of trousers produced on Tuesday, and hung them in the hack yard to dry. She went to look at them some time afterwards, and found they had gone. Pri- soner went into Mr. Brouchemite's lodging-house on</p>
        <p>Tuesday night and offered to sell him the pair of trou- sers, but the offer was declined and the police informed. They were found to be the property of the complainant. —The prisoner said she did not steal them but had them given to her. She was sent to gaol for 14 days. DRUNK.—For being drunk in Wilson Patten-st. at 12.30 that morning J. Brown was fined ss. and costs. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES —Mr. G. Riley prosecuted In following cases of irregular attendance at school : David Boyes, forgeman, 11 Lilford-st., ss. ;Jhn Coyne, labourer, Glass House Row. 5.; James Hennigan, labourer, 12 Buckley-st.. ss. ; Thomas Bradshaw, forge- man, 23 Lilford-et., as. 81. ; JO9. Houghton, forgeman, 86 Winwick-road, 55.; George Battams, Ship Canal labourer, 30 Hind-st. , ss. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Messrs. T. Birchall, J. W. Hayward. and S. Wright. KICKING A WOMAN.—Thomas Mitchell. a yeung man residing at 16, Hill-st., was charged with being drunk on Saturday niaht, and alsa with assaulting a woman named Kate Hinds. Inspector gpinks stated that at 9.50 on Saturday night he was on duty in plain clothes wken he heard screams coming from the direction of and on proceeding there he saw the Drisoner rush out of the house and kick a woman named Hinds. The prisoner had a knife in his hand at the time. Wit- peas and P.C. Dean took the man into custody. The man was drunk at the time, and the woman was lame from the effects of the kick. P.C.Dean corroborated,and said the woman Hinds went in to the prisoner's house to quieten him, as lie was quarrelling with his sister. Bate Hinds said the prisoner was wild with drink, and did not know what he was doing. She did not wish to say anything against the prisoner. but admitted that he kicked her. The magistrates imposed a fine of 104. and costs (18s. 6d.) or 14 days' imprisonment. Dxcecx AND DLSORDEHLY.—CAtherine Ryan, a stranger in the town,was fined ss.and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Sankey-st.,on Saturday night. P.C.Loxham proved the case.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. —Before Mr. John Geddes (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Owen.</p>
        <p>DIrCNIC AND Drso-n-n-n.riLy-:-.--GeOre Dixan was fined 2.3. 6d. including costs, for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-st., the previous morning. AN AnscoNnErt.—Samuel Wilkinson was charged with running away from the borough of Warrington on the 14th August, 1393, leaving his wife and Fix children chargeable to the Nirrrington Union. The prisoner pleaded guilty. Mr. Shearer stated that the prisoner's wife and six children were admitted t, the workhouse on the 14th of August, 1393. They had all been charge- able to the union up to the present time, with the ex- ception of one child, which died on the lEth February. A warrant was issued for the prisoner's' apprehension, who was apprehended at %Vest Hartlepool. The cost to the union amounted to over £5O for maintenance, and £2 to bring the prisoner to Warrington. He was a boiler maker, and when in work could earn e•wd wages. He had deserted his wife and children two years ago at West Hartlepool, and it seemed a case where the man was determined not to support his wife and children.—</p>
        <p>The prisoner said it was owing to drink, and losing his situation.—The magistrates committed him to gaol for two months' hard labour. MUSIC LICENCE.-Mr. Mayhew applied for and was ranted a further fortnight's licence for singing at the auction tent in the Fairground.</p>
        <p>TIIURSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair), Coubeillor James Fairclough and Major Ridgway. DRUNK.—Charlotte Macdonald, a married woman who said she belonged to Liverpool was charged with being drunk in Bank-st. She was discharged on promising to leave the town. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—John Turner, a stranger in the town was committed to gaol for seven days' hard labour for being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-st. at 12.5 that morning. A DESERTER.—ThORIR4 Leonard surrendered himself the previous day at the Ortord Barracks as a deserter from the Duke of Cornwall's Light Tnfantry since Octo- ber, 1891. It was found that the prisoner was gazetted as being a deserter at that time, and the magistrates committed him to Strangeways gaol to await an escort. ARRESTED ON SUSPICION.—John H. Wheldon, serving; in the Militia was charged with being found in the pos- session of a man's overcoat and not giving a satisfactory account of the same.—P.C. Sankey deposed to seeing the prisoner wearing the overcoat produced on the top of his uniform the previous night. When asked as to how he came to be in possession of it he made several different statements. He was drunk at the time.—Sorgeant Stringfellow from the Barracks said it was an offence to wear a coat over the uniform.—lnspector Spinks said he asked the prisoner that morning when he was sober about the coat and another coat and vest left with Mr. Allen at the Pelican Inn. The prisoner said he pledged them in Liverpol when he joined the Militia a short time ago and a friend of his had brought them over for him.—The prisoner told the magistrates that he was going to pledge the coat in order to " back " a horse for the National.—The magistrates discharged the prisoner, leaving the military authorities to deal with him on the offence of wearing the coat.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.—Be fore Alderman Wright (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts.</p>
        <p>LODGING HOUSE KEEPER FlNED.—Richard Tickle, lodging house keeper, Turner-street, was charged that on March 21st, he neglected to clean the scullery floor in the forenoon, also with neglecting to have the bed clothes thoroughly cleansed, and with neglect to not take away the liquid filth before nine o'clock in the fore- noon. Inspector Norman proved the case, and the de- fendant was finect 103. and cost and a cmviction re- corded against him. A Womiates QUARREL.—Jane Ellen Davies, Water-st was summoned by Elizabeth Leigh, Pirrnoint-street, on the charge of assaulting her on March 22nd. The com- plainant said that she was sent for by Mrs. Bag,uley,and on going to the house she met the defendant, who at, once accused her of stealing some gloves. This caused some words to be passed, and Mrs. Davies at length gave her a blow on the ear.—The defendant and Mrs. Baguley each denied that any blow was struck, and the magistrates dismissed the case. ATTF3IPT AT FRAUDULENT ENLISTMENT.—BeIIiAMin Wright, Manchester, was charged with atte:npting to fraudulently enlist at the barracks on the previous day, although he had been previously discharged with ignom- iny from the South Lancashire Regiment. The prisoner who pleaded guilty said that he had just come out of gaol in Liverp-)ol, and was on his way to Manchester. He wished to get a night's lodgings, and told the guard at the gates that he wished to enlist, so that he could get inside. He had no intention to enlist. Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month. OCCASIONAL LICENCE. —Mr. Hankinson, Bridge-street, was granted an occasional licence to sell at the Conser- vative Club, on April 2nd on the occasion of a ball.</p>
      </div>
