      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WIDNES PiLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. H. Wade-Deacon.</head>
        <p>WIDNES PiLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. H. Wade-Deacon.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—Maria Bate was fined 23. 61. and costs for this offence.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON; THE POLICE.—Mary Fildes was charged with assaulting the police. Ai only one ma;ist rate was present, the case was adjourned to Thursday, defendant being bound over to appear on that day. firmaxiNG THE PEACE.—Joseph Killallee was charged with this offence and was bound over to keep the peace for six months or to go to gaol for seven days. THEFT OF CLOTH.—Williaill Millar, James Millar, George Millar, John Forster and Thomas Lowe, were charged with stealing a quantity of cloth and other arti- cles from a railway siding at Rainhill. The priso errs. were remanded until April 3rd to enable further inquir- ies to be made.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—Thomas Hardy, and John James Stranger, were charged with begging. P.C. Bamforth stated that he watched the prisoners for a great length of time on Saturday. and saw them go into several shops. When searched S:ran«,-er was found to have in his possession is. Bd. in coppers, and Hardy 7d. in coppers. The pris- oners were each sentenced to seven days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICF COURT. WEnNEsDAY.—Before Messrs. J. BrUnclritt (in the chair), and P. Sp-akniAn.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN BOATMAN AND HIS EMPLOYE EC. —Peter Johnlam, of Oilbert•street, Runcorn, waq charged with stealing 10s., the moneys of Richard Abel, canal boat owner, of Runcorn, on the 26'h March .—Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Joseph Burton, solidi' or, appeared for the prosecutor.—Mr. Richard Abel stated that the prisoner bad been in his employ as a horse driver on the Widnes canal between that place and z..;t. Helens. On Eastcr Monday, he gave into his charge a nay mare with instructions to take it to a Warrington s,laugliterer and sell it. Sufficient money was given to prisoner to go to the town named, and also to return. He was further told to brim, back with him two horses from Widnes. Instead of bringing the horses himself he sent another man with them. Prisoner entered witness's office on the 27th inst, and in reply to a question con- cerning the whereabouts of the horse said lie had sold it and had spent the money. The amount for which he sold the animal he did not state. The prisoner had not been authorized in this way.—Evidence was given by Samuel Hough, employed by the prosecwor and by P.C. Wylie, the latter proving the prisoner's apprehension.— Johnson admitted having sold the horse, hut contended that he was not guilty of theft. He had spent 10s. on the understanding that it would be stopped out of his wages at the end of the weelc.—The magistrates refil“,(l to take this statement as an excuse, and fined him 20s. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FIIODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FIIODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. A. Thomas and J. Handley. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence Satnuel Dimelow, Samuel Spruce, and Wm. Johnson were respec- tivelv tied 55.. 2s. 6d., and 55., and cost&amp; CONTRAVENTION OF THE RABIES ORDER.—TiIoS. Yowl was ordered to pay the costs for allowing', his do z t,o he at larze in Frodsham, on the 20th March, without a KEEPINr: A DOG WITHOUT A LICENSE.—AIicP Nixon was found guilty of this charge on the 15th inst., and was fined 6d. and costs.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS ON LICENSED PREMISEs.—Joseph 014,4 pleaded not guilty to an offence of this kind on the 24th February, at the Weaver Tavern, Frod,zharn Evidence in support of the charze was tendered by Sergt. Thoma.s, who said that he found the defendant sittinr apparently asleep in an intoxicated state in the taproom, on the afternoon of the day already named. On beim, questioned, Mrs.Faulkner,the landlady of the houge,t4-1 witness that defendant camein the worse for liqnor,and asked for drink, but was refused. He also declined to leave the house when requested.—Other tes,imony hav- ing been given, the defendant was fined 54., or seven days' hard labour.—George Okell was found on_iilty of similar conduct at the Bull's Head, Frodshain on the 28'11 February. The circumstances of the case were almost identical with those of the previous one,and having heard the depositions, the defendant was fined 55., or seven days' hard labour, as an alternative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mesr.q.J. Handley (in the chair),and</p>
        <p>F. J. Norman.</p>
        <p>SELECTION OF OVERSEERS FOR THE TOWNSIIIPS.—The rnag,istrates appointed Ulm following gentlemen as over- seers of the townships named:—Aston-by-Sutton.Messrs. Wm. Wilkinson and George Percival ; Aston Grauge, Messrs. Thomas Challoner and Robert Wade ; Clifton, Messrs. Joseph Stokes and Samuel R-Nlands ; Norton, Messrs. Thomas Percival and Clement Baguley ; Halton, Messrs. Thomas Wright, and Hugh Rowe ; Runcorn, Messrs. Edwin Imison and Samuel Taylor ; Stockham, Messrs. J. W. Baguley, and James Clarke ; Sutton, Messrs. Wm. Lowe and John Bate; Weston, Messrs. Edward Aaron and Thomas Ford senior. DISORDERLY DEVOTEES OF BACCHUS.—John Howard was tined 54. for being drunk and disorderly in Church- at., on the 17th inst.—John Edwards was fined 7.4. 61., for a similar afence in Weston-road, Runcorn, on the same date. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>T-HE COST or KEEPING Doos.—Thomas Lightfoot was mulcted in a fine of ss. and costs amounting 9s 6d., for keeping a dog without aceicense on the 13th Marc,ll at Aston-by-Sutton.—James Hier was ordered to par 55. cests for allowing his dog to wander at large without either a muzzle or a collar on the 20th March. THE ALLEGED THEFT OF BOOTS BY GIRLS AT RUNCORN. —Mary Bradbury (15). Margaret Harrison (14), and Beatrice Burgess (12) were charged with stealinc, boots to the value of Bs. 6d. from the shop of John Peebles, Church-st., Runcorn, on the 21st inst.—Evidence was given by the prosecutor to the effect that the defendants visited his shop on the afternoon of the day named and made purchases. After they had gone the boots were missed. He did not see the defendants take the boots. Subsequent to his giving information to the police the defendants called upon him and wished to make arrange- ments to pay.—All three defendants pleaded not guilty. —The Bench ultimately discharged the girls with a cau tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TCESDAY.—Before Mr. W. C. Jones (,-.llairtnan), Messrs. W. Long, C. M4rson and H. Nield.</p>
        <p>STAGE PLAYS.—Sydney Mobbetly applied for a licence to perform some plays at Stretton National schools for two nights. The application was granted. TRANSORMINO DOG OWNERS.—Thomas Hall, Apnle- ton, for having a dog without a licence on the 231 February was fined in the costs.—Robett Buchanan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</p>
        <p>Bostock-st., Steckton Heath was similarly dealt with fw the same offeace on the 24th February. Thomas Denning, Bartington, was also ordered to pay the costs for allowing his dog to be ari large on the Bth March. REFUSINti TO QUlT.—Robert Griffiths, Stockton Heath was charged with refusing to quit the licensed premises of the Mulberry Tree Inn, on the 2nd March. • P.C. Brew said on the date named, he was called to the Mulberry Tree Inn. The defendant was there and was using very had language to the landlady. He also used bad' language to witness and refused to leave the house. The defendant said he was Sorry for what he had done. He was fined 2s. 61. including costs. DRUNK AND DISORDF.RLY.--For this offence at Grap- penhall on the 24th February, Patrick Conner, green- grocer, Latchford, was fined 2s. 6J. including costs. P.C. Millington proved the case. DRIVERS IN TROUBLE.—George E. Davidson, Warring- ton, was fined 53. including costs for being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and grocer's cart at Newton on the 17th March.—John Arnold, carter for Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Limited was charged with driving two horses furiously at:Stretton at the 3rd March. P.C. Bratt proved the casf.. The defendant was fined 53. and c4sts.</p>
        <p>_ _ APPOINTMENT OF OVERSEERS--The following appoint- ments of overseers were confirmed : Acton Grange,Peter Hall and Thomas Basnett ; ttntrobus, Burchall Frith,and Peter Percival ; Appleton, Henry Eyes and Wm. Barber; Bartington. James Lowe and Peter Mounfield ; Dares- bury, Charles Win. Phillips and John Elison ; Dutton, George Kinsey and John Kinsey ; Grappenhall, Alan Lister Kaye and Richard Boardman ; Latchford, John Lunt and James Strange; Kekewich, Samuel Done and John Done ; Hatton, Henry Mawdsley and James Hat- ton ; Little Leigh, Daniel Massey and Ralph Francis Horton ; Moore, Henry Middleton and Peter Gleave ; Newton-by-Daresbury, Philip Darbyshire and Samuel Basnett : Preston n'th Hill, Daniel Basnett and James Cowap ; S,venoaks, Adolphus Wilkinson and Joseph Wilkinson ; Stretton, John Rutter and Peter Leather : Walton Inferior,Thomas Smith Burgess and Henry Dodd: Walton Superior, PettT Shearer and Alfred Broady : Whitley Inferior, Frank n. Acton and George Mounfield: Whitley Superior, Herbert Samuel Wright and Samuel Cartwright.</p>
        <p>sentative one. Tho repeated losses sustained by Kendal, made it impossible for them to take a full team, and the Saints were without Foulkes, Gladwin, Rimmer, and Winstanl,y, who were suffering from injuries. The game was very one-sided, the St. Helens men having matters all their own way. Sudlow and Doherty scored three tries each, Whiteley two, and Cross, Ashcroft, Ap- pleton, and Gill one each. 66%l kicking was altogether off, only one try being improved by Sudllow. Final result :—St. Helens, 1 goal 11 tries (38 points), Ken- dal nil. ST. HF.LRNS V. BARTON.—These teams met on Saturday at St. Helens, before 2,000 spectators. The home team were without Foulkes, who had his leg injured in the Warrington naatch on Friday,and Hopley filled his place. St. Helens lost the toss, and Cross started against a strong sun. The visitors pressed at the commencement, Fish almost getting over. As the game progressed the Saints showed more combination, and Jones scored, Graham kicking a goal. Fish was injured on the neck and retired. Half time score :—St. Helens 1 goal, Bar- ton nil. Barton got a dropped goal in the second half. Final result :—St. Helens 5 points, Barton 4 points. WARRINGTON EXCELSIORS V. PORT SUNLIGHT.—These, teams met on the Excelsior's ground. The home team started, and from some loose play Longmore ran in Gibson improving. Thornton soon after ran in grandly, Gibson again converting. Warrington seemed to have the play to themselves, and Thornton again scored, Gib- son kicking his third goal. In the second half Sunlight played uri better, but Woods scored again for Warring- ton, and the Excelsiors won by 3 goals 1 try, to Port Sunlight nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. WIDNES (" A " rEAMS).—The junior home team travelled over the water on Saturday to play their old opponents the youthful Widnesians on the Lower- house ground. Play was of a most exciting and rough nature, which resulted in Beesley,a Widnes forward get- ting his collar bone broken. Though the homesters ap- peared to have the best of the play, they were unable to score in the first half. Eventually the contest ended in a victory for Widnes by one goal to nil. This is the first defeat the juniors have sustained this season. •</p>
        <p>NEWPORT V. RUNCORN.—PIayed at Newport, in brilli- ant weather, on Monday, and in the presence of 7,000 to 8,000 spectators. In the first half, Gregory scored for Runcorn, and IA atts ran in for Newport. At half-time the score was even. During the second half, Runcorn pressed for a long time, but failed to score. A few minutes before the end Renshaw and Rowley scored, Gould converting one. Result :—Newport 1 goal 2 tries, Runcorn 1 try. RUNCORN RECREATION T. ALTRINCHAI.I.—This match was played at Altrincham on Easter Monday and resulted in a victory for Altrincham, by one goal, four tries to nothing. During the first half the Recs dis- played capital form, and several times were extremely dangerous. Towards the end of the game however, the homesters had the best of the play. RUNCORN V. GLOUCESTER.—An encounter took place on Easter Tuesday between these teams at Gloucester, and resulted in a defeat for the visitors by eight points to four.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MINERS' MEETING AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>MINERS' MEETING AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ADDRESSES BY MR. S. WOODS AND MR. T. GLOVER.</p>
        <p>The second annual social gathering in connection with the Steadlas.t Lodge of the Miners' Society was held at King's Cocoa-rooms, St. Helens, on Sat- urday night. Mr. Thomas Glover, J.P.,miners' agent for St. Helens district, presided. and moved the following resolution : —' That this meeting of St. Helens miners is glad to see the progress made with the Miners Eight Hours Bill ; we strongly condemn the action of the House of Lords in destroying the E:nployers' Liability Bill, and we fur- ther believe that the only security of the working cl:".s.s;-s is a strong and powerful union." He said there had never been a bill which had made such progress as the Eight Hours Bill. When it was first brought into Par- liament there were very few who were willing to sup- port it, but they had convinced members that the working classes were in earnest, and had shown that the measure was a necessity. Mr. Jas. Atherton seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woods, M.P., who supported the ressilution, was loudly 'applauded. He said that was a small sec- tion of a large district which he represented. They were trying to break up those little divisions which divided one district from another, and have one consoli- dated body of working men from one end of the coun- try to the other. Last year they determined they would never work again at the terms proposed by colliery owners. They fought a terrible battle and came out without disgrace, with the contention that wages were low enough (a voice : "They have come down since") Unfortunately that was the case. What employers could not get by a -..,eneral demand they were getting by insi- dious private reductions. They -fought a battle in the face of keen suffering and dire distress ; there never was such an event in history, and it brought forth that sudden outburst of generosity which was so manifested by the public of the country in supporting the miners during the sixteen months they were out. One of the first points that had to be decided by the Conciliation Board was the question of a minimum wage, below which wages should .not go. What he (Mr. Woods) defined as a living wage was what they often sang . .</p>
        <p>Eight hours work, eight hours play, - Eight hours sleep, and eight shillings a day (laughter and loud applause). If the Bishop of Man- chester wanted a true definition of what the living wage was he it in those twe lines. The second point to be consider: d was that if the miners' wages were regulated by contract prices then the miners should have a voice lin the fixing of prices. They might be compelled, but they would never willingly proceed one single inch fur- ther in the proceedings of the Conciliation Board unless those que=tions were decided. Referring to the Eight . Hours Bill, he said this year would see it s.ttled in the House of Commons. He was not azing to say about the other place, because they had a knack of throwing out anything that was passed in the House of Commons— (a voice: " Down with them ")—hut he would be a ereat deal surprised if the country did not take drastic m-asures to throw them out, (Applause.) The com- plaint at present in the coal trade was short time and large stack 4 of coal. There was no earthly power to prevent it, because they had 100,000 more men in the mines than the demand required. He -believed the adopti.n of he Eight Hours Bill would prevent the pr .veet short time, and also allow workin g men more tire for recreation. The resolu ion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>I;ESPITE OF THE- CONDEMNED S'l.</p>
        <p>LLENS COLLIERS.</p>
        <p>General satisfaction was felt in S. Helens on Monday evening on receipt of telegrams from the Home Office stating that the SJcretary of State had advised Her Majesty to respite the capital sentence passed at the Liverpool Assiz,.s upon three colliers, Leahey, Carney, and Riley, for the murder of Police Constable Gordon a 7, St. Helens, with a view to commutation of the sentence. Petitions for reprieve have been signed by over six thousand persons. Announcement of the respite in local places of amusement on Mond ty night elicited enthusi- astic cheers.</p>
        <p>The formal letter of the Home Secretary, intimating that her Majesty had been advised to exert the Royal prerogative, was received at Walton gaol on Tuesday afternoon, and was promptly conveyed to the prisoners by the governor. Needless to say, the men were over- joyed by the intimation that they would not he called upon to undergo the death penalty. When the feelire, of exultation at their liberation from the sentence of the gallows had somewhat suh4id-d, the men made anxiotel inquiries as to the fate which wa in store for them, het on tl‘is point the governor was enable to give them any definite information. The men will in due coorie he re- moved from Walton, aria taken at an early hour in the morning from P-e.ten .Enrictiee or Walton Stati •ti down to Liverpool et. Edge Hill, and be placed in a train lor the South. Their destination will be either Pentonville, Portland or Dlrtmoor, unless the Home Secretary in the meantime sees fir, to commute their sentence into such a short ter- ii that they w;11 no placed in one of the prisons where the worst class of long-senti-nce men are not to he found. It is feared that the fate of the unfortunate men will he a considerable period of penal servitude, because there is a somewhat had record against them. The var4ms petitions, containing, it is estimated, shout 10,000 signatures, including those of the Bishop ,ofLivernool and a nember of clergy, will be forwarded to the Home Secretary on Wednesday morning. As the tofficial notification of a respite has been made, the peti- ion will be accompanied by a request that the Royal clemency may be exercised in the direction of a moder- ate term of imprisonment. A juryman writes so Mr. H. L. Riley :—" I cannot get from my mind an extract from a letter which was in the " Liverpool Echo" on Thursday last, in which one of the jurymen states that he was r.f opinion that. the action, of the men was not the immediate cause of death, or words to that effect, and that if the judge had not sum- med up against the men in favour of a verdict of murder, the jury would not have returned the verdict they did, hut even then it was with the stringent recommendation to mercy. I have been juryman at the assizes on three or four occasions during the last fifteen years, and un- fortunately it has heen my lot to be on both murder and manslaughter cases, and I think this, that if theie poor fellows are executed it Is leg,ali,ed murder, and if they are only commuted to penal servitude for life it is a disgrace to a civilised country, for my opinion is that the chances were that he was liable to die from the same thing even after the excitement of an ordinary street row."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BYE-ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE BYE-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The result of the nolling in 14,-ith Burghs was declared on Tuesday. 111,r. Munro-Ferv,uson, who sought re-elec- tion on his acceptance of office as a Junior Lord of the Treasury, was returned.the figures being—R. C. Munro- Fer.uson (Liberal), 5.823 ; W. A. Bell (Conservative), 4,692 ; Liberal majority, 1,131. The polling. for the election of a member of Parlia- ment for the flawick Burohs took place on Tuesdar. The vacancy was caused by Mr. Thomas Shaw's acceptance of office as Solicitor General for Scotland. Mr. Shaw wag opposed by Mr. Fullarton, Q.C., Unionist. The conteo- resulted in the victory of the Libera' candidate by 617 votes,the figures being—Mr. .Shaw (Liberal), 3,203 ; Mr. Fullarton (Unionist), 2,556.</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION AND COI-MIL—SO eness and dryness. tieltling and irritation,indlicing cough and affecting the voice For these symptoms use Epps's Glycerine .Tujubes. In con- tact with t'e glands at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, the Glycerine in these agreeable confections becomes actively healing. Sold only in boxe.. 'PA.. tins is. 11d.. laoelled " JAMES EPPS &amp; Co.. Ltd.. Homoeopathic. Chemist 3, T.Andon," Dr. Moore. in his work nn 'lose anti Throat Disea4es," says "Tie Glycerine Jujub. eprnpssred by James Epps and Co.. are of tintionb`ed service as a cut.ll- - or palliatiYe agent." NA, i•e Dr. Gordon Holmes. Senior Physician to the Municipa' Tht o a and Ear Intirma^y,writps: " After an extended tn si. I hav, found your Glycerine Jujnbes of considerable be Irilt in vlmost all forms of thma disease."—Adrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>*.* Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsev-street. Warrington, not later t an ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>GEORGE G. BUSSEY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey, Golf, and requisites for Sports, Games, and Gymnasia. Catalogue may bs had on application to the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London, S.E. BEST &lt;CE.l3&lt;t. BRAND.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. MANCBESTER.</p>
        <p>Warrleaeoat Irat team suffered an unexpected defeat in their engagement with Manchester on Saturday. They journeyed to Manchester via the Ship Canal on the steamer Water Lily. The team however, were not as complete as it has been for some time, as Broady, Bar- ber, Potts, Jolley, Dakin and James •Bate were.away For Manchester, one player Mullineux had made a spe- cial journey from London. The following were the teams :—Warrington : J. Boscow, back ; F. Heesom, J. T. Massey, ,J. W. Evans, and P. Carey, threequarter backs ;John Bate (captain), and W. Nevins, half-backs; J. T. Thorniley, J. Berry, E. Bailey, J. Taylor, %V. Houghton, J. Fairhurst, J. Holmes, and W. Handles,for- wards. Manceester : H. Vichoff, back ;C. L. Jones, T. C. Rogerson, F. A. Jones, and W. B. Steel, threequarter backs; M. Mullineux, and S. Craven, half-backs ; G. Cameron, K. I'. Birley, A. G. Hunter, J. E. Louden, E. W. Broadbent, J. %V. Needham, E. R. Foy, and J. M`Monies, forwards. Referee, Mr. E. Beswick. Touch- line judges : Mr. G. P. Wincey, Manchester, and Mr. H. Ashton, Warrington, The game opened quiet, and nlething of moment ' occarred until Nevins, Evans, and Massey passed well, and the visitors gave Vichoff trouble. In a couple of minutes from the commencement both Taylor and Hee- som were almost hailed scorers,the players having the ill- luck to ground the wrong side of the line. Rogerson again relieved, only to see his career stayed by lleesom, whilst Steel tried to get threugh, hut failed to account for Massey. Berry and Holmes dribbled splendidly. whilst Eloughton from a line out got finely away,but a bad finish was put to his effort. Carey was next penalized,but he made amends a moment later, for,dribbling through the oppo- sition, he was only just beaten for possession in the race over the line. Taylor made a bold bid soon after the drop out, and Vichoff again saved. Birley next dribbled well for Manchester. and a fine all-round bit of passing saw Manchester setting up a vigorous attack,another spell of . passing on the call of half time ending in Jones scoring . the only tangible point of this half. Nevins was hurt in the second half, whilst saving a dangerous rush,and was forced to retire for a considerable period, higplace being taken by Berry. With 14 men Warringtore first got an insight of their opponents' quarters, but there was no finish to the display, and the local front rank.headed by . Cam,-roe, secended by good passing by Mullineux and Steel, were nipped in the bud by Massey. Manchester next hid a fine openine. but Evans spoiled the essay, and robbing his ,ipoorwrits lie found touch near halfway. tesey next eui,ted well.wfrlst Carey got past all oppo- sition. hut a failure to take the oval at the first time of asking robbed him ef the c,hance,and C.L.Jenes tackled. Manchester saved a minute later, and Nevins then re- turned. Handles met the drip out,and Berry was hauled down tinder the posts, but vigorous play by Manchester quickly changed the venue, and the locals kept up the itt'ai.k until the close without recording anything sub- stantial. Final score :—Manchester, one try (3 points) ; ‘Varringtore, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. LANCASTER.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. LANCASTER.</p>
        <p>A large gate assembled on the Wilderspool ground on Easter Monday to witness the match between these two teams. With both sides about equally represented, an even game was anticipated, seeing that the Lancaster men accounted for our local team on December 23rd, though on that occasion the Northerners had the assis- tance of several players frown Cambridge and Oxf ard Universities. Taylor started on behalf of Warrington, and although the play was even in the opening stages, the locals eventually showed superior combination, and after five minutes had elapsed, Lancaster were forced tq adopt defensive tactics. Smart passing by John Bate, Evans, and Nevins was frustrated by Cowell, and the burly visitors got the better of a hot sczimmaus, Wilson leading the way to the centre flag. The Warringconians however, attacked again, and though Bleasdale slightly checked the ne ward movement, Berry was almost under the posts from one forward rush. They got the sphere away, only to see Nevins find touch near the corner flag, and then ensued a round of passing by Evans, James Bate, Carey, and Nevins, Leaks saving accur- ately near the line. Again did Bleasdale clear the invaders, and although Carey and Massey ran well, Thorniley and Dakin tested Leaks again,the Lancastrian coming out:of the ordeal with credit. Lancaster were here penalized, but Taylor only made a poor bid at goal, and Kitchen returned, a prorupt response by Nevins sending up a minor. The locals continued to have the best of it, and a fine run by Carey was negatived by Matthews,and a minute later the Warrington forwards swept down in a body, and Leaks having a kick charged, an easy try was lost through over • eas.,erne'ss. Following this hard luck came another brilliant exposition by the home backs, wad the forwards seconding the effort in true stele,the oval was taken over and Dakin scored far out, which point remained unimproved. Leading by a try, Warringtnn renewed the attack Soon after re-opening the second half,and fine play by Heesom, Nevins, and Evans severely menaced the Lancaster defence. Leake essayed a couple of shots at goal from half way without success, whilst Massey, in following up a kick, Was deliberatsly fnuled,but the appeal fora tangible score was overruled. However,the free kick caused Warrington to attack, and the venture resulted in Houghton scoring near the posts. This was followed by another raid on the Lancaster goal, and Taylor picking up smartly, scored far out, the kick at goal, like its predecessors, being a failure. The War- rinEttonians were within an ace of scoring repeatedly, and it was not until the close that Lancaster took ad- vantage of some kicking, and Rogers scored easily, an interesting game ending in a win for the 'Warringtonians by three tries to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON '6 A' V. LANCASTER " A."—The War- ' rineten " A " team journeyed to Lancaster to play the "A "team of that town. The weather was fine and there was a moderate attendance of spectators. Randles ' commenced operations for the visitors, who at once pressed. A neat passing movement by Foster, Mann ioe, and Gibson saw Clark tackled near the line, and a charge by Cross saw toe !atter run in under the pest, the s imp player failing to improve. The home team Nil,' had a turn, and the leather travelled to Boscow, whe reterned in capital style. Lawless, jun.. was next eorespireoue with a nice run and kick, and after Thorn- ton had led a dribble, Randles looked like scoring, but wee Tweed lin near the line. Gibson put in some good work, ejerli deserved better success, but Randles on getting heed threw nicety to Cross, who transferred to La %%lees, and this player kicked neatly over his op- ponent's head, and Cross racing up secured and ran in his eecond try, Lawless missing an easy place. At half time the visiters led by two tries to nil. Resuming, Warrineton again pressed, and Mannion had hard lines on two occasions. However, Lawless passed to Gib- son, who ran in, Foster kicking a goal. From the kick out Boscow was celled on to defend his lines, which he did with credit. Foster secured frem the scrimmage, and el-riding his opposing halves passed neatly to Law- less, jun., who scored. Gibson converting. The visitors continue] to press. and tleetly ran out winners be two goals two tries to Lanca ter RUNCORN v. WIDNES.—The rivalry existing between these local clubs is very well-known, consequently when it was announced that a oonteet was; to take place between them, predictions were very numerous that there would be a huge "gate." These prophecies proved correct. The Runcornians have so often defeated their neighbours across the "fish pond" that no small amount of surprise would be expressed were the tables to he turned brit for once. Though the homesters have fre- quently been victorious the teams as they stood on Sat- urday were well maeche I, and this was made very apparent adieu play commenced. In the first half the home team had the sun in their faces so were, to a great extent, at a disadvantage. Operations were of a very even nature, and notwithstanding the fine form of both teams,neither could score so that at the terval there was a clean sheet on both sides. In the second half play was a little more one-sided,being in favour of Runcorn. Their opponents were very stub- ' helm however, and often threatened to score. Consider- able excitement prevailed both on the part of the spec- tators and the players, the latter being sometimes ap- parently unable to control themselves. One of the visi- tors' half-haeks (Barber) was injured and forced to re- tire which somewhat weakened the defence of the Wid- neeians. Shortly afterwards, a very warm tnAsle took piece out of which the homesters came the victors by goal. Both teams now endeavoured to improve, but without sunceee, the game ending :—Runcorn one goal; Widnes nothing. ST. EIPLES9 RF.cgRATIoN V. LEIOI4.—At Leigh on Satur- day, before a good attendance. The Recs., who were short of Crossley, Ellison, Hughes, Pilkington, Fildes, and Dolan, started, and Leigh was penalised. Seddon almost snoring. The Rees. preesed. Coop relieved to centre, and two minors fell to the RPCSI. Leigh dribbled to the Rees.' territory, and passing between Harper and Orford gained ground. Anderton mate a good run for Leigh, hut wa, brought down by Seddon. Molyneux relieved to centre.Pilkineton tackled spleedidlyeand pas- sing between Pilkineton. Potts, and Reynolds almost re- sulted in a try. Scrimmaging ensued, the Recs, having slightly the best of the pack. Trom was conspicuous with his neat running and kicking. The Rens. were penalised. and Arderton dropped a goal. The Recs. re- started. and Leigh were forced to touch down. Even play followed up to helf time, when the score stood : Leigh 1 penalty goal, St. Helens Recs. nil. In the second half Leigh Pressed almost throughout, but with- mit suceess. Final result :—Leigh 1 penalty goal, St. Helens Recreation nil.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. Ksxue.t..-11,,ndal visited the Knowsley- road ground, St. Helens, otiMonday, to play the return match with the town club. The game was not a rem-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN "A " TRAM V. ST. ANNE'S, BIRKENHEAD.— This was a match which created a large amount of inter- est and exeitement on Monday, owing to the fact that the St. Anne's are endeavouring to secure the champion- ship of the League, and the result of this fixture prac- tically decides it. Runcorn, since their last encounter with the Birkenhead men, which resulted in a draw, have defeated all their opponents, but St. Anne's were unable to do more than draw with New Brighton,so that when the two teams met on Monday at Runcorn, the visitors were one point behind. The home team was comparatively weak, six of their men being away at Newport. The weather was beautifully fine and there was a very large "gate." From the commencement of hostilities the home team started to press very strongly. A capital defence, however, was sustained by the visi- tors making it almost impossible to score. At length St. Anne's were penalized for picking out, and amid the wildest excitement Lightfoot kicked a beautiful goal. Some dashing play was witnessed until the call of half- time, when the score was one penalty goal to nil, in favour of Runcorn. A short time after the re-start a good opportunity was missed by Williamson through his apparent eagerness to score. The St. Anne's men then became rather dangerous, but eventually the leather was shifted and Webbe was responsible for a try. Runcorn continued to press and Woodward placed two tries in succession. Towards the finish of the game Williamson secured a drop goal in fine style and was loudly applauded by the spectators, who were wound up to a very high pitch of excitement. At the close of play the score was :—Runcorn two goals, three tries to nothing.</p>
        <p>LOCAL TOWNSHIP MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>STOCKEIAM.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the ratepayers was held on Monday evening at Mr. Baguley's farmhouse,Stockham, under the presidency of that gentleman. The only busi- ness transacted was the nomination of Messrs. Pau- ley, Clarke, and .Nickson as overseers for the ensuing year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PENKETH 19. HOLY TRIN ITY,PEASLEY CROSS. —The return fixture between these clubs came off at Peasley Cross on Saturday in be tutiftil weather. The visitors were short of five of their regular players, Powell who at one time played for the defunct Warrington A.F.C., assisted the visitors and played a good game throughout. Dumbill started for the visitors with the sun in their faces, ply was mostly in the home quarters; Jackson scored for the visitors with a long shot. Shortly afterwards Powell added a second goal, and hall-time arrived without any addition to the score. Restarting the game was of a slow nature. A good run by Bowyer ended in Davies scoring. The visitors now had a look in and forced a couple of corners which proved futile. Powell to ,k play to the home quarters, their backs relieved, Dyi;os met the kick and scored a grand goal. The rest of ho game was anything but interesting and at the call of time Penketh were declared winners by four goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons reassembled on Thursday after the Easter recess. The Attorney General brought in a bill to provide for carrying into effect the award under the Behring Sea Arhicrati m, and Mr. Shaw- Lefevre a bill to make better provision for the equalisa- tion of rates as between different parts of London. Mr. MUNDELLA introduced the Government measure to make hotter provision f.r the settlement of labour disputes. He said it differed from the bill of last session in giving an initiative to the Board of Trade in certain cases to bring about an amicable settlement of differences. Power was given to the Board of Trade to set un •boards of conciliati,in, and, where an agreement for such purpose was come to, to appoint an arbitrator. The right lion, gentleman, after a reference to the coal- trade conflict, said our industrial supremacy was no longer undisputed, and when we had to contend w,ainst fierce foreign competition we could not afford these industrial wars. A brief discussion followed and the bill was then read a first time. The second reading was provisionally fixed for Monday. On the order for Committee of Supply Mr. FIELD moved that it was desirable to recruit the first division of the Civil Service by promotion from the second. Sir J. T. HIRBERT opposed the motion, as one which would tie the hands of the Government in an important question. Mr. GOSCREN expressed a hope that the House would not interfere too much with the Executive Government in the management of its servants. Sir W. HARCOURT said the Government tura their stand on the reports of the Playfair and Ridley Commissions. To say that no man should go in the first class unless he had first been in the second would be to lower the whole character of the first division. The motion was withdrawn. Mr. HAN- BURY moved a resolution declaring that the law officers of the Crown had departed from the arrangement as to private practice made by a Treasury minute of Decem- ber, 1892. He said the minute was a deception of the public. Sir C. Russell defended himself and the Solicitor General from the reflections cast upon them and Sir %V. HARCr,URT declared that the accusation that they had departed from the spirit of the minute was unjust. The motion was negatived without a division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WHAT IS THE INK-PLANT?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS THE INK-PLANT?</p>
        <p>In Colombia there grows a plant which learned folk call CoPiaria thymifolia, but whose popular name is the ink-plant. Its juice is a ready-made ink. At first the writing looks reddish, but it becomes deep black in the course of a few hours. It will not harm steel pens; and letters written with it, if soaked in water, even fora long time, will be quite higible when dried. The plants are poisonous, however, so that this natural ink will nee r take the place of the common or finger-staining sort of daily use.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Balfour says that Lcrd Randolph Churchill is Ithe most brilliant English conversationalist of the present day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>THE LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>_ The Little Sisters of the Poor have much pleasure fa bearing their testimony as to the efficacy of Ancor:lee Porou,, Plasters. They have seen great benefit derived from them on account of the relief they so quickly give the sufferer. They find them a very soothing remedy for all sorts of pains.— T.M.T. Home for the Aged Poor, Parkfield-avenue, Birkenhead. The REV. MARK GUY PEARSE says :—" I find them a very breast plate against colds and coughs." Mr. HENRY THORNE (evangelist) says: —"I have used them for pains in the back and side arising from rheumatic and other causes, always deriving benefit from their application. They are easily applied and very comforting." Over twenty millions of Allcock's Plasters have already been sold in Great Britain, the most telling testimony to this great external, remedy. Allcock's Plasters stop all aches and pains and sprains and strains, Lumbago, Sciatica, Rheumatism, Ruptures, Bronchitis, Asthma, all affections of the Throat and Lungs, acting directly on the tissues, dilating the pores of the slun, giving instant relief.</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S PLASTERS are of great efficacy in INDIGESTION if worn over right aide of the Pit of the Stomach.</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S—Of all Chemists, Ijli.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. D. Wray presided over this annual meet- ing, which was held in the Girls' school, Halton. The overseers for last year (Messrs. Thomas Wright and Fl. Rowe) were again chosen for the coming year. Messrs. Joseph Astbury and Wm. Nixon were re-elected burley- men, and Messrs. S. Blain and S. Sanders were selected as auditors for the acco'unts of the Burial Board. The meeting closed with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>• FRODSHAM LORDSHIP.</head>
        <p>• FRODSHAM LORDSHIP.</p>
        <p>The annual township meeting was held at the close of the last week in the Boys' school, Overton, Mr. H. E. Heywood occupying the chair. The formal business hav- ing been disposed of. the Chairman said that owing to the Parish Councils Bill havin,c., passed,it would be neces- sary to elect a waywarden.—Messrs. James Percival and Henry Tilley were nominated overseers for the ensuing year. The assistant overseer was allowed £lO for the collection of the sanitary rate for the current half year, and £lO for the coming year.-The members of the Parochial Committee were appointed as follows : Messrs. R. Bate, James Percival, and G. Beech.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL. The annual ratepayers' meeting was held in the Parish school-room, on Monday evening, when there were a large number of ratepayers present. The accounts were presented and passed. Mr. A. Lister Kaye and Mr. Richard Boardman were re-elected overseers, and Mr. Bradford waywarden. Messrs. Woodcock and Jones were appointed assessors of taxes, Mr. R. Boardman and Mr. R. Warburton burlevmen, and Mr. J. J. Fairhurst parish constable. It was stated that the present would be the last meeting held under the existing system on account of the passing of the Parish Councils Bill. The waywarden was asked to support the view suggested at the last meeting of the Highway Board, to the effect that that authority should ask the County Council that it might remain in force for another three years. —This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>DUTTON.</head>
        <p>DUTTON.</p>
        <p>The annual township meeting for Dutton was held in the Board school at the end of last week, the chair being occupied by Mr. Georzo Kinsey. There were also pre- sent :—Me.srs. John Kinsey, Wm. Foulkes, J. Withen- shaw,and S.E. Stokesf—Messrs.Geerge Kinsey and John Kinsey (retiring overseers), Thomas Baxter, C.C. , Henry Miller. Joseph Withenshaw, John Yarwood, John Blain, John Tomlinson, and Wm. Moore were nominated over- seers, and Messrs. S. E. Stokes and D. Basnett as asses- sors and collectors of taxes. The burleymen appointed were Messrs. Wm.Fovrles and Joseph Withenshaw. The assistant overseer reported that the balance in hand on the 25th March, 1893, was £24 Gs. 7.1. ; received from rates during the twelve months,£l.oB7l.4s.; total,Rl.lll 14s. 7d. Expenditure : Poor. £574 ; highway, £303; Scho3l Board, £126 ; sanitary, £63; other expen.es, £25 103. 6d. ; total, £1,091 10;. 6d. ; leaving a balance in hand of £2O 4s. id. The rateable value of the t,-)wnship is £10,040 ss. Bd.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. The annual township meeting of the ratepayers of Warrington was held on Thursday morning in the par- ade room at the police station. 'Mr. John Harrison was voted to the chair and there were also present : T. Birtles, and J. L. Tunstall (assistant overseer) A. T. Smith J. Throstle, J. Smith, H. Isherwood, J. Lawton and C. Lyon.</p>
        <p>THE ACCOUNTS. Mr. J. L. Tunstall submitted the accounts from which it appeared that at the ye tr endinF! 29'h September there was a balance in hand of £2.956 75. 51.; there had been received from the rates £17.403 104. 71. and from other sources £357 ss. Bd.; the total receipts including the bal- ance brought forward, being £20,717 3s. 81. The 11- lowing payments had been made :—To the Uni0n,£5,043: County rate £1.594 ; Rural Sanitary Authority (several expenses) £77 13s. 11d.; Union school attendance Com- mittee expenses, £l7 7s. 91.; to the Borough rate £864 16s. 11d.; other expenses, £652 les. 31. There was a balance in hand to be carried forward of £3,968 43. 101. The accounts were adopted. COLLECTOR'S RE3IUNERATION. On the proposition of Mr. Isherwood, seconded by Mr. C. Lyon it was resolved that the remuneration to the collector for the past year. in respect of rates other than poor rates be at the rate of £lO for each separate rate collected.</p>
        <p>OVERSEERS OF THE Poem The names of the following gentlemen were approved of to be submitted to the Justices of the Peace, as quali- fied to act as overseers of the poor :—Messrs. John Harri- son, David Jones, Thomas Birtles, Peter Leigh, David Hooley, Thomas Grime, Richard Toft, J. B. Isaac, S. Lees, J. E. Wright, John Hatton, T. Lees, and B. Carter.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH' COUNCILS BILL. The Assistant Overseer called the attention of the meeting- to the provisions of the Local Government En"nd and Wales Act, 1894, with regard to the division of the rural area of the township into wards. He said that from the 28th November next, the urban area of Warrington would form one township and the rural area would form another and it was the duty of that meeting to say whether they would like the rural area put into wards. That area consisted of three parts which wer; quite separate from one another. There was Op. Orford ward to the North. Little Sankey to the South-West,and Arpley to the South. His suggestion was that the County Council be asked to form the township into two wards, namely Orford ward and Little Sankey ward, which would include Arpley.—Mr. Tunstall's suggestion was adopted and it was agreed to forward ots to the County Council, a resolution embodying the suggestion.</p>
        <p>_ TEM POOR RATE. in reply to Mr. Isherwood, the Assistant Overseer said that the poor rate would be increased by 4d. in the £. which would make a total poor rate of 2s. Bd. instead of 21. 4d. It was possible that the rates all round would be increased during the year and the probable total rate would be 63. Bd. The meeting terminated with the usual votes of thanks to the Chairman and to the retir- ing overseers for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FRODSTIAAL</head>
        <p>FRODSTIAAL</p>
        <p>INTOXICATED.-At the Frol9ham Police Court (In Mon- day rn,rning before Mr. Thomas, Samuel Jannion, labourer, was fined 24. 6d. and costs for being drunk in Chester main road on the 24,h inst.</p>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT —On Wednesday evening the second concert of the season in connection with the above society was held in the Town Hall, Frodsham, numerous audience being present. In addition to the choir, which numbers sixty voices, the society had secured Miss Louise Cestria, soprano; Mr. A. F. Them- borough, tenor ; and Mr. Woodville Francis, baritone ; all artistes possessed of undoubted talent. A most pleas- ing programme had been arranged, the first portion con- sisting of Gaul's historical cantata, "Jean of Arc," the three leading parts being undertaken by the professionals already named. The latter part of the programme was also admirably sustained, and was made of a part song, trio, aria, ballad, chorus, and two songs. There were frequent outbursts of applause, some of which were duly acknowledged. The duties devolving upon con- ductor were efficiently performed by Mr. F. H. Crossley, and whilst Miss B. Lewis ably accompanied the various singers on the pianoforte.</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP MEETING.—The annual meeting of rate payers was held on Saturday, Mr. Thomas Davies presid- ing. Messrs. T. Davies and J. W. Rethwell were nominated overseers on the motion of Mr. H. Tiley, seconded by Mr. J. M. Williams. The remuneration to the Assistant Overseer was fixed for the collection of the sanitary special rate tribe £l5 for each half year. Col. Ashton read the attendances which ran as follows Messrs. Williams, 24 ; Fleywood, 24 : Lewis, 8: Shere 22; Riley, 23; Linaker, 16; Spencer, 12; Tiley, 23; Earlam, 25 ; Ashworth, 3 ; Thomas.2l ; Davies, 13 ; and Robinson, 4. Messrs. A. 'Thomas, J.P.,Heywood, Williams, Davies, A. Linaker, They, Riley, Shore, Earlam, Lewis, H. J. Linaker, J. Garner, and H. Lewis, were elected the committee for the ensuing year.—ln the course of a short discussion upon the water question, Mr. Heywood remarked that the contract for the supply would be ter- minated bySeptember next.—Mr.S.Davies said the works had gone on favourably and that towards the end of the following week the work of laying the mains would be commenced. The usual vote of thanks terminated the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>"THE MORALS OF DOUGLAS."</head>
        <p>"THE MORALS OF DOUGLAS."</p>
        <p>ALLEGED LIBEL BY A WESLEYAN MINISTER. An application was made to his Honour the Clerk of the Rolls, sitting in the Chancery Division, Douglas, on Saturday. by Mossrs. Speneer and Hannay, prenriotors of the "Manx Sun," against Messrs. Clueas and Fargber Proprietors of the" Mona's Herald, for an injunction to restrain the defendants and all others from printing and 7,ublishing a certain pamphlet, entitled "The Morals of Douglas," alleged to contain a wilful, false, malicious, de- famatory libel of and concerning the plaintiffs, purport- ing to have been written by the Rev. Thomas Rirpen, of Douglas, Wesleyan minister. Messrs. King and Kneen appeared for the plaintiffs, and Messrs. Creer and Gell for the defendants. Mr. Creer admitted an injunction. His Honour thereupon granted an injunction against the defendants and all others against publishing the alleged libel, and gave warning that, inasmuch as in this case a fine might not have a deterrent effect, any person dis- obeying the injunction would be Sentenced to a long term of imprisonment. The case excites great local in- terest.</p>
        <p>Miss Kathleen O'Brady-Jones, the eldest /daughter of the Vicar of St. Luke's, Wallsend. near Newcastle-on- Tyne, was on Wednesday shot dead by a revolver acci- dentally discharged by a boy named Dewar, who has been arrested.</p>
        <p>" TOBACCONIRTS commencing" Illustrated guide (ot,itt fre3 TOBACCONIVe °tr.:FITTING Ilustou-roF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>„s. Fr ,P, E</head>
        <p>„s. Fr ,P, E</p>
        <p>That it is not wise to experiment on diseases with cheap medicine;3. purporting to be Blood Puriii,3rs and Cleansers, but which have no real medicinal value. They are made to sell and to amass an enormous fortune for the proprietors.</p>
        <p>Th...) C. A. Voge2er CUiIf4TiVE COMP,'.)LegNID</p>
        <p>Is a scientifically prepared, high-class pharma- ceutical preparation, the result of the latest, discovery in medicine. It positively cures Dyspepsia, Indigestion, Cora-tipation. Scrofula, Biliousness, Skin Diseases, Liver Complaints, Stomach Affections, Kidney Difficulties, and Female Complains. Can always be depended upon, the same standard of superior excellence is invariably maintained. It acts directly on the Liver, Stomach, and Kidneys ; it Purifies the Blood and remove;', all impurities from the system. The dose is 15 drc:p.4, taken immediately after meals. The taste is pleasant, the bottles are large, prices low, riame!y, and 2s. Cd. ; from Chemists. or sent i'ost Free by the Proprietors on receipt of Price.</p>
        <p>THE. CHARLES A. VOGELER CO., 45, Farringdon Road, London.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL GATHERING OF RUNCORN . CONSTABLES</p>
        <p>The fourth annual social gathering of the Runc)rn Police Constables took place on Wedne,lay evening in the Bethesda Lecture Hall, Runcorn, under the presidency of Mr. Samuel Taylor, JP., and, in point of success, completely eclipsed all its fore-runners. The windows of the room had been tastefully draped with art muslin curtains of various pretty hues, bunting was hung profusely overhead, whilst, the lat- form was nicely relieved with pretty curt tin 3 and foliage plants. Mr. Steele, of Runcorn, who is well- known for his readiness to aid any des,rving object, kindly lent the curtains, and Messrs. Shaw and Littler with their usual generosity, supplied the bunting. The proceedings, as on all former occasions, opened by the provision of a most enjoyable tea, which was partaken of by a company numbering more than forty, the following ladies, amongst others, super- intending the necessary arrangements :—Mesdames S. Coventry, J. A. Smith, T. Wood, and T. Cooke. This meal passed off with the utmost satisfaction,after which the room was cleared for the evening's diversion. Pre- vious to commencing the musical portion of the pro- gramme,</p>
        <p>The Chairman said there was no one who sympathised more with that m..vement than himself. (Hear,. hear.) When he heard some years ago that efforts were being made to organise a treat for the police force he was de- lighted, hut he felt surprised that he was not appealed to for subscriptions. Subsequently he was told that it WPS principally supported by magistrates, and this year he had the honour to he amongst the contributors. (Ap- plause.) A policeman's lite was a monotonous one and the duties to be performed were not always pleasant. A great deal was expected frorr them and he had no doubt that from time to time they felt disheartened at their services not being recognised as they should be. He, however, assured them that such was not the case. All law-abiding citizens respected the police. When people could go to their beds feeling that their property was in sate keeping it must he gratifying to every good civilian. The position was an honourable one, for a man could not join the force if his character, after severe scrutiny, was not found to be good. With regard to the consta- bulary of Che:ihire he felt perfectly safe in saying that more honourable, straightforward men could not be found in the kingdom. (Applause.) He felt proud of their chief, for from what he had heard and read of him. there was not a more considerate gentleman than Col. Hatrenersley. (Hear, hear.) So long as men did their duty they had nothing to fear, for under that gentleman they would always secure justice, as far as promotion vas concerned. He ( he speaker) was sorry that Supt. Atherton was not able to be present that evening through indisposition. He knew him when a boy, and when he was aware that he had entered the police force he was convinced that he would make his mark. Consequently he as not surprised when he heard that Mr. Atherton had secured the offiee of superintendent. From what he had seen of him he was a man of determination and was bound to come to the front. (Applause.) In conclusion Mr. Taylor expressed his pleasure at seeing so many ladies present.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cooke read a letter from Superintendent Large. of Middlewich regretting his inability to be present ow- ing to enforced business, and hoping that they would Spent a happy evening. A telegram was also re-id from Superintendent Leah of Nantwich expressing sorrow for not being able to attend. The programme was then proceeded with and proved of a most entertaining nature, the items including an over- ture and a selection by the Ladies' guitar and mandoline band ; (Mesdames Cooper, Farkinson, IVarburton, and Burns, and Misses Carter and Warburton, and Master II Cooper;) duetts : Miss Sinikin and Mr. R. Morris ; songs: Nlisses Sinakin, and Amy Atherton, Messrs. W. Coventry,. Leathwood, and R. Morris ; humorous songs:. Miss E. A. Cooke, and Messrs. F. Scott and B. Morris ;. piano- forte duett : Miss A. M. Cooke and Mr. W. Cooke; violin solo: Miss A.Smith; and lastly,but by no means. the least in talent, was a recitation by Miss A. Rogers. Several songs, both smtimental and comic were also given by members of the police force. The en cores were. very numerous, almost all the artistes named being recalled for his or her contribution, so creditable were the performances. One of the most mirth-produc- ing items was the comic singing and dancing of a couple of members of the compa.ny, who assumed the names of "Messrs. Bimbo and Timbuctoo, from Ash•antee." These gentlemen on being encored, created considerable amusement by their humorous singing of an original song, introducing some of the well-known men of the town ; the Chairman being atnongst the number. There were also comic allusione to the police force,its Superin- tendent and Inspector, which made the song extremely laughable. The entertainment having been brought to. a close,</p>
        <p>Inspector Cooper said that he had the utmost pleasure on behalf of the police, in proposing a hearty vote of thanks to the chairman and the remaini . magistrates and other gentlemen who had contributed to the even- ing's feast and entertainment. He could assure those gentlemen that their efforts in assisting to provide the entertainment were appreciated by every participant. The police force was daily brought into contact with the dark side of the picture of life, but that evening's proceedings enabled them all to look upon the brieht side of it for a few hours.—Sergeant Woolley said lie was extremely leased to second the resolution so ably moved by his sTllperior. He could fully endorse every word that had been spoken by the mover, but he would like to say further that he felt it a great privi- lege to thank the magistrates and other gentlemen for their thoughtfulness and liberality in providing such an excellent entertainment.—Sergeant Lea in supporting the motion said that the whole force esteemed to the fullest extent the generosity of the gentleman who had contributed to that evening's enjoyment. Each indi- vidual member of the force felt more than gratified he was sure for such kindness.—Mr. T. Cooke, on behalf of the organizing committee, said that Mr. Taylor hid been very anxious to be one of the contributors to the social gathering, and this year he had had the honour of becoming one. Every person pre- sent that evening, when they considered how often Mr. Taylor had helped forward any deserving movement, would express a wish that those ' beautiful lines of the Runcorn poet would speedily be- come true. Those lines were to the effect that their chairman having attained the honour of "J.P." would further attain " C.C." and ultimately become an "M.P." (Applause.) With regard to the performers when they were asked to give their services not a refusal was given, but everyone seemed on the contrary to he pleased to act. As to the donors it was astonishing how ree-ly the responses were made to the appeals for contributions, they all seemed to look forward to the gathering. He had been complimented by one magistrate for having succeeded in securing Mr. Taylor to reign over them that evening. (Applause.) Mr. Cooke concluded amidst. the singing by the audience of " He's a Jolly Good ' Fellow."—The Chairman having suitably responded on behalf of the donors and himself said he would like to move a vote ot thanks to the four gentlemen who had worked 80 hard to organise the gathering: Messrs. T. Cooke, S. Coventry, J. A. Smith, and T. Wood. Every praise was due to these gentlemen, because it was no easy matter to raise the funds for such a treat as that one—(hear, hear,)—and not only that but to prepare it.—The motion was unanimously carried. Mr. Coventry in acknowledging the honour on behalf of the Committee said this was one of the sides of socialism in which he believed. It had given them great pleasure to labour for such an object and had they not succeeded it would have given them very serious pain. He hoped now that they had got a " J.P. " to take the chair, the affair would become an annual event. (Applause.) Messrs. Smith, Wood, and Cooke also briefly acknow- ledged the thanks accorded them. _ .</p>
        <p>to Subsequently a substantial supper was prepare c! 'which the whole company sat down and did full justice. A little dancing was afterwards indulged in and finally the party broke up after having spent a very enjoyable evening. Amongst the gentlemen who have given donations to the fund are the following magistrates : Mr. John Brundrit, Sir R. Brooke, Major Hazelhurst. Messrs. S. Taylor, P. Speakman, F. J. Norman, C- RPI-nclds, H. Reynolds, James Reynolds, A. Thomas! r-J• Wigg, and James Handley, and Dr. Steele. Contribu- tions were also received from Dr. McDougall awl Mr. F. A. Lake.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CITED WITHOUT OPERATION—S. J. She'll's°. Hernia Specialist, 64. Chancery-Lane, London ; 26. street', Manciheater ; and 4. Burlitaxton-chambers. Now-sitr"'"; Birmingham. Book on rupt,.kria ao.ti evil of l*ls• free. 7d.....44i;t,</p>
