        <head>BURTON WOOD.</head>
        <p>BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION CONTROVERSY AT</p>
        <p>! RESULT OF THE SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION. 1 The election of a new School Board for Burton- wood, near St. Helens, took place on Tuesday, and in consequence of recent difficulties great excite- ment prevailed throughout the district. The dis- pute originally arose on a question as to whether the trustees or the vicar had a right to the old schoolhouse. It will be remembered that a few months ago the vicar put a new tenant in posses- sion, when one of the trustees entered the house and refused to allow any furniture to be brought in. The trustee was ordered out by the vicar, and as he refused to go he was expelled. As the trien- nial election for the School Board was pending, the matter remained in abeyance; until the vicar (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) pulslished the correspondence which had taken place. The vicar stated that lie thought it was due to the. parishioners that they should know what had passed between the outgoing School Roard and the clergyman of the parish. He had therefore had the cor- respondence printed. They would do well to read it carefully, and to consider how very serious and even critical was the state of things, educationally,at Burton- wood. He asked them to bear in mind (1) that the board as then constituted was not representative, being com- posed of farmers only. and labour had no representative; (2) that the School Board would neither provide a school at Collins Green nor agree to the Church doing so, and he had been repeatedly asked by the people at Collins Green to start a school there ; (3) that the School Board had been unable to accept the challenge thrown down by him, and was therefore unworthy of the ratePayers' .undivided confidence ; (4) that the attitude of the board towards the church was simply an act of betrayal such as the late incumbent never dreamt of when he assisted in the introduction and formation of the board; (5) the inconsistency of the board was apparent, and it had never been explained how it came to allow a Roman school to be planted in the parish. The vicar alleged incompetency of the board to cope with the growing needs of the district for years past. Then follows a cor- respondence,in which the vicar had asked the board whether they would raise any difficulty to the starting of an elementary Church school at Collins Green, and whether the board was prepared to sanction religious teaching in the Burtonwood school by the vicar or any clergyman who might be assisting in the parish. Mr. William Webster, clerk to the School Board, replied that the board were net aware of any deficiency of public elementary school accommodation in the district, and when such deficiency arose the board would take the necessary steps to supply the same. The Board,further, could not legally permit any denominational teaching in their schools, and was therefore unable to grant the re- quest. The vicar considered the words "legal disability" meant" Church hostility." The retiring members were Mr. James Warburton (Church of England), Mr. John Warburton (Church of England),Mr. William Fairclough (Church of England), Mr. Thomas Perks (Church of England), and Mr. Thomas Norcott (Nonconformist). Althcugh four of the five were churchmen, as a. body they were opposed to the vicar's views. All the old members were nominated, and in addition to them the vicar himself came forward, and Mr. H. Dawson,station master, of Collins Green, also came forward to support him. Mr. J. Jackson, manager of the Collins Green and Bold Collieries, also came forward as an Independent candidate. The result of the counting was as as follows: —Mr. Jackson, 677 votes ; Mr. Norcott, 264 ; Mr. Fair- dough. 130; Mr. James Warburton, 130; and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, 111.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NEW MISSION CHURCH AND SCHOOLS</head>
        <p>NEW MISSION CHURCH AND SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>IMPOSING MASONIC CEREMONY. Under most imposing circumstances and with full Masonic honours, the memorial stone of the new mission church and schools in connection with the St. Helens Parish Church was laid on Tuesday afternoon by the Right Hon. the Earl of Lathom, G.C.8., M.W. Prov. G.M., and R.W. Prov. Grand Master of the Western Division of Lancashire. An especial Grand Lodge had been called for the pur- pose, ,and there was is very large gt:therin.2; of MaFons, wearing regalia, and decorated with the jewels of the order. The Earl of Lathom arrived at St. Helens at noon, and was met by Colonel Gamble, C. 8., and the Vicar 01 St. Helens (Rev. J. W. Willink) and other gentlemen. The noble lord was driven in Colonel Gamble's brougham to Windlehurst, where the colonel entertained a small party of Grand Masons, including the Earl of Lathom, and also the vicar, and his brother, Mr. W. E. Willink, architect, of Liverpool. A proces- sion, headed by the Engineer band, left York-Et. Schools about three o'clock. It included Tylers with drawn swords, an inner guard with a sword, the architect with the plans, Masters of lodges bearing a cornucopia with corn and two ewers with wins and oil, Past Masters bearing the mallet and trowel, the Pro. Grand Super- intendent of Works bearing a plate with the inscription for the foundation, the Pro. Grand Secretary with the book of constitution, the Pro. Grand Treasurer bearing a phial containing the coins to be deposited in the stone, the Corinthian light, the Doric light, the lonic light, and columns of the order, the Pro. Jun. Grand Warden with the plumb rules, the Sacred Law borne by four Master Masons' sons, and opened at the 10th chapter of Num- bers. Then came the Pro. Grand Chaplain and Grand Officers of England, and Pro. Grand Masters of the pro- vinces. The Deputy Pro Grand Master carried the square. Then came the standard of the Pro. Grand Master, the Pro. Sword Bearer, and the Right Worship- ful thir Pro. Grand Macter (the Earl of Lathom, G.C.8.), attended by P.G. Stewards. The rear of the procession was brought up by Pro. Senior G. Deacon, the Pro. Gran el Tyler, with drawn sword and two heralds. The Masonic procession was met by a procession of clergy and choirs, who preceded the brethren to the site. There was a large gathering of the general public present at the ceremony. The Rev. J. W. Willink, "M.A. (vicar) in presenting the trowel to Lord Lathom, took the opportunity of submitting a short history of the movement. —ln acceptint the trowel, his Lordship addressed a few appropriate remarks. The Provincial Grand Chaplain then offered prayer, and the brethren responded "So mote it be." After singing " 0 God our help in ages past," the Prov. Grand Sec. read aloud the inscription on the plate ; the Prov. Grand Treasurer deposited the phial containing the coins, and the Pro. Grand Secretary placed the plate on the lower stooe. The cement having been spread, the Earl of Lathom adjusted the same with the trowel handed to him, and the upper stone was lowered with three dis- tinct stops, the band playing appropriate music. The Prov. Gravid Master (the noble earl) then proved the posi- tion and form of the stone by the plumb rule, level and square, which were successively handed to him by the Prov. Junior Grand Warden, the Prov. Senior Grand Warden, and the Deputy Prov. Grand Master. Having been satisfied in these particulars the earl gave the stone three knocks with a mallet also delivered to him. , The cornucopia containing the corn and the ewers with the wine and oil were then handed to the Prov. Grand Master, who strewed the corn and poured wine and oil owr the stone with the accustomed ceremonies. A hymn and prayer followed, and then the arthitect pre- sented the plans for the earl's inspection. The earl,hav- ing examined them, returned them to the architect, and desired him to proceed without loso of time in the com- pletion of the work in conformity with the plans. A hymn and the "National Anthem "having been sung the Masonic procession was re-formed, and returned to the York-st., schools. The collection at the stone amounted to £4.0 13s. 6d. About £3,000 is still required. Tire building will be of brick, with the .best pressed bricks for dressing. The principal feature of the in- side is the general use of sliding and rolling shutters. By means of these, the ground and first floors can be divided into one large schoolroom or two small school- rooms and two classrooms, each forming ono large room with nolmterruption of view except by iron columns. Mr. W. E. Willink, architect ; Mr. John Whittaker,con- tractor ; Mr. T. Wood, manager ; Mr. Aspinall, clerk of j works ; Mr_ Aukland, mason; and Mr. W. Harrison, I joinery work, were present at the ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ST. HELENS TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>_ JR ADJOURNMENT OF THE INQUEST. lury empannelled to inquire into the circum- ' attending the death of Joseph PH:avarice, aged !en years, of Boundary-road, St. Helens, reassem- 'a Thursday morning at the Town Hall.—lnspector ilted that the young woman Elizabeth Johnson +as shot in the hand and head before the deceased s own life), was still in the Providence Hospital. ctor stated on Wednesday that it was not at all ;that she would be able to appear this day week, about a fortnight they might expect ber to b'i in ince.—The Coroner: The questien is whether it not be better to adjourn for three weeks. Ido at to bring, you (the jurym( n) here in a 'fortnight ?o have to bind you over again. It would he your was decided to adjourn until the • aril.</p>
        <p>IRTHE</p>
        <p>_ The Medical Annual advises Practitioners to remember that. whcin recommending COCOA.S as food and beverage for invalids the name CADBURY on aitypacket of Cocoa is a guarantee of pnrity.—Advt. SUNLIGHT SOAP WRAPPER COMPETITION.—The results of the Sunlight Soap Wrapper Competition for Feb., are) now t'i hand. In No. 5 district which embraces m Cuber and, Westmoreland, Lanc:shire and the Isle of Man, Thomas Smith, 39, Alexandra-road, Patricroft, near M4nchester, and Henry Naylor, Chapel-lane, Cron- ton, neai• Prescot, have won " Premier "Safety Bicycles, with DUnlop's pneumatic tyres; value £2O each. ,Miss• Faith' KNowlss, 10, Penriphiw Terrace, Gilicar-lane, 1 Ashton-in-Maiterlield, has also been successful in one if the *inners of th Silver- Keyless Lever " Waltham watcheslvalue £4 4s. . -tiered' I)ary Diseases, so-called, come largely from a weak emaciate condition. when the system hasnot the energy to throw of disease. General debility is a weakened state. and breeds 41s0rdErs. Scott's Emulsion creates a fountain of strength and energy in the system, more than counterbalanc- , inF.wealcuesses, whether hereditary or otherwise, Chemists , 6d. aucl4a. 6d.—(3)</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS BUTCHERS AND THE PRO- POSED NEW ABATTOIR.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the St. Helens Health Committee on Wednesday, a deputation. headed by Councillor J. A. Chadwick, from the St Helens branch of the Liverpool and District But- chers' Association, attended with respect to the prososal of the Corporation to erect a new abattoir. Mr. Chad- wick said they were not present to oppose the scheme. but rather to support it. As practical butchers they were prepared to admit that the present Corporation slaughter-house was inadequate for the requirements of the trade, and not at all suitable for a public slaughter- house. There was one question, however, on which they were very anxious, and that was how the new pre- mises when erected would affect the existing licensed slaughter-houses. At the present time there were twenty- one licensed slaughter-houses in St. Helens. Plans had been submitted and approved by the Council for the erection of the premises, and licences had been applied for and granted. The total cost had been £13,000, and the owners were anxious to know what the Corporation intended to do. It would be rather hard if their licences were withdrawn or cancelled, so long as the premises were kept in a thoroughly sanitary condition.—Mr. J. Houlton endorsed the remarks of Mr.Chadwick,and said it was the existing licences they wanted protected.— The Chairman (Councillor J. Forster) said at the last meeting before they finally decided that the new pre- mises should be built the question the deputation had raised was discussed, and they decided, what had been in the minds of the committee for a long thne, that so long as the private slaughter-houses were kept in a sani- tary condition and the surroundings were all right,there would be no need whatever to close them. It was stated, and the deputation would no doubt be aware,that there were one or two slaughter-houses which were over- crowded, and not proper plaees to slaughter cattle in. ("That is so.") The statement had been made that the present public abattoir was inadequate,and there was a very great necessity, for the sake of the public of Bt. Hele...,s,as well as for the sake of the butchers of the town, that an adequate one should be provided.—The deputa- tion thanked the committee for the very favourable answer, and withdrew.</p>
        <p>Bell IL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>HAYDOCK READING ROOM.</head>
        <p>HAYDOCK READING ROOM.</p>
        <p>. -- PRESENTATION TO MR. JAMES FORREST.</p>
        <p>_ _ On Thursday evening an interesting meeting was held in the Haydock Reading Room, when a pre- sentation was made to Mr. James Forrest who was secretary to the institution for two or three years from its commencement, and who is leaving the township for Clifton near Bolton. There was a good Lumber of the members present, and the chair was occupied by Mr. Arthur Evans, the president and foun- der of the reading room. The meeting took the form of a "social," and a splendid programme was arranged by the Committee. At the commencement a reading en- titled "The jumping frog," was given by Mr. Johnson. This was followed by a song, "The Pilot," by Mr. Pui- fer ; song, " Kathleen " Mr. Valentine; song, "The soldier and the man," dr. S. Pimlett. The presentation to Mr. Forrest was then made by Mr. A. Evans. He commended Mr. Forrest for the very active interest he had taken and the hard work he had d,ne at the formation of the reading-room, and also for the interest he had evinced in its welfare. He regretted that Mr. Forrest was leaving the township, but at the same time, he was pleased to know that he was improving his position, and that he was going to friends with whom he had worked many years ago. He would nO doubt go on and prosper. He (Mr. Evans) had great pleasure on behalf of the mtmbers of the Haydock reading room, in presenting to Mr. Forrest a walking stick, a. handsome briar pipe and tobacco pouch, and a silver match box, which had been voluntarily subscribed for by the members of the reading room, as a token of the esteem and respect in which he , was held by the members of the club, with their best wishes.—Mr. Forrest thanked the members for their kind wishes, and for their splendid present.—The pre- sentation was followed by a reading, "God bless these poor folk," by Mr. James Taylor, and a song by Mr. Johnson.—Mr. James Taylor then proposed, Mr. John Matthews seconded and it was carried unanimously that Mr. James Forrest be made a life honoraey member of the reading room on acconnt of his services rendered to the institution.—Mr. Edwin Jackson next favoured the company with a song " Monks of old " ; Mr. Forrest sang " Hound goes the wheel " ; and Mr. Walden read in an excellent style " McWilliams 'Lightening " from Mark Twain. Dr. Dowling caused roars of lauehter by the Irish song" Bally Hooley," and on receiving an encore gave another Irish song" Kilaloo." " When other lips" was also given by Mr. Thomas Abbott, and "Lather 'em shave 'em," by Mr. Johnson. " Auld lang sync" was thee sung by the whole of the company, after which Dr. Dowling, proposed a vote of thanks to the president for the great interest he always took in the reading room. His visits were like angels visits, but he wished they could have. him oftener.—Mr. Forrest seconded the motion, and it was carried with musical honours. An interesting meeting was brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
