      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>TOWN CLOCK.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN CLOCK.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 flnd it difficult to believe that Mr. Peer's state- ments concerning the pendulum suspension of the town clock are seriously intended. The method of suspension which he professes to consider so defective and " so -fatal to good time-keening," is approved by Lord Grim- thorpe (formerly Sir E. Blckett), generally considsred to be the greatest authority on the subject of turrent clocks, and it is fully described in his book, "Clocks, and watches, and bells." Only for unusually large clocks in which the greatest possible accuracy is desired, does he recommend that the pendulum should be sus- pended from the wall of the tower, as in the Westminis- ter clock, which was made from Lord Griinthorpe's de- sign. Mr. Peers speaks of the massive iron frame (cast in one piece) which supports the town clock, as a "gimcrack iron box swaying by every breeze of wind." It cannet possibly be swayed by the wind unless the whole tower rocks, in which case perhaps Mr. Peers can explain to us how the pendulum would escape the effect of such vibration even if it were suspended from the wall. I shall not trouble to reply to any further assertions Mr. Peers may cliooss to make about the clock ; and I should not have felt called upon to reply to his last let- ter if he had simply confined himself to the personal re- marks and misrepresentations of which it mostly con- sists —Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>F. W. REYNOLDS, B.A.</head>
        <p>F. W. REYNOLDS, B.A.</p>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF CALF-LYIIPH VACGINA-</p>
        <p>TION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As the evils of arm-to-arm vaccine virus are now reluctantly admitted by many public vacoinators. it is proposed, with the view of warding off opposition, to extend the use of calf-lymph. The dangers of calf- lymph, however, are equal to, if not greater than, those due to arm-in-arm vaccination, besides which it eseeuld be quite impracticable on a large scale, and " so far from being likely to produce fewer ailments and cutane- ous eruptions in the predisposed, it would, as being more irritating, produce more." (lids Dr. Seaton's " Hand- book of Vaccination." page 397.) The following extract from an address given before the Kansas Medical Asso- ciation, February, 1883, by Dr. T. Arthur Wright, vice- president, may interest the readers of the " Examiner :" "The query comes to us pertinently if human virus transmits disease, will not brute virus do the same? What physician or veterinary surgeon will presume to say when the body of the human or brute is free from disease, and that virus from them can be used with safety ? . . . . It will not do (when accidents oc- cur) to say we selected impure virus, for our ability to choose he best is as good as any physician present. In 1874 we vaccinated over three hundrd children from selected calf-lymph, and after a careful observation of the most of them from that time to the present, we must say as an honourable man, a father, and a physician, they have not been so healthy since. The immediate effect in a large number of cases was traumatic fever, followed by large excavated ulcers, of from three weeks to four months duration. In others a severe vaccinal sore throat, involving the palate, pharynx, and tonsiles, leaving the latter enlarged, and the former in an atonic condition. Still others were attacked by erysipelas, "at the point of inoculation," extending to the shou:dcr and elbow, enlarging the glands of the axilla and chest, Snally resulting in deep abscesses beneath the Iturneral, asillary, and pectoral tissues." After submitting numerous testimonies from medical men as to disasters consequent on the use of calf-lymph, Dr. Wright goes on to say :—`• In our judgment, pure vaccine virus, from any source, is but a creature of imagination, ar; best, the offal of a festering pustule prcduced on the body of the human or brute by disease. Can it be possible, after centuries of accumulated knowledge that medical science has nothing better to offer for the relief of human frailties than disease, and through confessed ignorance is compelled to seek statutory jaw in order to maintain the existence of a medical dogma ? Then shame be up- on it. If governments instead of making medicine compulsory or establishing; bureaus for the production and distribution of blood poison, would hold physicians responsible for the evils growing out of their practice, there is not one but would abandon vaccination as a wrong physielogically before the present century is out." Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>JAMES R. WILLIAMSON. 84, Seymour-street. Ruston Square, London, N. W. 28th March, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ARTISTIC ASPECTS OF PHOTOGRA PHY.</head>
        <p>THE ARTISTIC ASPECTS OF PHOTOGRA PHY.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Amateur Photographic Society held on Tuesday evening in the Museum Committee Room, Mr. C. B. Aylward read a paper on the above subject. In the absence of the president (Mr. T. J. Down), Mr. H. N. Houghton presided. There was only a small attendance. Tie essayist said that he would con- tinue the subject of his previous paper on which occasion he dealt principally with the training which an artist inelerwent, and dealing with the laws and prin- ciples regulating composition, tac. That evening, he prapassal to deal with the artiitic aspects of photo- graphy. They laid arrived at a period when the scien- tific and technical aspects of photography were very clearly definea, bui; its artistai aspect remained to a certaaaaatent in&amp; fined. He was sure that they would all be areal that thi artistic element in photography had progressed during the last 20 years, and every true artist, williway appreciated an artistic photograph. Photographers like artists committed mistakes, hat the blatneshauld not be laid upon photography or art, hut to the want of capability of the photographer or artist to produce a work of art. The lecturer then read ex- tracts tram tie speeches and writings of various well- treown artists, in which they expressed the opinion that platography generally, was of ereat value to the artist.- Continuing, he said that photography and it; artistic development were only yet beginnina to dawn, and therefore it, ',thence was scarcely felt, but what influence it had, had been for the better, ;mei it hail and would continue to instruct the general pablic. He would offer a few suggestions whereby photearsphers might obtain some artistic results. The purpose of art was to represent objects with gracefulness and truth, and much of the charm of a picture depended upon skil- ful management of contrast. The practice of introduc- ing equal quantities on each side of a picture, of large objects in the centre was very objectionable and should be as far as possible avoided. If this be necessary the objects should not be obtrusive. The true artist was not an ordinary copyist. It was the study and selection of nature's best aspect which made a good picture. Photography had the power of catching fleeting effects, securing truth of detail, and of depicting with ease at- mospheric phenomena which could only with difficulty be reproduced at all even by the ablest painter. The sense ot sight required educating, as his experience was that when the students saw the varied objects with a true vision, they began to progress with their studies. He would advise amateur photographers to take objects near home and then compare the plate with the object, and thus find out the faults. Photographers should not glaze a print just because they liked glazing, as some pictures such as sea-scapes and a bold rocky, shore, were better upon rough paper. The matt surface was a more true representation of nature as the artist saw it. Ia a true work of art, there was subserving of tine and colour in every part of the picture so that the general effect was harmonious to the eye. When this was unattended to, a harshness was producd which gave improper isolation to indivi- dual parts and the Octure was said to he " out of keeping." Respecting the photographic tone of a print, it should contain all the elements of the artistic tone,and it must be suitable to the subject. For instance, for printing from a classic building he would advise a grey, Or black, whereas a Gothic building would be more pleas- ing printed by some warm tone. Artists were distin- guished by different powers. One arrested the eye at a distance, while another solicited a close examination. •One excelled in landscape, another in buildings and a third in figures, while each maintained that original I •character which constituted his peculiar charm. In conclusion, genius or natural talent, conscious of its inmate powers, often resisted discipline as superilueus, 'but in art, as in all knowledge, there were no myateries, 'no secrets discoverable by genius only. The entrance .to every scientific pursuit might appear somewhat uninvit- ing, and the requirements of mechanical labour,. com- bined with close application, discouraging conditions, yet each progressive step would afford ample amends. Want of genius and want of talent were common lamentations and common excuses, but wonders might be achieved by the lowest ability, if assisted by an unre- mitting diligence. Nothing was denied to well directed labour, and nothing was to be obtained without it, and the essential requisite to artistic pursuits was applica- tion. At the conclusion of the essay, Mr. Houghton moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Aylward, and asked him if he would read the paper on some future occasion, as he was sure the small attendance was due to the holiday- season. —The motion was carried unanimously,and Mr. A Ylward briefly replied and sail that he would accede to Mr. Houghton's request.—The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>An order from the War Office wa4 received on 'Thurs- day at Chatham 3utliorising the introduction of the • day among th men employed on WI gun eight'h°llr ,harf. ,te •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>What have we here ! " Warrington," says a contem- porarv, " had a peculiar experience on Saturday. They were -due at Whalley Ilange, and, thinking they wiuld combine business with pleasure, they journeyed to the p,)rt of -Manchester via the ship Canal, Whether the heaving waves disturbed their diaphragms or the strong scent of czone laden breezes upset their delicate systems we know not,but it is a fact that the explorers and navi- gators failed to show a stropig and powerful front to their Mancunian opponents,and to the surprise of every body who has followed the fortunes of the Warringto- nians, defeat came with crushing force. Now there is SOPrOW in the homes of the wirepullers, and the Canal is nct regarded as a blessing. Its liquid glories have no charms for sea-sick footballers." And I always under- stood that Warrington were a match for any sort of water, not turbulent canal water alone, but even best s--but still, it is of no importance !</p>
        <p>Still, lain inclined to believe! that it was not so much the effect upon the players, of the motion of the Ship Canal boat, which lost Warrington the match, as the absence of such capable members of the team as Broady Barber, Potts, Jolley, and Dakin. The defeat was only three points to nil, and it is quite possible that Warring- ton, being under the influence of a midsummer sun, and probably feeling like taking matters easy this holiday season, did not trouble much about good play. At least it is to be hoped they did not, for there was not much about the performance to persuade anyone into believ- ing that either teain was anxious to demonstrate its capabilities in putting on the field a scientific exhibition of the game.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday a tremendous gate assembled at Wilderspool to witness the match between the home- sters and Lancaster. The unusual heat, however, had a detrimental effect upon the game, and the players all round soon showed signs of fatigue, while the specta- tors became too uncomfortably hot and drowsy to exert themselves either in cheering or getting excited about the result. Llowever, as the game proceeded Warring- ton did manage to get in a little scoring and by the time the whistle was blown for " tine," the wirepullers were victors by three tries to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>In a few weeks the cricket season will open at War- rington. when,according to such prophets as are capable of forming a fairly accurate judgment, a good time will set in for Warrington. What the precise grounds are upon which this prophecy is based, I cannot tell, but I know that they include the services of throe or four new players whose assistance has been secured. Chief among these is the new first pro., Mr. Win. Bate—better known as " Billy " Bate—of the Yorkshire County. As any average well-read cricket enthusiast knows, the genial " Billy" was a short time ago the leading player in the Yorkshire County; he always went in first, and gener- ally came out last. As a batsman he was ever and as a bawler, his quick "greased lightening" sort •1 balls have often been the terror of both batsman and wicket keeper. He had to resign his position in the county owing to an optical accident; but he has almost recovered from this, and I believe his .play ha a been affected but very little. Clark, the old professional, has been re-engaged as second "pro."</p>
        <p>The list of fixtures which he been arranged, is a very excellent one,and the club has been fortunate in getting on engagements with Manchester and Liverpool, as well as making fixtures with two clubs which they have never met before, viz. : Oldham and the Northern Club. The annual general meeting of the club will be held on the 9tt April, when all final arrangements will be com- pleted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>The wheelman was very much at large last week end, and ample proof was given of the fact that cycling has now become the most popular of all English pastimes. The roads about Warringt-m were literally "swarming " with the knights of the wheel. The members of the Warrington C.C., were of course not behind the times. They had three days crammed as full of jollity, and cheap teas, and big drinks, as it was possible for three days to be crammed. In a universal and wide-reaching sense of the phrase, they had a " rare old time of it." On Good Friday they journeyed to West Kirby, via Liverpool ; on Saturday they went to "fond memoried " Cranage, and enjoyed themselves as cyclists only can enjoy themselves at dranage.</p>
        <p>On Monday quaint old Chester attracted the. travellers and there they spent a delightful day. The old city, I suppose, had that day more cyclists in it than a:iy town its size ia supposed to have had before. They were to he seen about the streets in hundreds from all parts ot the country, many of them making a call on their way from or to Wales. On each of the three days all the officers of the club, together with the chaplain were on duty, so that nothiog more serious thus puncture or two, occurred to mar the harmony of the holidays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>EASTER VESTRIES. ---- (Continved from page 3)• WIDNES PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRIES. ---- (Continved from page 3)• WIDNES PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual Easter vestry meeting was hol4 in the Waterloo-road schools, on Tuesday evening. The Bev. W.Hodgson, M.A.,vicar,presided, and there wan a large ' attendance. The Chairman thanked the church officials for the way in which they Lad rallied round him, and said he could not have two better curates to work with than Mr. Adams and Mr. Jones, who were earnest and hard working men. Mr. D werryhouse read the statement of accounts of St. Mary's Cnurcli. This showed that the receipts for the church expenses amounted to £BB 3s. 101., and the expenditure £.8365. 51., leaving a balance in hand of £4 17s. 51. The total receipts from all sources amounted to £lB3 10s. Mr.Brookes read the statement of accounts of St. Paul's Church. The church, receipts were £76 19s. al., and expenditure £75 18.41., leaving a balance in hand of £1 0.3. 10.1. Toe total re- zeipts from all sources were £350 18.9 d. The Chairman in tneving the adoptien of the repotts, said that the figures were very gratifying,considering the distressing year they had passed through. He also said that St. Paul's had, during the strike, helped the town to the amount of £43 18s. 81. Messrs. J. Roberts and W. Lunt wore unanimonsly elected auditors. Mr. Brookes and Mr. Dwerryhouse were unanimously re-elected as war- dens. Mr. Neil and Mr. Crawford were unanimously re-elected as lay representatives. Mr. Jeynson was re- elected as vestry clerk. The following were elected by the vestry as.sidez:men :—St. Mary's, Messrs. A. Borth- wick, F. Beattie, A. Treacy, W.Roberts, k!e. Stowesand .1. Grocott. St. Nut's, Messrs. J. Burrows, J. Garton, J. Mi'cha, A. Case, F. Snaith,and W. Lunt. The following were nominated by the Vicar as sidesmen :—St. Mary's, ' Messrs. W. Dixon, H. Elufton, G Bruce. G. Stead, F. Cave, and F. Hartland. St. Paul's, Messrs. J. Arnell,W. Palin, Williams, J. Hough; ..1. Bingham, and W.Lowe. A hearty vote of thanks to the organists and choirs for their work during the year was passed,. A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WIDNES PE ITY SESSIONS.</head>
