      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 31, 1894.</p>
        <p>the assessor. He stands between them and their cons:ciences. Mr. Stead employed competent men to go through the city books and he gives figures that amply prove these rich men to he not only criminals but a standing menace to the welfare of society. An honest assessor could not meet his ex- penses, whereas a man need not; be very dishonest to make £20,000 during his term of office of four years.</p>
        <p>- Speaking of the social evil he states that the habit of levying black-mail is almost universal, and these poor women are as much slaves as ever negro was, but worse than all is the power of the police over the honest woman and his power to black-mail her also. The following is part of the speech Mr. Stead made to the ladies of 'Chicago just before he left them and against which an indignation meeting was at once held. "Those women who have great opportunities only to neglect them, and who have great means only to squander them upon them- selves, are more disreputable in the eyes of God and man than the worst. harlot on Fourth Avenue." The book abounds with figures culled from the best sources. The names of men and women are given, and the disreputable sources of some of their in- comes. Altogether I take it to be best authority ever issued in proof of the utter selfishness of un- bridled individualism and competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>" IF CHRIST CAME TO C ELICAGO."</head>
        <p>" IF CHRIST CAME TO C ELICAGO."</p>
        <p>BY Mn. H. WIIITHAM.</p>
        <p>During the closing days of the World's Fair, Mr. Stead, editor of the "Review of Reviews," paid a ! visit to Chicago. His object seems to have been,not so much to view the Fair, as to try his hand at a little reforming work. That he contrived to stir the good easy folk up is an undoubted fact, judging . from the numerous Press notices that have appeared in which his name has been connected with tar and feathers, duck ponds, and so on. This only goes to prove that the native American is a hard " cuss" to deal with. He not only tkinks that his native sod is the biggest in the Whole creation, but he thinks be has the biggest amount, of gray matter tumbling about in his biggest of all big brains, and woe be to the man, especially if he be an Englishman, that tries to drown the scream- ing of the American Eagle. I have had a little over six years' experience of the country and its peoples, and I have no hesitation at all in saying that if the successful running of the country depended upon those who scream so much about their nativity, the country would simply be hustled or rushed to the dogs. Three of the largest cities of the Union, New York, Chicago, and San Francisco, stand before the world to-day the most miserable object lessons it is possible to imagine in respect to municipal manage- ment. The daily Press, the judge on the besich, the senator, the congressman, the alderman, and the juryman are all heavily smirched with dirt. No need for the American to get riled because it is a foreigner that tells him these things. The Press day by day pours out the facts on the principle that when thieves fall out honest men come by their own. A few weeks ago I re-perused the " De- bate on Democracy " between Professor Blackie and Ernest Jones. I seemed to remember the Scotch professor saying something about cor- ruption in America. If my readers would know what America is to-day let them turn to this debate that took place over ten years ago, and they will have a faithful picture, It was then written, as it is written to-day—" The worst kind of pecuniary corruption prevails in America. Election agents bribe the lowest, classes of the populace; and hon- ourable members are paid openly for their votes:, Strange, is it not? America is supposed to be a democratic country and yet corruption prevails. Warrington is understood to be a Tory borough and yet the Licensed Victuallers can send into the Town Council as many tools of the common brewers as they think fit. This letter is likely to be a long one, an I my pur- pose in writing it is to prove or show how awk- wardly the beautiful idea of renunciation fits the individualist, or, in other words to make clear to my readers by liberal quotations what Mr. Stead thinks Christ's opinion would be of self should He ever come to Chicago. Mr. Stead is well-known as a very active literary man, and he has well earned this title, and something more, by the immense amount of work he managed to put in during his short, stay in the Windy City. In a book ot 400 pages, and for which he has taken the striking,and sensational caption at the head ot this letter as the title, he has gathered a mass of facts together , which, he says, many of the leading citizens of Chicago have been kind enough to enceurage him to publish. He says—" Throughout ail my work of interrogation and condensation I .have clung to the hypothesis which forms the keynote and the start- ing point of the whole,' If Ckrist came to Chicago.' To men of the world, to busy administrators, to labour egitators, to the crook and to the harlot,the question "If He came to Chicago, whet would He think of us and our lives?" was often strangely unfamiliar, and sometimes provoked the most incon- gruous replies. Mr. Stead tells us that the conception of Christ coining to Chieage reached him through reading a short poem of Lowell's, styled " A ' Parable," which seerp.ed to him to Slim up the vital essence of Christ's teaching. The poem after des- cribing Christ's visit to the earth and its unfortun- ate results so far as the rich were concerned, goes on in the last two verses as follows : Then Christ sought out an artiz-in, A low browed, stunted haggared man, I And a motherless girl, whose fingers thin, Pushed from her faSntly want and sin, These set he in the midst of them, And as they drew back their garment hem, For fear of defilement, " Lo, here," said he, The images ye have made of me. The book is divided into five parts, under the following titles :—" Some images ye have made of " ; " Christ measuring rod in Chicago "; " Satan's invisible world displayed " ; " Christ's Church in Chicago "; " What :would Christ do in Chicago? As a reporter Mr. Stead sought out and deals with the" foot sore leg-swollen tramp." He found him in retreats of all kinds from the empty trucks' to the damp and miserable cellars of the saloon, and this he cries is all that civiliesd Chicago has to offer its poor. He speaks of one of the police stations as a " nerve centre-of crime" and says that " behind the iron bars of its underground cages are penned up. night after night, scores and hundreds of the moat dissolute ruffians of both sexes that can be raked up in the dens of the levee." It. is well to know when money is wanted in the police courts the un- fortunate women of the city are 'raided from time ,to time for "revenue only.' When the Justice needs dollars he orders bail bonds to be paid, and the poor sinner is allowed to go and sin once more. Sometimes as many as 200 women are in the cells, waiting for samples of Justices justice. The city, for her casuals,provides nothing but shelter,warmth and a stony bed. Russia is more human, she at least provides a sloping wooden bed, with straw pillows for her prisoners. Bat Chicago deems she has done enough foe " the honest workman tramp- ing-.around in search of work when he has packed them together like herrings in a barrel." Under the heading of " Maggie Darling" he deals with the fallen women question. " One of the most zealous and faithful of the saintly and devoted women who have dedicated their lives to the ee,vice of fallen women told me, with a heart sore with the anguish of thwarted sympathy that far from her efforts being supported by the church they are regareed !as a development net to ha encouraged." This was ' .to Mr. Stead a .4 revelation," and drew from him !forcible words. " Stich a church tney he respect- able, but its respect:ability will not save it from per- dition. For the abiede of such is nigh unto the gates of bell." It is declared that the keepers of brothels buy off the police with Ioe. worth of free drinks each week. _ _</p>
        <p>The chapter dealing with whisky and pelitics is very effective telling how votes are bought and paid for. " Bribery, intimidation bulldozing of every kind, knifing, shooting and the whole swim- ing whisky. "You see, said Farmer Jones, speak- ing of the recent election of mayor, 'we is a black Republican ward, and we polled a majority of ninety for Hopkins, I took most of them to the poll myself, and it did not cost me more that 2s. a head, whereas the Republicans had to pay their men 12s. each before they could get them to the pcll and I was offered £2O if I would stay at home and do nothing." Chicago although Christian does not care much about the Trinity but has °nerd! to itself Marshall Field, Philip Armour and G. M. Pullman. " These three millionairs are the real work-a-day deities of Chicago." "If Christ came to Chicago would these men of many talents be able to show a good account of their stewardship!" The answer to this is applicable elsewhere besides the American city. After giving these men credit for smartness, and i•hrewiiness and a capability to handle dollars and cents, he charges that they have used all their talents for self while the city treasury is emuty,and ' that with two thousand millions ot taxable property Chicago has only two hundred end fifty million's that can be legally taxed. In connection with the I system of thieving which is rampant in high places Mr. Stead freely mentions names, and gives inst- ances saying indeed that "King Boodle is monarch of all he surveys " and that from the streets alone £1,000,000 is yearly used as boodle. The" pack ot hungry aldermen simply fill their pockets by bar- tering away the property of the city. A lawyer of a railway corporation speaking on the subject said. There are eixty eight aldermen in the City council and sixty six of them can be bought, I know be- cause I have bought them myself." Tax dodging is a subject I have often noticed ass burning question, and Mr. Stead deals with It with- out. gloves. It is almost impossible to make the rich people pay any of the direct taxes. I don't think it would be possible to find a tax gatherer that is not perjured up to the eyes, and the etrmag- est argument used against the suggested levying of an income tax is that it would cause the whole of the moneyed classes to become yet more steeped in perjury. The assessment system of Chicago "puts a higher premium upon perjury than upon any other vice or virtue under heaven." Mr. Steed would find if he were to look further afield that perjury is a protected industry in nigh every city in the Union. How can it be otherwise when every tax-gatherer holds his position subject to his hav- ing an elastic conscience. The owners of millions ,do not make out their own returns. They prefer tbe unerring judgment and trained experience f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WARRINGTON SAILING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SAILING CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL RACE.</p>
        <p>(BY A F.PECIAL CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>Saturday last was the occasion of the 28th annual race in connection with the Warrington Sailing Club. The public, however, seems recently to have lost something. ot the ardent interest which it took in the event years ago. A slight amendment was made in the arrangement of sailing the boats round a buoy near Walton Arches and finishing up oppo- site Litton's Mill. Instead of the race being com- pleted at the latter. spot it was agrftd—after con- siderable debate—that the race should continue to a spot opposite Monks, Hall an Co's. works. Thc boats left on Good Friday and proceeded down the river, many of them making their anchorage at the starting place opposite Snig Pie House, Widnes. As on , other occasions the down trip was accompanied by a slight accident. The unfortunate boat this time was " Mayfly, "which sank near Widnes. The next morn- ing, however, she was in her place at the starting point, when—lo ! and behold !—her orginal name had been effaced and she was sailing under a new appellation of bright verrnillian " XXX," While speculation was rife as to the reason of this new name—for no one knew why it should be XXX—a fresh Cause ftir speculation arose. One of the crew of " Punk" came and "wanted to know what to do," with a simulation of great distress in hi voice."For"said he "our captain has gone off this morn- ing and got married without saying a word to us, and there's no one to sail the boat." Farther investigation 3110WPd this to be tru., and while " Pink's' crew were seeking advice as to how to get a new captain, the truant commander returned as cool as the proverbial cucumber, and having assured his eager crew that he "should not go and do it again," set about his work with the calmness of a stoic.</p>
        <p>The various beats were measured by the keel and handicapped a minute per foot. The fleet sailed in the following order : " Pink" (Captain W. Warburton), " Mayfly " (Captain E. Gleave), " Florence" (Captain H. Warburton). "Rose" (Captain J. Barrow), " Siesta" (Captain J. Leigh) "Wookcock" (Captain J. Peers), " St. George" (Captain J. Green), " Maggie" (Captain W. Warburton), "Silver Cloud" (Cap- tain John Leigh) " Gipsy" (Captain J. Dickinson) A start was not made until six o'clock, twenty minutes behind the original time fixed. The run from Widnes down to Bank Quay was not very exci`ing. There was a very pleasant " slip" breeze blowing, not sufficiently strong, however, to bring into play the better qiialities of the heavier boats. "Pink," maintained the start she ' received up to the Ferry and then soon showed her fol- lowers a" clear pair of heels." A rather unpleasant feature attended the race up to this point however, and this was the presence among the competing boats, of three or four IN idnea fishing smacks. A very great amount of annoyance was felt about this unsailorlike, and to say the least of it,inconsiderate behaviour. True, no serious consequenses were the outcome of it. but , nevertheless on several occasions it required difficuit manceuvering to prevent the smacks" taking the wind" of one or other of the boats. The sailing on the whole was good, and when the boats got to Bank Quay " ratch," the only boats whose positions seemed to be uncertain, were "Siesta "and " Gipsy." " Maggie" was following" Pink 'S up closely and was certatn of a second place, while " Siesta" and " Gipsy" were mak- ing a hard and exciting fight forth e third positien. It seemed as though " Siesta " was going to be the fortu- nate boat, until at length, through not " coming about" quick eneugh, she grazed the bank and her competitor glided ahead. Rowever," Siesta" made another plucky bid for it, and for the remainder of the race the interest • of the sw.sctatera was in Only centered on these two boats which now alternately gained and lost on each other 1, amid the cheers of their reepec'ive admirers. When the , majority of the bolts neared the buoy at Walton Arches the ebb had set in, telt its strength did not become de- trimental until the het boat had turned. The run friion the buy down to the win nine flag was much pleasanter than it has been fer yeara.At 2.30 amid a veritable tumult of applause the smart little " Pink " passed the winning flag as winner, closiely followed hy that admirable craft " Maggie." The flele.. had become keener between "Siesta " and " Gipsy," and the excellent devices which the captains of both, crafts nut into force, in their efforts to secure the third place, math. up one of the mast ex- citing features of the race. Uneaeely. however, " Gipsy" came in one length ahead of the "Siesta. Both of these beats are of no mein sailing capacities. The old familiar favourite " Waedcack " came in fifth. "Silver Cloud," who came in sixth,was put laat,bec mu an oar was wad by her crew to nuah her.off a bank,this being an infringement of the rides of the hindicap. The following are the pi-I -Ilion of tin. boate :--1, Pink ; 2. Maggie ; 3, Cripiy ; 4. Siea.a ; 5. Woodcock ; 6, Florence ; 7, St. Our''; 8, .••laytly ; 9, 11se :10. -4'11,r Cloud. Mr. Ra4ley offieiated aa starter, Mr. J. We k:ow as judge, Mr. F. Trusco• t,nember taker ; Mr. George t . Rigby, secretary ; and Mr. E. 61 -aye, treaeueer. Li the evening the boatmen assembled at the Bowing Cireea, Inri,and sat down to an excellent repast provided by Mr. Dutton, the host, a most enj iyable time bing spine. hooking at the race train every s:andpoint, it can be claimed to be the most successful held for many years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WEEKLY REPOIa ON THE HEALTH OF WAItRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPOIa ON THE HEALTH OF WAItRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 12th of the current year, the following births and deaths wers registered :—Births: Warrington, 38 ; Latchford, ; total,4l. Deaths: Warrington. 20; Latchford, 4 ; total 24. The excess of births over deaths was 17. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 22-2 per 1.000, against 20.1 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 11.3 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 37 per 1,000, against 3 9 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 0-9 the correAponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were :-0 to 1,8; 3; total. 24. The deaths included-1 from enteric lever; 1 from measles ; '2 from whooping cough ; 2 from puerperal fever; 1 from croup 1 from plit hisis ; 1 from ancernia ; 4 from lung disease ; 2 from heart disease ; 2 from kidney disease; 2 from brain disease. The following cases of infectious dis- eases have been reported during the week :—Erysi- pelas 1; scarlet fever, 3; enteric 1; puerperal fever, 3. Patients in Hospital 24 March, 26. METKOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 435 Fah., and therefore 2.7 deg. higher than the preced- ing week. The maximum temperature 61 F., was recorded on the 24th inst.. and the minimum temperature 28 F., on the 18th inst. The rainfall measured nil inch. The general direction was Easterly, the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1,200 miles.—JoHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PEN KETH..</head>
        <p>PEN KETH..</p>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY AND EAST/M.—These holidays were taken advantage of by all the workpeople in tke village. The bustle of the old Kister holidays, however, when crowds used to assemble at Fiddler's Ferry, was conspicuous in its absence and the air of rrpose and quiet which pre- vailed form. d a great contrast. The villagers gladly embraced the opportunity of" tripping" and of visiting friends. On Friday the usual tea meeting was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom, and was very well attended. This meeting was originally organised to counteract the attractions of the Ferry, and seems to have attained its olject. Alter tea the annual meeting was held, and the annual reports of the officers, Messrs. J. Jackson (trea- surer), J. A. tsmith (hon. sec.), A. Dunabin (sitlt club sec.), and R. Goodwin (band sec.), were read. These were fa v.urable, Mr. Smith, however, having to report a falling off of a great many scholars. The prizes for attendance were distributed by Mrs. R. Cooke. The Rev. J. Gunnell addressed the meeting as also did Messrs J. Watson and J. Atherton (superintendents). Master R. Cooke gave a recitation and Misses Walsh and Usder- wood sang a duet. Mr. R. Cooke was in the chair. '</p>
        <p>Spgakinz at Maryport OR Wednesday evening, Sir Wilfrid Law4on said that they were pledged to carry out Mr. Glsdstone's Irish policy, and if Lord Rosebery shown 1 any signi of faltering in giving justice to he. land he would fall to-morrow like lk.ad in water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>_WAR R I NGTON A -MANE-UR</head>
        <p>THE _WAR R I NGTON A -MANE-UR</p>
        <p>DR IMATIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>PERFORMANCE OF "SWEET LAVENDER." In essaying the production of Mr. A. W. Pinero's " Sweet Lavender," the Warrington Amateur Dra- matic Society undertook a task of no little diffi- culty. To use a colloquialism " they were flying at. high game.' Though containing none of the sensational incidents which are characteristic of the modern drama, nor the " catchpenny" com- plications which are an element in many up-to- date melodramas " Sweet Lavender" must be re- garded as one of the most successful plays of modern times. Its representations by professionals can already be counted by the thousands through- out the world, and yet we understand that the Warrington Dramatic Society comes within the first half dozen established Amateur Societies who have ventured the production of the play. This speaks well for the Society, and it is another proof of a fact that has been demonstrated before, namely, that the institution is imbued with the intention of wiping off the reproach of being " indifferent," which has become attached to it • during the p tst two or three years. That a perform- ance of some degree of. merit was anticipated by the public was sufficiently attested on Thursday and Fri- day evenings by the thronged state of the gymnasium, and that the public were not disappointed in this direc- tion was amply proved by the outbursts of applause, which greeted the various points scored during the per- formance.</p>
        <p>"Sweet Lavender," tiough to a great extent a realistic study of life in the legal quarters of our great capital, offers some exalted ideals to the artistes engaged in its production. There are three acts and only one scene. This is a somewhat scantily furnished sitting- room in the Higher Temple. On each side of the room is a door leading respectively to the bedrooms of Richard Phenyl and Clement Hale, its residential occupants. At one end of the room is a passage leading from the rooms, whilst the other end, where are latticed windows looking into the court, forms a kind of recess curtained off from the rest. For a moment or two after the rise of the cur- tain, the sp-..ctators had an opportunity of critically regarding this scene, which is probably one of the finest pieces of stage craft ever seen in Warrington. The model was dpsigned by Mr. A. Woods from an actual chamber in the Temp!e, and the work was carried • out by Mr. J. Hesketh, assisted by Messrs. H. Fergusson, Mottram and Kinsey. In every detail the design was executed with almost fastidious care. The whole of the wood-work was in imitation of old oak, the lattice win- dows of an antique style specially made ; even the doors were constructed like serviceable house doors. The furnishing of the room was faultless, and it included ainong other things two paintings, one of a judge and the other of Mr. J. E. Davies as " Phenyl" in his barris- ter's robes, the work having been executed at a week's notice by Mr. Thompson,late master of the School of Art. 'rile warmest praise is due to those who carried out the work, and especially to Mr. Heskath who lent the material, which is valued at considerably more than £lOO,</p>
        <p>The plot told briefly is this. Clement Hale is the adopted son of Mr. Wedderburn, a banker, and he is studying for the bar. The sharer of his chambers is Dick Pnenyl, a weak natured, down-at-heel barrister whose good hearted ness proclaims itself in a gentle humanity and getiial humour which makes the character the life of the play. Mrs. Ruth Holt (said to be a widow) is the housekeeper. Her daughter Lavender is a pretty young girl, between young Hale and whom there is an attachment which the latter indulges under the laudable plea of helping the girl out with her lessons. She has not been looking well of late and the first act brings on Dr. Delaney, a fashionable physician, who has called to see Lavender. 'flee doctor who is a genial Irishman, "sees how the land lies," and under the pre- tence of prescribing for Lavender's health orders her to discontinue herdessod for some weeks, so as to prevent if possible his old friend's (Mr. Wedderburn) adopted son "making a fool ef himself." Whilst the doctor is explaining this to Mrs. Rolt in Clement's preeence, a noise is heard in the next room and Dick Phenyl comes staggering into the room. Having recovered hitnself slightly he is introduced to Dr. Delaney. Then Phenyl in a desperate effort to remove curl- picion as to his sobriety explains to the doctor that the noise was caused by "the pattern on my berrorn carpet—had it turned torrom upwards last week —still, damn annoying pattern. Trip up anybody." Pre- sently, however,the two inmates of the chamber are left alone, and Phenyl being now "sobered up," learns what 1 the doctor has prescri bed ,and also that his young friend 1 is in love with Lavender,and then follows some genuine- ly humerons by-play on the part of the old man, who advises Clement to " pull up before he goes too fat." Clement, however, proposes to overcome the difficulty by reading to Lavender, as she is not allowod to read herself, and is aboat to s&amp;., out to order several works. such as the history of " Frederick the Great" in TO volumes. At this point Horatio Bream, a young Ameri- can, comes in. He lets just returned from tke continent with Mrs. Gilfillian (Mr. Wedderburn's sister) and her daughter Minnie. He has saved the life of the latter in Paris, and since appears to have courted the ladies' company incessantly, until losing them in the crush at Euston Station. Finding them not at Clement's cham- , hers, he leaves and shortly afterwards the love passages between Clement and Lavender are brought to a climax by the young gentleman proposing. While on his knees in this act, he is secretly observed by his aunt e nd cou- sin. The former is shocked, and has an indignant ieter- view with Mrs Rolt, in which the latter, having heard of Clement's proposal, expresses her deter- mination to prevent the marriage and to quit time locelity. In the meantime, Clement is continu- ally haunted by the two ladies, who wish to prevent him being alone in the company of Lavender The latter who is a sensitive and pretty girl, is rendered very ill by the shock. When she is strong enough to leave her room her mother decided upon departing. She has geld good by to Phenyl who is much affected, and draws from him a promise to assist her in her pur- reuse. The unfortunate man, however, has promised to help Clement's suit—and also to keep sober. He would probably have broken neither promise, by remaining inactive, hal it not been for Lavender, who emtarrased !dm by ;taking delicate questions abut her lovers affec- tions. lie suecumbs to the strain, breaks one promise by taking ten much whisky, tries to atone by sending a messenger" to run for Clement " to come and see Lav- ender, and then rememberine his promise to Mrs. Rolt becomes perplexed and endeavours to meet the difficulty 1 be shouting to the messenger " No—no ! Don't run— walk ! " While Clement, having returned, is engaged with Lavender, his foster father arrives. Heating argued and then remonstrated with Clement about his attach- ment, he finally comma-ads him to give up Lavender or t relin,guieli all claim to his connection with him. At this point a telegram is handed to the banker tellme him that is house is suspended. Mrs. Rolt atthe'same time comes into the room, and upon seeing her the banker falls in a fit. A week or more has elapsed—or supposed te have done—when the curtain rises on the next act. Mrs. Rolt and Lavender have ditappearefl. Mrs. Gilfillian and her daughter are living in the chambers and getting used to their ill- fortune, whilst Wedderburn is bedfast. The young American calls every day to enquire about the sick banker—and courts Minnie on the stairs. One day Dr. Delaney, suspecting some relationship between Mrs. Bolt and Wedderburn, broaches the question to Phenyl, I and decides up in bringing Mrs. Rolt and the banker to- gether again. Mrs. Rolt, as a nurse in a home founded by the doctor, comes to nurse the sick tanker. And now occurs a scene of singular pathos, for Mrs. Rolt 18 years ago, was the banker's sweetheart, whom o he left unmarried to bear a child—and that Lavender. The shame and repentance of the banker, and the noble and forgiving conduct of the woman, made of this meeting, a picture of appealing force. Their old affection is re- newed.and the secret is kept between theta. Lavender's and Clement's suit is no longer opposed, and the American at last succeeds in obtaining, the hand of Minnie, whilst bachelor P -ienyl becomes the affectionate friend of all.. The chief character, as we remarked before. is Dick Phenyl, whose impersonatian was left in the hands of the popular Mr. John E. Davies. From the commence- ment to the close this character is before the aedience, and as pourtrayed by Mr. Davies, it was the life and soul of the piece. He entered most thoroughly into the humour of the character, aad was a model of drollery from the time he explained about being tripped over the patterne of the carpet to the close: every line which fell to his lot was made to tell,andivrhen he had nothing to say his gestures and by-play were spontaneously funny. In contrast with this character, was Mr. Geoffrey Wed- derhurn, a character which was allotted to Mr. H. VV. Cookson. In this character there are some fine traits, especially in the last ant, where,stricken by illness and ruined in fortune, the banker meets the woman he be- trayed. The scene is full of peculiar delicacy. More than once the actor's power is severely tested, and the fervour and passion.under a curb of self restraint,which it was necessary to display, was represented with an artistic taste of high excellence. It is a character which in 9 cases out of 10 would have been overdone— hut Mr. Cookson played it as though it had been written for him. As Dr. Delaney, a deoided success was ecered by Mr. Alfred Woods, who imparted a freshness and genialty to the character, which . together with his make up and masterly command of the ' bregue," went to make up an impersonation worthy of Mr. Wood's reputation. His work did not end with this character, however, for upon him fell the duties of the responsible office of stage manager, which made no small call upon his time and energy. A com- mendable interpretation of Clement Hale was given b Mr. G. R. Williamson, whothrew a considerable amsent of verve and genuine ability into his work. As the young American Horace Bre,am,'Mr. G. T. Brundrett hit ;ff capitally the peculiarities of an irrepressible " Yank," and though his well affected tone of sangfroid was somewhat marred by a nervous energy of move- ment, he acqui,ted himself with more than average success. Mr. A. Browne had as his task the portrayal of Mr. Maw, a solicitor. What little work there was in this part Mr. Browns performed with ability.and boasted a "get up" which was second to none in the whole caste. Some light work fell to the lee of Mr. Dents.</p>
        <p>Davies,as Mr. Bulger,a hairdresser,and as a meek poeti- cal admirer of Mrs. Rolt, he was very amusing. The two chief female characters were Mrs. Ruth Rolt, and Lavender. The personation of the former fell to the lot of Mrs. John E. Davies, who imparted to the charac- ter a tenderness sand nobility which made it the most realistic interpretation in the piece. Miss /Arty Pierpoint as Lavender, made her debut, and considering the difficalties of the character, she has every reason to be proud of her performance. Although occasionally lacking a little in vigour she introduced an amount of gracefulness of movement and accuracy of elocution, whieli would have been creditable in an artiste of much greater experience. Miss Schofield and Miss Collas, of Liveepool, impersonated respectively Mrs. Gilflllian and Minnie. in toe former character Miss Schofield scored a great success, whilst Miss Collas, as Minnie was de,ightfully bright in the scenes with flarace Bream. At the conclusion of the performance the performers were enthusiastically applauded as they passed before the curtain, and when Mr. John Davies appeared the applause gave place to a ringing round of cheers. The audience also displayed their appreciation of the work done by Mr. Efesketh,by calling for him to appear bef are the curtain ; hut Mr. Flesketh did not respond. The performance may be deemed one of the most successful ever given by the Society, and the perforrners—especi- ally Messrs. J.E. Davies, and A. Woods.joint managers— have reason to feel highly satisfied with their work as a whole.</p>
        <p>During the evening an excellent programme of music was played by an orchestra consisting of Messrs. W. Allen, J. Forster, A. linovvles, J. Burrows, H. 3 mid, J. Cliarnoek. W. Podmore, J. Ward, W. Hopkiss, W. Waterfleld, J. B. Caldwell, and the conductor, Mr. R. 11. Pearson.</p>
        <p>The performance was repeated last night, when there was again a largo and appreciative audience present.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN SCEIOOL TEA MEETING.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN SCEIOOL TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with the above named school was held in the schoolroom on Monday, when 116 sat down to tea. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. Baxter, Mrs. Pritchard, Mrs.J.Hard- ing, jun., Miss A. Morrell, Miss L. Light, Miss Brough, Miss Highley,and Miss Merrington. After tea the annual meeting was held in the chapel,the chair being occupied by the Rev. J. Kirtlan. After singing and prayer,Mr.S. Blythe read the annual report,which stated that the num- bers on the books were: teachers, 32 male, seven female. scholars 120 male, 93 feinale, and 60 infants, total 273, The average attendance was : teachers, morning 13, afternoon, 2S; scholars, morning, 100, afternoon 200. There were seven girls and eleven boys classes. The young womens'class was under the control of Mrs.llaxter, and the men's class of Mr.C. George. Two teachers, Mrs.. Bromley. awl Mrs. Bailey had left during the year; and seven new teachers,Miss L. Light, Miss M. Highly, and Messrs. Albert Foden, J. Lawton, E. Wilkes, J. Dauber, and R. Schofield had been added. During the year Mr. J. L.George had resigned the superintendency and Mr. W. Summerfield, the treasurership of the school, positions they had occupied since its commencement. The offi- cers for the year were superintendents, Messrs. W. Brough, and S. Blythe ; secretaries, Messrs. T. Light, and J. Blythe ; treasurer, Mr. J. Corra.dine ; visitors, Messrs. R. Lawton, and R. Schofield. The in- come for the past year had been £.61 2s. 4d., and the expenditure, including the cost of treats, and £lO put in the . bank to zaa 12.1., thus leaving a balance of 103. ad. After the reading of the report an anthem was given by the choir, eatitled " Be glad in the Lord," in which the solo part was well rendered by Miss A Morrell. The Chairman then ad- dressed the meeting. He said that he considered the Easter season to be the glad lest season of the year, and with the associations of that season combined with the fine weather they were having, they ought to be truly glad. He was always delighted to come to Sankey • Bridges for there he came into contact with tens de.ine- cracy. Ha hoped that the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel would go on prospering in the future as in the piast.—Mr. Whittaker at this point gave one of his wonderful exaibitions of skill upon the concertina consisting of imitations of all the farmyard inhabitants, and varieus other birds. Upon being encored,he imitiated the church bells, and various work b,lls and very cleverly.—The Rev. J. Gunnell delivered an inter- esting address upon Christian cheerfulness, and gave some excellent advice to attain that state of mind.—The choir then gave the anthem "This Lord is my Shepherd" in which Mrs. Bailey and Mr. Entwistle efficiently took the solo parts.—Rev. G. D. Mason congratulated the choir upon the manner in which they had gone through the anthems that night. He considered that it showed a great talent for music. He also corninented upon the hearty congregational singing at the Sankey Bridges Chapel. This was one element of success in a chapel, for singing always attracted people to it. He could al.s bear testimony to the efficient manner in which the Sunday school was conducted.—Mr. Wnittaker again gave a number of selections upon the concertina for which he, was encored.—Rev. Owen Watkine followed withone of his characteristic speeches, and caused roars of laughter at his frequent allusions to " Mr. Brouali and 1.." Referring to Sunday afternocna, he said tha- all Sunday afternoons ought to be pleasant, and there would be no ri._•ed for Pleasant Sunday afternoon services if every one were like the Welsh people who attendel Sunday school until they were old people. This wa; one reason why the Welsh preachers were such power- ful preachers.as they were trained in the Sunday school. —The proceedings of the meeting were brought to a close by the choir mintiwt, anthem " Trait yo in tiv. Lord," in witieli Miss Morrell took the solo part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>HONOURS FOR A LOCAL STUDENT.</p>
        <p>Amongst the list of successful candidates for the final examination for the degree of M. 8., Ch. 8., which appeared in the "Manchester Guardian" of the 23rd inst., was the name of Mr. Peter Thomp- son, jun., of Beech House, Lowton, and Bridge-sc., Earlestowc. Mr. Thompson, it may be stated, was oneof the only two gentlemen who were awarded hynours in the examination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>TRIP ACCIDENT AT THE_ TIILCA.N.</head>
        <p>TRIP ACCIDENT AT THE_ TIILCA.N.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening, as Mr. Morris, of the Sunbeam Inn, accompanied by his wife, was driv- ing home from Warrington to Wargrave, beyond the Railway Bridge at the Vulcan, he got 6411 the usual road and the trap was overturned on the em- bankment near the brook, both occupants were thrown out. Fortunately neither were injured,but the horse which felt received a severe injury, am', the trap is badly ashed. VULCAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. The Committee of the Vulcan Social Club have banded over to the Committee of the Vulcan Wesleyan Sunday School, the sum of £7 being the proceeds of a cbncert given in the Vulcan Works dining-room on March 17th. The teachers and officers of the school desire to take this opportunity of thanking the members of the Social Club for the interest they evince in the welfare of the school, and for the timely help they had afforded it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT ITAYDOCK:</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT ITAYDOCK:</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Haydock Polling clis- trict of the Newton Division Conservative Associa- tion was held at the Waggon and Horses Hotel, Haydock. on Wednesday evening. There was a good attendance of Conservatives and Unionists. Mr. A. H. L. Evans presided, and was supported by Messrs. R. Bradshaw, C.B. F. Borron, E. Jackson, W. Johnson. J. Turton, J. Jackson, W. Mawdsley. and T.Norths:of the National Union of Conservative Association. After the election of officers. Mr. Johnson proposed, and Mr. Borrnn seconded, a re- solution of gratitude to Lord Salisbury, and the Conservative leaders " for their untiring efforts to preserve the union of the British Empire." Mr. T. North spoke in support of the resolution, which was carried. Votes of thanks to Mr. North for his address, and to the chairman for presiding, con- cluded the meeting. SONV OF TEMPRBANCB.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Cadets of Temperance was held on Wednesday in the Co-oper- ative Hall. Bro. H. Pearce, W.A., presided. The quarterly meeting of the Britain's Need Division met at 7.30, when there was a large attendance of members present. Bro. S Foden, W.P., in the chair. The business of the meeting was the election of Financial Scribe, in place of the late Bro. John Present. Bro. William Pearson was elected unani- mously to fill the office. Bro. Joseph French was elected as assistant patron of cadets.. The election of representatives to next grand division meetine which will meet at Earlestown on April 14, took place, when the following brethren were elected : John Judson, P.W.P.W.P., William Clare, P.W.P., John Pickett, P.W.P., S. Foden, W.P. ; alternate representatives: Joseph Hughes, P.W.P., Samuel Marsh, P.W.P. Bro. E. Mulling was elected an auditor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-31_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>EASTER HOLIDAYS AT EABLESTOWN AND NEwTert.</head>
        <p>EASTER HOLIDAYS AT EABLESTOWN AND NEwTert.</p>
        <p>Owing to all the works in the district closing on Thursday evening until Tuesday morning, a large number of people were released from their ordinary toil, and the beautiful summer weather which pre- vailed came as a delightful boon to holiday-makers. The railway company offered special facilities in the way of cheap excursions, to enable people to reach the many popular resorts. The privilege granted to their workpeople by the railway company of travel- ling at quarter fare, was taken advantage of to a very large extent. On Thursday the bookings to various places,was exceptionally heavy,and on Sat- Mrday and. Monday a large. wataer of ticke,t Nom</p>
        <p>issued,and the precincts of the station wore a busy' aspect during eaeh Oily. Those who did not care to leave the town by train went for walks into the coun— tty,and the neigh bournood of Newton and Winwick presented a lively appearance. On Good Friday the Earlestown football club catered for the amusement of their patrons, and during the afternoon the Ear- lestown band played selections of music, and at the conclusion of the inatcli dancing was indulged in until dusk. There was a large influx of visitors to Newton from the outside districts. ACCIDENT TO AN OLD LADY AT EARLESTOWN. On Wednesday afternoon an old lady of inde— pendent means, named Johnson, living at Eccleston. Park, near Sr. Helens, had a miraculous escape from death at Earlestown Railway Sration. In• Company with her sister she entered the station, intending to take the train to Warrington. They crossed by the level cros.sino. at the end of the platform, just as the 2.15 train for War- rington was approaching the station. The officials on the platform called to them to warn them o.f their danger, and the ladies thinking that the train was going to Manchester,- stepped out on the road, as they supposed, but unfortunately dis JOhnson, the elder of the tWo, got in front of tha incoming train, and was knocked down by the engine. The engine and two carriages passed over her,but being in the four foot between the rails she escaped In- stant death. The station officials on going to the spot assisted the unfortunate lady from under the carriages and they then found that her left arm had been completely severed, and she had received injuries to her head. She was taken to the ladies waiting room and medically attended by Dr. Mooncey, who dressed the amputated limb, and she was sent to her home in a cab.</p>
        <p>. John Kenyon, a labourer; sixty-six years of; age, wbe lived in Broad Oik-road, St. Helens, was on Friday night walking along Blackbrook-road, and by some means slipped or staggered in front of a steam tramcar which was coming along. HP was struck on the head, and a serious wound was inflicted. The unfortunate man was taken to the Providence Hospital,. whore he lay unconscious until death ensued on Tuesday night. AS the 11.20 steam tramcar from St. Helens to Prescot was proceeding up Liverpool-road, St. Holenson Wednesday morning, an axle broke near the nave of one of the wheels, and the wheel rolled away into the road. The car dragged a few yards, and was•then stopped. There were few passengers on the car, sio that very little incon- venience was exp_qienced. A new set of wheels were brought and put in, and traffic was restored as quickly as possible.</p>
      </div>
